      "totalSpecint": "Total SPECint",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the count and current age of your servers, plus their annual power and support costs.",
          "Enter the price and expected efficiency of the replacement hardware.",
          "Read the payback period and 3-year total cost difference to decide whether to refresh now."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "When should you refresh servers?",
        "formula": "annualSaving = oldOpEx - newOpEx; paybackYears = (newCapEx - salvageOld) / annualSaving",
        "explanation": [
          "Older servers draw more power and cost more to maintain, so replacing them can lower operating expenses even after the purchase cost is included.",
          "A refresh is usually justified when the annual savings recover the new hardware cost within your acceptable payback window, often 2–3 years for dense workloads."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is a good payback period?",
            "a": "Most IT teams target 2–3 years, but it depends on workload density and power prices."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I include salvage value?",
            "a": "Yes, any resale or trade-in value of old gear reduces the effective capital cost."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does refresh always save power?",
            "a": "Newer nodes are far more efficient per rack unit, but only if consolidated; sprawl can erase the gain."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "age": {
      "name": "Age",
      "description": "Calculate exact age from birthdate in years, months, and days",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your exact age in years, months, days, hours, and minutes",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the birth date (Y/M/D); add the time for more precision.",
          "Enter the current date (defaults to today).",
          "Read the exact age in years, months, days and total days lived."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How is age calculated precisely?",
        "formula": "age = current date − birth date (calendar-aware subtraction)",
        "explanation": [
          "Exact age is not a simple year difference; you must handle borrowing between months and days. If today's day is before the birth day, you borrow from the month and convert to days, giving an accurate years/months/days result.",
          "Total days are useful for milestones, insurance or legal age. Watch time zones and leap years: someone born on 29 Feb is usually counted on 28 Feb or 1 Mar in non-leap years, varying by custom."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why is subtracting years wrong?",
            "a": "It ignores whether this year's birthday has passed. Step-by-step months/days give the true age."
          },
          {
            "q": "Born on 29 Feb in a leap year?",
            "a": "In non-leap years it is usually counted on 28 Feb or 1 Mar depending on custom; the tool follows the common rule."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can it show total days lived?",
            "a": "Yes, the result includes total days from birth to today, with leap-year differences."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "date": {
      "name": "Date",
      "description": "Add or subtract days, weeks, months, and years from a date",
      "metaDescription": "Add or subtract time periods from any date",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter a start date and choose add or subtract.",
          "Specify the number of days, weeks, months, or years to shift.",
          "Read the resulting date, plus the count of days between two dates if comparing."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Working with calendar dates",
        "formula": "Result = Start date +/- interval, with month-length and leap-year rules applied",
        "explanation": [
          "Date arithmetic must handle months of different lengths and leap years, so the calculator counts actual calendar days rather than assuming 30-day months.",
          "When subtracting, you get both the target date and the exact number of days elapsed, useful for deadlines and age."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Does it count the start day?",
            "a": "It counts whole intervals; for days between it returns the difference without double-counting endpoints."
          },
          {
            "q": "How are leap years handled?",
            "a": "Years divisible by 4 are leap, except centuries not divisible by 400."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I find a due date?",
            "a": "Add the required number of days to the start date to get the deadline."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "time": {
      "name": "Time",
      "description": "Add or subtract hours, minutes, and seconds",
      "metaDescription": "Perform time calculations with hours, minutes, and seconds",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the time of the day, including hours, minutes, seconds.",
          "Toggle the display between 12 hours and 24 hours, if required.",
          "View the time after standardization and any converted format."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Read and convert clock time",
        "formula": "24 hour = (12 hour mod 12) + time offset; 24:00 to zero",
        "explanation": [
          "The clock time circulates every 24 hours, so 1300 and 1 p.m. are the same two at the same time.",
          "Seconds and minutes in 60 digit, hour in 24 digit, and inflows can be maintained as valid time.",
          "Time is converted by adding \" Target Offset - Source Offset \" to original time. For example, Beijing time (UTC+8) 14:00 is converted to New York (UTC-5): the deviation = −5 - 8 = −13, so 14:00 - 13 = 0100 the next day. If the result is negative, add 24 and record ** the previous day**; above 24, minus 24 and recorded as ** the following day** ** - The cross-Japan judgement is the most easily missed step of the handwritten time zone conversion.",
          "** Summertime (DST) is the largest pit**: most parts of Europe and the United States speed up the clock by one hour in the summer, so New York Winter is UTC-5; Summer is UTC-4; and Beijing is between 12 and 13 hours. Europe began the last week of March and ended the last Sunday of October; North America started the second week of March and ended the first Sunday of November. ** China stopped summering after 1991**, so the year was full UTC+8, which is why the time difference with Europe and the United States changed half a year."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What's the difference between 12 hours and 24 hours?",
            "a": "24 hours between 0 and 23 to avoid m/pm; 12 hours to divide a day into morning and afternoon."
          },
          {
            "q": "2.30 p.m. How do you turn to 24 hours?",
            "a": "Pm hours after noon plus 12, received 14:30."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the clock on 60?",
            "a": "Minutes and seconds are 60-plus, which is the old Babylon's practice to date."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do international meetings avoid mistakes in time zones?",
            "a": "The most prudent approach would be: 1 to be agreed and indicated in the invitation by UTC time; 2 to be attached the IANA time zone name (e.g. Asia/Shanghai, America/New York) rather than the abbreviation (CST may refer to both Chinese standards and Central American time) and it would be misleading to use calendar invitations with time zones (.ics) to automatically convert the participant ' s calendar software. The words \"3 p.m.\" have been verbally agreed to almost always go wrong in a time-zone scenario."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does China cross five geographical time zones and use only one?",
            "a": "China ' s geography extends from the Eastern Five (UTC+5) to the Eastern Nine (UTC+9), but Beijing Time (UTC+8) is the administrative arrangement established in 1949. The benefits are national harmonization of transport, communications, broadcasting hours and ease of coordination; the cost is about two hours between the actual daylight time and the clock in the western part of Xinjiang, where the \"UTC+6\" is often used as a reference."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Time difference between major global cities and UTC (standard, non-summer)",
        "tableHeaders": [
