    "data-size": {
      "name": "Data Size",
      "description": "Convert between bytes, KB, MB, GB, TB and more",
      "metaDescription": "Convert data sizes between bytes, kilobytes, megabytes, and more",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the values bytes, KB, MB, GB or TB.",
          "Select binary (1024) or decimal (1000) interpretation.",
          "Reads the equivalent size under all other units."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Data Size Unit Conversion",
        "formula": "1 GiB = 1024 MiB; 1 GB = 1,000 MB (decimal); MiB = 10242 bytes",
        "explanation": [
          "There are two systems for storage: the binary (kiB/MiB/Gib, 1024) for operating systems and the commercial Decimal (KB/MB/GB, 1000) for harddisk plants.",
          "So the 1 TB hard drive shows 931 GiB in the file manager Rio.",
          "The storage unit has two sets of standards, which are the source of \"1TB hard disks with only 931GB\":** the harddisk plant commercial Decimal** (1 TB = 1012 bytes),** the operating system is binary** (1 TibB = 240 = 1,099, 511, 627,776 bytes). 1012 ÷ 230 ≈ 931.3, so 1 TB hard drive shows about 931 GB in Windows. This is not a matter of water contraction or quality, but of measuring different calibres.",
          "When converting, remember the difference of 1024 (binary) or 1000 (decimal). Quick estimation techniques: **1024 ≈ 1000 x 1.024, so the difference of 2.4 per cent for each step ** and of the level 3 (GB→B) cumulatively about 7.4 per cent. Document size, memory capacity, binary; hard disk label, network speed, decimal. Network operators say \"100 trillion broadband\" means 100 Mbps = 100 x 106 bit/s divided by 8 to 12.5 MB/s theoretical download speed."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does a TB hard drive show 931 GB?",
            "a": "Plant commercial Decimal TB (10^12 bytes); system binary GiB, 10^12 / 10243 ≈ 931 GiB."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which one?",
            "a": "It's all right under its system, and the confusion only results from unit labels."
          },
          {
            "q": "Where's Bitt and byte?",
            "a": "Net speeds by bits; bytes divided by 8."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the speed of downloads much smaller than the bandwidth?",
            "a": "Three reasons: 1 unit conversion, 100 Mbps = 12.5 MB/s (1 byte = 8 bits); 2 protocol cost, TCP/IP head, with an ethoscope of about 5 per cent-10 per cent, with lower actual effective bandwidth;3 network wear and tear, with reduced real speed for peak hours, cross-operators, and Wi-Fi interference. So 100M broadband actually downloads 10-12 MB/s are normal, about 200M 22-25 MB/s, about 90-110 MB/s."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does the document actually occupy more space than the document itself?",
            "a": "File system**cluster** mechanism: Disk distributed space by block of fixed size, default 4 KB. A 1 KB file will also occupy 4 KB, and a 4.1 KB file will occupy 8 KB. This is a huge waste of small documents. In addition, space will be occupied by NTFS MFT, logs, wastebins, system reduction points. An accurate statistic can be viewed with a tool to support the comparison of folders with vs file sizes."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Storage units converted (binary versus decimal)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Unit (binary)",
          "Actual bytes",
          "Decimal Unit",
          "margin"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
