    "time-lapse": {
      "name": "Time Lapse",
      "description": "Calculate interval, clip length, and memory for time lapse",
      "metaDescription": "Plan your time lapse photography projects",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the time you want to film the real world.",
          "Enter the frame rate for the interval and final video.",
          "Reads the total frame number and the final piece length."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Number of frames and fractional calculations for late photography",
        "formula": "Frame = length / interval; fractional seconds = frame / frame rate",
        "explanation": [
          "Timed photography is sampled at greater intervals, compressing slow change into short video.",
          "Common target is 24-30 fps smooth play; spacing determines how much time each frame represents.",
          "Time-lapse is a video that compresses long and slow changes into seconds to minutes. ** The length of a piece = the number of sheets frame rate (usually 25 or 30 fps)**, the number of sheets = the total length of the time taken. Example: 30 minutes of clouds, 3 seconds apart, 600 ÷ 25 = 24 seconds. ** The choice of spacing depends on the speed of change**: clouds, vehicle flows of 1-5 seconds, flowering of minutes and orbits of 20-30 seconds (which is also subject to 300 rules).",
          "** Three main hardware preconditions**: 1 absolutely stable tripod (any shaking in a piece is a catastrophic \"fruit effect\"); 2 Spacing function (a body menu or fast-door line to ensure \"Spacing = exposure time + processing time \" ); 3 Manual locking of all parameters - ** Must be with M, manual focus, fixed white balance** (Automated mode will jump between frames and scintillation). ** Late-stage velocity adjustment in PR/AE using LRTIMELAPSE + Lightroom (Professional) or inside brands, or directly using sequences based on fixed parameters. ** Note the battery/storage surplus, with a long breath of hundreds of thousands."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How much?",
            "a": "Depending on movement: clouds 2-5 seconds, stars 15-30 seconds, plants may grow in minutes to hours."
          },
          {
            "q": "How long?",
            "a": "Speculation seconds = total frame number ÷ frame rate; the longer the frame, the smoother."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the speed of the gate matter?",
            "a": "The gates should be much smaller than the interval (e.g. within half) and avoid gaps and scintillation."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the time-delayed video flashing?",
            "a": "is caused by an inconsistency in the parameters of the frame. The most common causes: 1 ** use automatic exposure/autowhite balance/automated ISO**, with a slight change in colour per frame, and a visible blinking (especially AUTO ISO jumps in light); 2 ** ** radial leaves or ND mirrors enter light changes at different angles** (low VND rotation); 3 Intra-aircraft noise is activated occasionally on long frames (closing long exposures down noise); 4 too short intervals result in some frames not being processed and jumped. ** Solved: complete M-file, manual focus, fixed white balance, off all automatic and noise reduction, slightly longer space than exposure + processing time. This post is part of our special coverage Global Voices 2011. **"
          },
          {
            "q": "Do you have time-delays on your phone? How do you do that?",
            "a": "Yes, modern mobile phones all have a \"time-delaying\" mode (iOS/Android support, automatic synthesis). Point 1: Use a tripod or place (hand-held); 2 Locked exposure (slowing the sun behind a focus frame long enough to avoid automatic flashing); 3 Subject selected scenes with marked changes (clouds, traffic, currents, sunrise); 4 Cell phones are usually automatically compressed into short videos, which cannot be fine-tuned at a later stage as a camera. ** Progress: a third party, App (e.g. Lapse It, ProCam), can space and export sequences at their own later stages. ** Note that mobile phones are fast-consuming, heat-released, long-duration recommended electrical plugs or low-temperature environments."
          }
        ],
