    "focal-equivalent": {
      "name": "Focal Length Equivalent",
      "description": "Match settings between different sensor sizes",
      "metaDescription": "Free online focal length equivalent calculator. Convert any lens to full-frame equivalent fast, no signup, compare cameras easily.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the actual focal length of your lens.",
          "Enter the camera sensor crop factor (e.g. 1.5 for APS-C).",
          "Read the full-frame equivalent focal length and its angle of view."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What Is a Focal Length Equivalent?",
        "formula": "equivalent = focal length × crop factor",
        "explanation": [
          "Different sensor sizes capture different fields of view for the same lens. The full-frame equivalent tells you what focal length on a full-frame camera gives the same perspective.",
          "This makes it easy to compare lenses across camera systems and understand how 'zoomed in' a shot will look."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does sensor size matter?",
            "a": "Smaller sensors crop the image, effectively magnifying the focal length, so the same lens looks more zoomed in."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is a crop factor?",
            "a": "It is the ratio of a full-frame sensor's diagonal to your sensor's diagonal; APS-C is about 1.5, Micro Four Thirds about 2.0."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does equivalent focal length change depth of field?",
            "a": "Perspective stays the same; only the field of view is matched. Depth of field also changes with sensor size at equal framing."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "hyperfocal": {
      "name": "Hyperfocal Distance",
      "description": "Calculate hyperfocal distance for maximum depth of field",
      "metaDescription": "Free online hyperfocal distance calculator. Get the focus point for max depth of field in landscapes fast, no signup, with formula.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the focal length and aperture you plan to shoot with.",
          "Enter the circle of confusion for your camera sensor.",
          "Read the hyperfocal distance and set focus there for maximum depth of field."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What Is the Hyperfocal Distance?",
        "formula": "H = f² / (N · c) + f",
        "explanation": [
          "The hyperfocal distance is the focus distance that keeps everything from half that distance to infinity acceptably sharp. It is the secret to maximally deep landscape focus.",
          "When you focus at the hyperfocal point, the far limit extends to infinity and the near limit is as close as possible."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why focus at the hyperfocal distance?",
            "a": "It maximizes depth of field: the background reaches infinity and the foreground stays sharp, ideal for landscapes."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does a wider aperture change it a lot?",
            "a": "Yes. A wider aperture (smaller f-number) pushes the hyperfocal distance farther away, shrinking the near sharp limit."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do I need a tripod?",
            "a": "For deep landscape focus you usually stop down to small apertures, which needs slower shutter speeds, so a tripod helps."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "nd-filter": {
      "name": "ND Filter",
      "description": "Calculate exposure time with ND filters",
      "metaDescription": "Free online ND filter calculator. Convert shutter speed for any ND stop value fast, no signup, for silky water and long exposures.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the shutter speed you would use without the filter.",
          "Enter the filter strength in stops (e.g. ND8 = 3 stops).",
          "Read the new shutter speed needed with the ND filter attached."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What Does an ND Filter Do?",
        "formula": "exposure time × 2^(stops)",
        "explanation": [
          "A neutral density (ND) filter reduces the light entering the lens without changing colors. It lets you use slow shutter speeds in bright light for silky water or motion blur.",
          "Each stop of ND doubles the required exposure time, so an ND1000 lets you shoot multi-second exposures in daylight."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What does ND8, ND64, ND1000 mean?",
            "a": "The number is the light reduction factor; the stop count is its base-2 log. ND8 = 3 stops, ND64 = 6 stops, ND1000 ≈ 10 stops."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I stack ND filters?",
            "a": "Yes, but combined stops add up and can push exposure so long you need a remote shutter and tripod."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why use ND for video?",
            "a": "It lets you keep a cinematic aperture (shallow depth) in daylight without overexposing the frame."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "golden-hour": {
      "name": "Golden Hour Guide",
      "description": "Calculate golden hour times based on your location",
      "metaDescription": "Free online golden hour calculator. Find golden and blue hour times for your location and date fast, no signup, for better photos.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your location (or let the calculator use your device position).",
          "Pick the date you plan to shoot.",
          "Read the golden hour and blue hour start/end times for that day."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What Are Golden and Blue Hour?",
        "formula": "Derived from solar elevation angle ≈ 6° (golden) and −4° to −6° (blue)",
        "explanation": [
          "The golden hour is the period shortly after sunrise and before sunset with warm, soft, directional light that flatters subjects and landscapes.",
          "The blue hour follows, when the sun is just below the horizon and the sky turns deep blue — perfect for cityscapes and long exposures."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How long does golden hour last?",
            "a": "Usually 20–60 minutes depending on latitude and season; it is longer near the equator and in winter at high latitudes."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is blue hour for?",
            "a": "Its even, cool light and lit windows make it ideal for architecture, bridges, and reflections after sunset."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does it work for indoor portraits?",
            "a": "The warm window light during golden hour is excellent for natural-looking indoor portraits near a window."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "cooking-units": {
      "name": "Cooking Units",
      "description": "Convert between metric and imperial cooking measurements with ingredient densities",
      "metaDescription": "Free cooking unit converter. Convert cups, tablespoons, teaspoons to ml, grams with density-aware conversions for flour, sugar, and more.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the quantity and the unit you have (cups, grams, tablespoons, etc.).",
          "Enter the unit you want to convert to.",
          "Read the converted amount and the approximate volume or weight."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Converting cooking units",
        "formula": "target = sourceAmount × (factorFrom / factorTo)",
        "explanation": [
          "Cooking mixes volume units (cups, tsp) with weight units (grams, oz); the converter uses standard densities for common ingredients to bridge them.",
          "Because density varies by ingredient, conversions between weight and volume are approximate unless you pick the specific food."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why do cup-to-gram values differ by ingredient?",
            "a": "A cup of flour weighs far less than a cup of sugar; density drives the difference."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are US and metric cups the same?",
            "a": "No; a US cup is 240 ml while a metric cup is 250 ml, a small but real gap."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I weigh or measure by volume?",
            "a": "Weighing is more accurate for baking; volume is fine for casual cooking."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "exchange-rate": {
      "name": "Exchange Rate",
      "description": "Convert between cryptocurrencies and fiat currencies (CHF, EUR, USD)",
      "metaDescription": "Free online exchange rate calculator. Convert currencies fast with recent rates, no signup, supports USD EUR GBP JPY CNY CHF and more.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the amount you want to convert.",
          "Pick the source and target currencies from the lists.",
          "Read the converted amount using the latest available rate."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How Exchange Rates Work",
        "formula": "amount × rate = converted amount",
        "explanation": [
          "An exchange rate is the price of one currency in another. Rates move constantly with supply, demand, interest rates, and economic news.",
          "Our calculator uses a recent reference rate for quick estimates; for transactions, your bank or exchange service sets the final rate."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Are the rates live?",
            "a": "The tool uses a recent reference rate for fast estimates. Always confirm the exact rate with your provider before a real transaction."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do I get a different rate at the bank?",
            "a": "Banks and exchanges add a spread (margin) on top of the mid-market rate, so the rate you get is slightly worse."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which currencies are supported?",
            "a": "Major fiat pairs such as USD, EUR, GBP, JPY, CNY and CHF are supported for quick reference conversions."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "hash-calculator": {
      "name": "Hash Calculator",
      "description": "Calculate MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-512 hash values",
      "metaDescription": "Free online hash calculator. Generate MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-512 digests from text or files fast, no signup, for integrity checks.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Type or paste the text or file content you want to hash.",
          "Choose the algorithm (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-512).",
          "Copy the resulting digest to verify integrity or store a fingerprint."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What Is a Hash?",
        "formula": "hash(message) → fixed-length digest",
        "explanation": [
          "A hash function turns any input into a fixed-length string. The same input always produces the same digest, but a tiny change completely changes the output.",
          "Hashes verify file integrity, store passwords safely, and power blockchains and digital signatures."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Which algorithm should I use?",
            "a": "Use SHA-256 or SHA-512 for security. MD5 and SHA-1 are fast but broken for security and only okay for checksums."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I recover the original text from a hash?",
            "a": "No. Hashing is one-way; you cannot reverse it. That is why it is safe for storing passwords."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does changing one character change everything?",
            "a": "Hash functions are designed to be avalanche-sensitive: a single bit change spreads to a completely different digest."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "mining-calculator": {
      "name": "Mining Profitability",
      "description": "Calculate Bitcoin mining profitability based on hash rate and electricity cost",
      "metaDescription": "Free online crypto mining calculator. Estimate profit from hashrate, power and electricity cost fast, no signup, with daily and monthly views.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your hardware hashrate and power consumption.",
          "Enter your electricity cost per kWh and the coin's network difficulty.",
          "Read the estimated daily, weekly, and monthly profit after power costs."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What Drives Mining Profit?",
        "formula": "profit = (reward × price) − (power × hours × price/kWh)",
        "explanation": [
          "Mining profit depends on your hardware's hashrate, the coin price, network difficulty, and your electricity cost. Higher difficulty means less reward per unit of work.",
          "Profitability changes daily with price and difficulty, so estimates are snapshots, not guarantees."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does profit change daily?",
            "a": "Coin price and network difficulty move constantly, and your reward is shared among all miners on the network."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is electricity the main cost?",
            "a": "Usually yes. For many GPUs and ASICs, power cost decides whether mining is profitable at all."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the calculator include pool fees?",
            "a": "You can add a pool fee percentage; the estimate then subtracts it from gross rewards for a realistic net figure."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "wallet-validator": {
      "name": "Wallet Validator",
      "description": "Validate Bitcoin, Ethereum, and Litecoin wallet addresses",
      "metaDescription": "Free online wallet address validator. Check Bitcoin, Ethereum and more addresses for valid format and checksum fast, no signup.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Paste the encrypted currency wallet address to check.",
          "Select the block chain (bitcoin, Ethera, etc.).",
          "Reads whether format and checksum is valid."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Why check your wallet address?",
        "formula": "Address Checksum and Format Validation",
        "explanation": [
          "The wallet address is a public sign of receipt of funds. Even if a character is wrong, the money is sent to the wrong address that cannot be recovered.",
          "Validation check the address length, character set and checksum so you can find input errors before you transfer them.",
          "The central mechanism for address verification is ** checksum**: Hashi calculation of address subject, with several bytes added at the end. Any input error leads to a mismatch and is rejected. Base58Check for bitcoin will attach four bytes to the end of the address and detect most spelling errors -- –** This is why the address can be \"safe copyed\" instead of having to be character-to-characterized** (of course, large transfers are still recommended for cross-checking 4-6 bits).",
          "** Case is sometimes meaningful ** According to the Taifung EIP-55: a mixed case address that meets the specified rules is self-checked, while a full lowercase address is not verified. Therefore, changing the commingling case address to full lowercase, although most wallets still accept, would lose the verification protection. Special attention should also be paid to ** cross-chain transfers**: transfer of ERC-20 tokens to a bitcoin address or to a chain that does not support the token, where assets are usually not recoverable. ** The type of network identified before the transfer is more important than the address itself. **"
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can verification confirm ownership?",
            "a": "Can't. It only confirms that the address format is correct and cannot tell you who controls it."
          },
          {
            "q": "Could it be wrong to verify the address?",
            "a": "Maybe. It is valid in format but may belong to another person, and please check the full address with the recipient."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which block chains are supported?",
            "a": "Support for common formats such as Bitcoin (Base58Check) and Taipei (EIP-55 Checksum)."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the address have to be legal?",
            "a": "Not necessarily. The checksum only proves that \"this string conforms to the format rule\" and that the address has been created or has a balance. The block chain address does not need to be registered and anyone can create a legal address offline. So,** the transfer of the currency to a correct but unincorporated address would result in the permanent loss of assets**. This is also why the first transfer was recommended for a micro-test."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does some address look like it?",
            "a": "Base58 deliberately removed 0, O, I, l, but the same address-to-face \"Toxic attack\" still exists: the assailant first transfers you a very small token, the address at the end is similar to your usual collection address and induces you to copy the false address in the history record. The precautionary approach is to copy the full address from a credible source (e.g., face-to-face confirmation or official channels) and to check the middle part** rather than the first few."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Mainstream block chain address comparison",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Chain",
          "Prefix/ Format",
          "Length",
          "Verification"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Bitcoin bitcoin",
            "1… / 3… / bc1…",
            "26–35 / 42–62",
            "Base58Check / Bech32"
          ],
          [
            "Ethereum Etherme",
            "0x + 40-bit hexadecimal",
            "42",
            "EIP-55 Case Verify"
          ],
          [
            "Litecoin Lightcoin",
            "L… / M… / ltc1…",
            "26–34 / 43",
            "Base58Check / Bech32"
          ],
          [
            "Ripple XRP",
            "r…",
            "25–35",
            "Base58 + checksum"
          ],
          [
            "Dogcoin. Dogcoin.",
            "D…",
            "34",
            "Base58Check"
          ],
          [
            "Tron.",
            "T…",
            "34",
            "Base58Check"
          ],
          [
            "Solana",
            "Base58, no prefix",
            "32–44",
            "No internal verification (fault-prone)"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "mortgage-swiss": {
      "name": "Swiss Mortgage Calculator",
      "description": "Calculate mortgage payments with CHF/EUR support and Swiss market rates",
      "metaDescription": "Free Swiss mortgage calculator with CHF and EUR support. Calculate monthly payments, amortization schedules, and total costs with Swiss market rates.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the property price, down payment and the Swiss reference or SARON-based rate.",
          "Enter the term and any amortization requirements (typically to 2/3 of value).",
          "Read the monthly installment, total interest and remaining debt schedule."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Swiss mortgage calculation",
        "formula": "annualInterest = rate × debt; amortize to ≤ 2/3 of property value per rules",
        "explanation": [
          "Swiss mortgages commonly use a reference rate (historically SARON or formerly Libor) plus a bank margin to set the interest.",
          "Borrowers must amortize the portion above two-thirds of the property value within about 15 years, which drives the required payment."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What reference rate is used in Switzerland?",
            "a": "Most new loans use SARON plus a margin; older ones may still reference a fixed or Libor-based rate."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why the two-thirds rule?",
            "a": "Swiss regulation requires amortizing debt above 2/3 of value to limit loan-to-value risk."
          },
          {
            "q": "Fixed or variable rate?",
            "a": "Borrowers choose between fixed (predictable) and variable (tracks reference rate) structures."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "rental-yield": {
      "name": "Rental Yield Calculator",
      "description": "Calculate gross and net rental yield for Swiss property investments",
      "metaDescription": "Free rental yield calculator for Swiss property investors. Calculate gross and net yields, compare to market averages, and analyze investment returns.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter property price (or down payment + loan) and monthly/annual rent.",
          "Enter holding costs: fees, taxes, vacancy and repair reserves.",
          "See gross and net rental yield, plus payback years."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Rental yield: gross vs net",
        "formula": "gross = annual rent ÷ price; net = (annual rent − costs) ÷ invested capital",
        "explanation": [
          "Gross yield only uses 'annual rent ÷ price'—simple but overstates, ignoring taxes, vacancy, repairs. Net yield (after all holding costs) is the real return.",
          "Base capital on 'what you actually put in': full price if cash, or down payment + interest if financed—otherwise leverage inflates your yield.",
          "This tool gives gross/net yield and payback, helping compare 'buy-to-let' against 'save-and-invest' on equal footing."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How far apart are gross and net?",
            "a": "Depends on costs; net is often 2–4 points below gross. Ignoring costs badly overstates return."
          },
          {
            "q": "How to count capital with a mortgage?",
            "a": "Use down payment + cumulative interest as your real outlay; not the full price, or leverage inflates yield."
          },
          {
            "q": "What yield is good?",
            "a": "No universal standard—compare local price-to-rent, risk-free rate, and your taxes. The tool gives numbers; judge with your market."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "property-valuation": {
      "name": "Property Valuation Calculator",
      "description": "Estimate Swiss property values using the hedonic method",
      "metaDescription": "Free Swiss property valuation calculator. Estimate apartment and house values based on location, size, condition, and features.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter comparable sales prices and key diffs (area, age, floor, etc.).",
          "Pick method: comparison (nearby sales) or income (rent discounted).",
          "See a valuation range and the weight of key adjustments."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Property valuation: comparison vs income",
        "formula": "comparison ≈ unit price × area ± diff; income ≈ net annual rent ÷ cap rate",
        "explanation": [
          "The comparison (market) approach is most common: take recent similar nearby sales and adjust for area, age, orientation, floor, etc., to value the subject.",
          "The income approach suits investments: annual net rent ÷ capitalization rate (cap rate) gives value, reflecting cash flow. Less relevant for pure owner-occupancy.",
          "This tool gives a range, not a point—real deals are swayed by renovation, urgency, school district, so a band is more honest than false precision."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why a range, not a number?",
            "a": "Comps always differ, and mood, finish, urgency matter. A range reflects uncertainty more honestly than a fake point."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which method is accurate?",
            "a": "Owner-occupier: comparison (local sales). Investor: income (cash flow). Combine both: anchor with comparison, validate with income."
          },
          {
            "q": "Where does cap rate come from?",
            "a": "From local same-type property price-to-rent ratios set by the market; infer from agent or public transaction data."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "sun-position": {
      "name": "Sun Position",
      "description": "Calculate sun position, twilight times, and light phases for photography",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate sun altitude, azimuth, twilight times, golden hour and blue hour for photography planning",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the latitude, longitude, date and local time.",
          "Read the height and azimuth of the sun at that time.",
          "The golden moment, shadow or solar alignment is planned from an angle."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Sun position: Height angle and azimuth",
        "formula": "Sin (altitude angle) = sin (latitude) sin (latitude) + cos (latitude) cos (latitude) cos (latitude) cos (time angle)",
        "explanation": [
          "The location of the Sun depends on the location, the date of the year (latitude) and the time (time angle).",
          "The height angle is the angle of the horizon of the Sun; the azimuth is the direction of the compass to which the Sun is directed.",
          "Angular formula at midday sun height: **h = 90° - Local latitude + Sun at bare latitude** (summer to +23.5°, winter to −23.5°, spring and autumn at 0°). In Beijing (about 40°N) the winter to midday sun is only about 26.5°, summer to around 73.5° -** This is why the winter house is lighted and summer needs sun cover**. This pattern is very useful for building light, building photocopy and photo-sighting: to film the side of the building, to select the solar altitude close to the time of the building's direction.",
          "** Sun azimuth determines the direction of sunrise and sunset**: north-east, north-west, north-west and winter, south-west, south-west, north-west, north-east, north-east, north-east, south-east, south-west, north-east, north-east, north-east, north-east, south-east, south-east, south-west, north-west, north-east, north-east, summer, south-east, south-east, south-east, south-west, south-west, south-west, north-east, north-east, north-east, summer, south-east, south-east, south-west, south-west, north-west, north-east, south-west, south-west, north-east, north-east, south-east, south-west, south-west, south-west, south-west, south-west, south-west, south-west, south-west, north-west, north-west, north-west, north, north-east, north-east, north, south, south-east, south, north, north, north, This knowledge is directly used in the following: a wind camera selection (in order to shoot Sunset Golden Mountain, look at the point of sunrise on that day and head forward to the east/north-east of the mountain); 2 architectural shade design; and 3 blue-tempo (Sunset + Blue = Best Window). ** Practical tools: PhotoPhills, Sun Surveyor, Planit! The solar/lunar position and trajectories at any date and time location can be calculated with precision and are the frame of the wind. **"
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What's the sun?",
            "a": "It is the latitude of the Sun on the sky, which varies from −23.4° to +23.4° within a year."
          },
          {
            "q": "How's the precision?",
            "a": "Planning error of about 1°; air cover difference near the horizon is not modelled."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do you plan with azimuth?",
            "a": "The azimuth predicts the position of the Sun, which is used for alignment and shadow direction."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do you predict the location of the gold moment?",
            "a": "Four steps: Identification of the subject(s) to be photographed (mounts, buildings, lakes); 2 Use of App to check the azimuth of sunset/sunrise on the day (e.g. north-west of Beijing summer sunset, approximately 300° + 3); 3 Identification of an unshielded position on the map/field (Google Earth's \"Sunlight\" or Sun Surveyor's AR preview is visual); 4 ** Step forward or at least one hour ahead of the scene** - the light changes fast, and the position, structure, parameters must be in place. ** The best light for a golden moment is only 10 minutes and it's time to start over, so \"foreseeability\" is more important than technology. **"
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is it still light in the summer and the sun is set?",
            "a": "Because of ** civilian twilight** — during the time when the sun falls below the horizon at 0-6°, the high atmosphere still scatters sunlight on the ground, and it is not completely dark. This period of time (about 20-30 minutes behind) is the blue moment, the city's light and the sky's balance, the golden window of the night. And down to Twilight, 12-18° below the horizon, it's all dark. ** So \"the sun goes down\" and \"the sun goes down\" and \"the sun goes down\" and \"the sun goes down\" and \"the sun goes down\" and \"the night scene\" is about the blue of 20-30 minutes, not the moment the sun just disappeared. **Note: The higher the latitudes, the longer the summers, the longer the twilight, and even the \"white night\" in the Arctic Circle (the sun does not fall at 6° below the horizon)."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Seasonal changes in the Sun's position in the northern hemisphere (at midday sun height)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Gas/date",
          "Sun Declination",
          "Beijing (40°N) at noon height",
          "Sunrise."
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Spring Episode 3/21",
            "0°",
            "50°",
            "East"
          ],
          [
            "Summer to 6/21",
            "+23.5°",
            "73.5°",
            "Northeast"
          ],
          [
            "Autumn 9/23",
            "0°",
            "50°",
            "East"
          ],
          [
            "Winter to 12/22",
            "−23.5°",
            "26.5°",
            "South-east"
          ],
          [
            "Any day",
            "δ",
            "90° - Latitude + zirconium",
            "—"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "circle-of-confusion": {
      "name": "Circle of Confusion",
      "description": "Calculate CoC from sensor, print size, and viewing distance",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate circle of confusion for depth of field",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the view distance and the size of the image.",
          "Enter the visual acuity (resolution angle, usually 1/1500 ~ 1/2000).",
          "Reads the dispersive circle diameter used for the bathymetric calculation."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "CoC Detail",
        "formula": "c = view distance x tan (dissolved angle) (convertable to sensor)",
        "explanation": [
          "Fragmentation is the largest blurry circle diameter that the viewer still sees as a point.",
          "It depends on the size of the image and the distance from which it is viewed: a smaller CoC is needed for a larger picture to come closer.",
          "Circle of Confusion is the fuzzy circle ** formed by point light sources when they lose focus**. When this circle is small enough to be invisible in the final output (photo/screen), it is considered \"clear\" and the critical diameter is \"permissible dispersing circle\". It is the baseline for bathymetric calculations.",
          "**Standard extrapolation**: Assuming that 8x10 inches (approximately 20x25 cm) of photographs are viewed at a distance of 25 cm, human eye resolution is about 1/1500 rad, the identifiable diameter is about 0.17 mm. The full picture is zooming up to 8x10 approximately 8 times (250 ÷ 30.4 mm diagonal), so the permissible dispersing circle on the sensor = 0.17 ÷ 8 ≈ 0.021 mm. ** The most commonly used simplified formula is \"Screen diagonal ÷ 1500\"**, full 43.3 ÷ 1500 ≈ 0.029 mm. There was a slight difference in the value taken by different manufacturers (0.025-0.035), but the impact was modest."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "COC, how much?",
            "a": "Common defaults: Full drawings 0.03mm, APS-C 0.02mm, M4/3 0.015mm."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does output size affect COC?",
            "a": "Yes; larger maps require smaller COCs to maintain the same sense of clarity."
          },
          {
            "q": "Co.C. is the physical spot on the sensor?",
            "a": "It is a derived tolerance, not a sensor physical feature, and is used to drive the bathymetric equation."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do you want a smaller COC with a high pixel camera?",
            "a": "Depends on the output. The CEC is based on \"normal viewing distance and looking at the whole picture.\" If you are **100% zoom in on the screen** (equivalent to magnifying to a very large size view), you should really use a smaller CoC (recommended to divide by a pixel gain factor such as 60 million pixels for 0.015 mm), at which point the depth of the view will be significantly lighter —** that is why high pixel bodyshots are more likely to be \"crystalized\" and why high pixels require more sharpness and focus. **Practical filming proposal: take the usual COC, but know that the magnifying examination will be more rigorous and that a few more copies will be taken."
          },
          {
            "q": "What does coc have to do with pixel size?",
            "a": "They are independent but they interact. The pixel spacing of a full 45 million pixel sensor is about 4.3 μm, while the COC is 30 μm -- so the \"clear\" decision cuts across about seven pixels, and that's why the depth is gradient rather than mutation. But if the pixel density is very high (e.g. 100 million pixels painted, pixel spacing 3.8 μm), the same COC crosses more pixels and magnifies the view to see more \"not sharper\" areas. **Simplicit conclusion: the higher the pixels, the higher the focus accuracy, the deeper the actual available view. **"
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "The standard acceptable dispersing circle value for each painting",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Painting",
          "Sensor Size",
          "CoC（mm）",
          "Equivalent coefficient"
