    "bpm": {
      "name": "BPM",
      "description": "Convert BPM to Hz, bar length, and note durations",
      "metaDescription": "Convert beats per minute to other musical timing units",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the number of beats and the time (seconds or minutes) to be taken.",
          "Runs the calculator for a minute to count.",
          "View BPM; if you click an input, show the average speed of the click."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What's a BPM?",
        "formula": "bpm = taken / (seconds / 60) = taken x 60 / second",
        "explanation": [
          "BPM (number per minute) is the velocity unit of the music, indicating how many stable pulses are per minute.",
          "The metronome marker 120 BPM is two beats per second; a double BPM will make music sound twice as fast.",
          "BPM (Beats Per Minute) is the nodal number per minute, the larger the value the faster. It is very simple to convert to single-shot hours: ** single-shot milliseconds = 60000 ÷ BPM**. 120 BPM per 500 ms, 60 BPM per 1000 ms. This formula is used daily in audio editing, synchronisation of light, video card clips -- knowing that BPM can calculate the length of each subsection (4 beat): 120 BPM in 2 seconds.",
          "** BPM decides on speed, and it's on rhythm**. The most common popular rhythms are 4/4 beats (4 quartiles per subsection); 3/4 beats are chords of chorus; and 6/8 beats with a swing. The same 120 BPM, 4/4 and 6/8 sound completely different. When you do video card clips, you usually click \"one shot per N\", so you determine the BPM before you fix the clip rhythm, and the picture and the music go together."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How do you get by clicking on BPM?",
            "a": "The tool averages the speed of the interval for each click."
          },
          {
            "q": "The higher the BPM, the faster?",
            "a": "Yes, more pulses per minute, more moving."
          },
          {
            "q": "What's the typical speed of a song?",
            "a": "The popular music is in 100-130 BPM; it's slower and faster."
          },
          {
            "q": "DJ, why do you match BPM?",
            "a": "To keep the two songs seamlessly connected. If two songs are two different BPMs, they're staggered every minute, and in a few seconds the drums are completely out of place, and it sounds messy. The DJ approach is to move the BPMs of the two songs to the same (singing the speed of the sings or synchronized with the software) and to switch them when the starting point of the section is aligned. Usually, the speed range is within > 6%, and people above this range can hear a clear tune change in their ears."
          },
          {
            "q": "What's the run speed, BPM?",
            "a": "Step 180 paces/minutes is the classic goal of long run (research shows that elitist runners are generally around 180). You can choose 180 BPM music per step, or 90 BPM music per step. Medium-intensity jogging corresponds to 150-165 BPM, which can be used for fast or intermittent training. That's what a lot of running music platforms do by BPM classification lists."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Common music speed term versus BPM",
