    "video-file-size": {
      "name": "Video File Size",
      "description": "Estimate video file size from resolution and duration",
      "metaDescription": "Estimate video file sizes based on settings",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the video duration, resolution, and target quality.",
          "Enter the codec and frame rate, or pick a preset.",
          "Read the estimated file size and adjust bitrate to fit your storage."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Estimating video file size",
        "formula": "size = bitrate × duration; bitrate ≈ resolution × fps × qualityFactor",
        "explanation": [
          "Video file size is driven mainly by bitrate and duration; resolution and frame rate set the bitrate needed for a given quality.",
          "Modern codecs like H.265/HEVC and AV1 compress far better than older H.264 at the same perceived quality, shrinking files significantly."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Does 4K quadruple the size of 1080p?",
            "a": "Not exactly; 4K has 4x pixels but efficient codecs and smart bitrate caps keep the increase smaller."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which codec saves the most space?",
            "a": "AV1 and H.265 typically beat H.264 by 30–50% at equal quality."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is audio a small part of size?",
            "a": "Audio bitrates (128–320 kbps) are tiny next to video bitrates (several Mbps)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "audio-filesize": {
      "name": "Audio File Size",
      "description": "Estimate audio file size from duration and format",
      "metaDescription": "Estimate audio file sizes for different formats",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the audio length and sampling rate (e.g. 44.1 kHz).",
          "Select a bit depth or code rate (no damage or impairment).",
          "Reads the estimated file size under the corresponding format."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Resize Audio Files",
        "formula": "Size = sample rate x bit depth x number of acoustic channels x length (PCM); or code rate x length (compression)",
        "explanation": [
          "The size of uncompressed audio increases with sampling rates, bit depths and acoustics; stereos are twice as large as a monosound.",
          "MP3/AAC, for example, is stored in target-code rates, while FLAC does not compress PCM data without loss and does not discard information.",
          "Audio volume formulae (detrimental format): ** Size (MB) = code rate (kbps) x length (sec) ÷ 8 ÷ 1024**. Example: 4-minute songs from 320 kbps = 320 x 240 ÷ 8 ÷ 1024 ≈ 9.4 MB. WAV is calculated in a different manner, depending on the sampling rate, bit depth, acoustic number:** bytes per second = sampling rate x bit depth ÷ 8 x acoustics**. CD sound (44.1 kHz, 16 bit, stereo) = 44100 x 2 x 2 = 176,400 bytes/sec, approximately 10.1 MB/min.",
          "** Non-destructive formats do not all account for large space**: FLAC, ALAC, APE are all compressed and intact (like ZIP), so FLAC is about 300-400 MB an hour, and WAV is about 635 MB. Selection proposal: The parent tape archive is WAV/AIFF, the music collection is FLAC, and mobile devices are 256-320 kbps AAC/MP3. ** Note that MP3 is a proprietary format (now expired), AAC is slightly more efficient than MP3 (better sound at the same code) and Opus is the best performer (suitable for voice and real-time communication) at a low-code rate. **"
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "WAV with FLAC who's big?",
            "a": "WAV is the original PCM (maximum); FLAC undamaged compression is usually 50-60% small."
          },
          {
            "q": "Better than that?",
            "a": "When only sound sources and ears can be used; 44.1/48 kHz is enough for most listeners."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the stereo twice as mono?",
            "a": "Two channels each store independent samples, and the data rate doubles."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the same rate AAC better than MP3?",
            "a": "AAC is a successor to MP3 and has improved several techniques: more flexible block length, better stereocoding, more efficient entropy, and better transient processing. The AAC sound quality is clearly better than MP3 at the measured homogeneity rate, especially under 128 kbps, which is why the iTunes/YouTube uses AAC. The low-rate (< 96 kbps) scene gap is even more exaggerated, at which point Opus is clearly better than AAC. In pursuing volume efficiency, the priority is: Opus > AAC > MP3."
          },
          {
            "q": "Will conversion lose the sound quality?",
            "a": "** The conversion between impaired formats will inevitably accumulate losses** (MP3 → AAC → MP3 with each recoding drop of information once more and three generations later with obvious underperformance). The correct course of action is to keep a non-damaged mother belt (FLAC/WAV) at all times and to transfer it once in whatever format it requires. ** No loss, no loss, no loss** (WAV → FLAC → WAV received the same bytes of the original document). So it's pointless to \"turn the music to MP3 and return to nothing\" — lost information won't come back."
          }
        ],
