    "common-bitrates": {
      "name": "Common Bitrates Reference",
      "description": "Reference guide for ProRes, DNxHD, DCP, and MPEG bitrates",
      "metaDescription": "Reference guide for video codec bitrates",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick the delivery target: streaming, broadcast, disc, or mastering.",
          "Pick the resolution and frame rate you will use.",
          "Read the typical bitrate range and choose one that balances quality and bandwidth."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Common video bitrates by use case",
        "formula": "Reference only: 1080p ≈ 5–8 Mbps (H.264), 4K ≈ 15–25 Mbps (H.264), lower with H.265/AV1",
        "explanation": [
          "Bitrate tables give starting points; the right value depends on codec, motion complexity, and acceptable artifact levels.",
          "Streaming services use adaptive bitrate ladders; disc and broadcast have stricter standards like Blu-ray or ATSC."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why do tables vary so much?",
            "a": "They assume different codecs and content; fast action needs more bits than static talking heads."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is higher bitrate always better?",
            "a": "Up to a point; beyond it you waste bandwidth for invisible gains."
          },
          {
            "q": "What about H.265/AV1?",
            "a": "They hit the same quality at roughly half the H.264 bitrate."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "dcp-filesize": {
      "name": "DCP File Size",
      "description": "Calculate Digital Cinema Package file sizes",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate DCP file sizes for cinema distribution",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the feature duration and chosen resolution (2K or 4K).",
          "Pick the frame rate (24, 25, 30, 48, or 60 fps) and JPEG 2000 bit rate.",
          "Read the total DCP package size including audio and subtitle assets."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Digital Cinema Package file size",
        "formula": "size ≈ (videoBitrate + audioBitrate) × duration; typical 4K @ 24fps ≈ 250 Mbps",
        "explanation": [
          "A DCP stores each frame as a JPEG 2000 image plus uncompressed or PCM audio, so sizes are huge compared with consumer formats.",
          "DCPs are built per-version (2D/3D, subtitle languages), and each version multiplies storage and ingest time."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why are DCPs so large?",
            "a": "They use near-lossless JPEG 2000 at cinema bitrates, far above streaming compression."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the 3D surcharge?",
            "a": "3D doubles the picture data, roughly doubling the video portion of the package."
          },
          {
            "q": "How long to ingest a DCP?",
            "a": "Depends on drive speed; a 200 GB package can take 20–40 minutes to copy to a server."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "foot-lambert": {
      "name": "Foot-Lambert",
      "description": "Calculate screen luminance for cinema projection",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate screen brightness for cinema projection",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the brightness (fL or nits) measured.",
          "Select the direction of the conversion.",
          "Reads the equivalent under another unit."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "foot-lambert and nits",
        "formula": "1 fL = 3.426 nits（cd/m²）；1 nit = 0.2919 fL",
        "explanation": [
          "Foot-lambert is a unit of beauty for cinema and projection; Nits (Kendra/m2) is an international unit.",
          "The typical dark cinema screens are about 14-16 fL, and bright living room televisions can exceed 500-1000 nits.",
          "Foot-lambert (fL, foot Rambo) is the unit for the projection industry to measure ** the reflection brightness of the screen**: 1 fL = 1/ π Candra ≈ 3.426 nits (cd/m2) per square foot. The reason for using \"reflection brightness\" rather than projector luminaries is that ** The light that people see reflected from the screen depends on the projector brightness, the benefit of the screen and the size of the screen **. Formula: fL = projector flow x screen gain ÷ screen area (square feet).",
          "For example: 3,000 luminous projector, shot at 120 inches 16:9 of the gain 1.0 (approx. 37.5 square feet, i.e. 121.8 cm x 68.5 cm converted), brightness = 3000 x 1.0 ÷ 37.5 = 80 fL - etc.), this result is high because it is actually multiplied by the coefficients associated with the thorium and takes into account the loss of light (light bulb decline, camera percelerity approximately 0.8). **Practical experience formula: fL ≈ gain x 0.85 ÷ area (square feet)**, the previous example of about 68 fL, still well above the cinema standard, indicates that family environment 1500-2000 fluency is usually sufficient; the luminous living room of the environment requires over 3000 fluency."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why do cinemas use fl?",
            "a": "It is the traditional unit of the United States market's projection screen."
          },
          {
            "q": "14 fl?",
            "a": "About 48 Nits (14 x 3.426), a classic SMPTE cinema target."
          },
          {
            "q": "Could HDR's nits match fL?",
            "a": ";HDR peak nits measure high light, not stable screen fL."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is it like ANSI?",
            "a": "It's different. ** ANSI Fluorescent** is a good-spectrum (takes an average of 9 points) and is the most authoritative;** ISO Fluorescent** is a dailies (most of the daily plants are commercial) and the values are approximately the same as ANSI; and many low-priced projectors are called \"light-sourced Fluorescent\" \"LED Fluorescent\" (LED) is a theoretical value that has not been standardizedly measured and may be 2-4 times that of ANSI Fluent,** with no reference value at all**. How to judge: The 1,000-dollar LED projector says \"5,000 Fluorescent\" is almost certainly a false sign, and real ANSI is probably 300-800. Pre-purchase physical data from professional assessments."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why are the projections dark in the daytime?",
            "a": "Because the projection is ** reflecting **, the environment will be diluted. Contrast = image brightness ÷ environmental light reflection, around 300-500 lux during the day, projection brightness may be only 50-100 lux, contrasting to extremely low, ash. Solutions: 1 luminous (high curtains with the most direct effect); 2 Increased projector brightness (marginal gains are decreasing and doubling can only raise one view); 3 ALR curtains, absorbing environmental light through reflections of special optical structures, with significant but high prices; 4 Acceptance of reality - projection is inherently suitable for the dark room and the bright environment should buy television."
          }
