      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Paste the URL or query string to encode.",
          "Choose percent-encoding (URL encode) or decode.",
          "Read the result where unsafe characters become %XX escapes."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What is URL encoding?",
        "formula": "space → %20; non-ASCII → UTF-8 bytes as %XX",
        "explanation": [
          "A URL may safely contain only a few characters (letters, digits and a few symbols). Spaces, Chinese text, & and = have special meaning or cannot travel, so they must be escaped as % followed by two hex digits.",
          "Encoding lets links parse and transmit correctly, avoiding truncated parameters or injection. Decoding reverses it, turning %XX back to the original. It appears in form posts, API calls and query building."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does space become %20?",
            "a": "Space is not URL-safe; the standard escape is %20 (a + variant exists for form spaces)."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why encode Chinese?",
            "a": "URLs are ASCII-based; Chinese is first turned into UTF-8 bytes, then each byte into %XX, and decoded back on receipt."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does encoding lose information?",
            "a": "No. It is reversible; decoding restores the exact original string."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "csp": {
      "name": "CSP Generator",
      "description": "Generate Content Security Policy headers",
      "metaDescription": "Generate Content Security Policy headers for web security",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the policy directives you plan to allow (default-src, script-src, img-src, etc.).",
          "Enter the domains or schemes you trust as sources.",
          "Read the generated Content-Security-Policy header to paste into your server config."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Building a Content Security Policy",
        "formula": "CSP = directive1 value1 value2; directive2 value3; ...",
        "explanation": [
          "A CSP whitelists where the browser may load scripts, styles, images and connections, blocking injection of unauthorized content even if an XSS bug exists.",
          "Start in report-only mode to log violations before enforcing, otherwise a too-strict policy can break legitimate resources on your site."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What does default-src do?",
            "a": "It sets the fallback for any directive you do not specify explicitly, so 'self' there covers most tags."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I use report-only first?",
            "a": "Yes, monitor reported violations for a while so you can tune the policy without breaking the site."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does CSP stop all XSS?",
            "a": "It drastically reduces impact but is a defense-in-depth layer, not a replacement for input sanitization."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "redirect-check": {
      "name": "Redirect Checker",
      "description": "Check HTTP redirect chains and status codes",
      "metaDescription": "Check URL redirect chains and HTTP status codes",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the source URL to test with the desired ultimate destination address.",
          "Enter the requested header, e. g. User-Agent or Authorization.",
          "Views the complete redirection chain and the final HTTP status code."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Redirect HTTP",
        "formula": "Chain = URL0→ (301/302) URL1→(...) URL(state code)",
        "explanation": [
          "The re-direction chain increases the delay, and if a link is reached by multiple jumps to the standard page, it may undermine the analysis data or SEO.",
          "Single clean 301 best preserves the link weight; multiple-jumping chains dilute the weight and slow the user ' s first byte time.",
          "The difference between 301 and 302 is not just \"permanent vs temporary\": **301 will pass the page weight (SEO's PageRank concept) to the new address, 302 not**. The site has to be re-edited and changed domain names in 301, and the use of 302 will result in the search engine continuing to index its old address, and the weights cannot be moved. In addition, 301 will be permanently cached by browsers and, if wrongly matched, users may jump -** test 301 with a traceless window or curl to avoid being misled by local caches. **",
          "** Redirected chain is performance and SEO's invisible killer**: A→B→C adds a full RRTT to each jump, and three jumps can add hundreds of milliseconds; search engines can reduce capture efficiency and even abandon chains that are above 3. Common causes: HTTP→HTTPS once, non-www@www.un.org, table of contents slash once, underwritten once. The solution would be to complete all regularizations at the edge node (CDN/Reverse Agent) at once** and jump to the final address with a single rule."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "301 or 302. How?",
            "a": "Permanent migration is 301 to convey SEO weights; temporary or A/B tests 302."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why avoid re-directional chains?",
            "a": "Each jump increases the number of trips and may lose the source data and ranking signals."
          },
          {
            "q": "How long should re-direction wait?",
            "a": "Redirection should be instantaneous (without delay) and browsers should follow immediately."
          },
          {
            "q": "What about redirecting?",
            "a": "Typical scenario: HTTPS station is behind the CDN, which returns with HTTP and the station requests HTTPS, and then circulates indefinitely. Scrutinizing step: 1 tracks the complete jump chain with curl-I-L; 2 checks for multiple layers of re-direction (CDN, Nginx, application layer each with one); 3 confirms whether the X-Forwarded-Proto head is correctly passed and whether the source station judges the protocol accordingly. Repair: Allows the source station to make only one judgement or to standardize the UDP protocol at the CDN level."
          },
          {
            "q": "302 will pass SEO weight?",
            "a": "Traditionally not, but Google announced in 2016 that \"302 is used as a hint rather than a directive\" - if you keep 302 for a long time, Google will judge itself and treat it by 301. But Bing, 100 degrees, and so on, the syntax of 302 is temporary. ** The normative approach remains the determination of permanent migration for 301, without relying on the error of the search engine. ** Short-term activity page, A/B test for 302."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "HTTP Redirect status code differentiation",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Status Code",
          "Meaning",
          "Retention of method",
          "Cacheable",
          "Typical uses"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "301",
            "Redirect permanently",
            "For most read GET",
            "Cacheable",
            "Domain name migration, HTTP→HTTPS"
          ],
          [
            "302",
            "Interim reorientation",
            "For most read GET",
            "Can not open message",
            "Temporary maintenance, A/B testing"
          ],
          [
            "307",
            "Interim reorientation",
            "Strict reservations",
            "Can not open message",
            "Save POST temporary jump"
          ],
          [
            "308",
            "Redirect permanently",
            "Strict reservations",
            "Cacheable",
            "Retain permanent POST jump"
          ],
          [
            "303",
            "See Other",
            "Replace with GET",
            "Can not open message",
            "POST Post Jump to Result Page"
          ],
          [
            "meta refresh",
            "HTML Class Jump",
            "—",
            "—",
            "Not recommended, SEO"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "spf-check": {
      "name": "SPF Record Checker",
      "description": "Analyze SPF records for email authentication",
      "metaDescription": "Analyze SPF records for email security",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your domain name to query its published SPF DNS record.",
          "Enter the mail servers or include mechanisms you expect to see.",
          "Read the resolved SPF policy and whether it passes or has too many DNS lookups."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Validating SPF records",
        "formula": "SPF = 'v=spf1 include:_spf.a include:_spf.b -all' (max 10 DNS-lookup mechanisms)",
        "explanation": [
          "SPF tells receiving mail servers which IPs may send mail for your domain; a missing or broken record lets attackers spoof your address.",
          "SPF allows at most 10 DNS lookups across all include mechanisms, so over-nesting includes causes permerror and mail may be rejected."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What does -all mean?",
            "a": "A hard fail: reject mail from sources not in the policy. ~all is softfail, more permissive."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why the 10-lookup limit?",
            "a": "To bound DNS query load; exceeding it yields permerror and receivers may reject."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can SPF alone stop spoofing?",
            "a": "No, pair it with DKIM and DMARC for real protection and reporting."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "https-check": {
      "name": "HTTPS Security Checker",
      "description": "Check security headers and TLS configuration",
      "metaDescription": "Check website HTTPS and security headers",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the hostname or full URL you want to test over HTTPS.",
          "Enter the port if it is not the default 443.",
          "Read the certificate validity, chain completeness and protocol grade."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Checking HTTPS health",
        "formula": "grade = f(cipherSuites, certExpiry, chainComplete, HSTS)",
        "explanation": [
          "HTTPS health combines a valid certificate, a complete trust chain, modern ciphers and HSTS; any gap can trigger browser warnings or downgrade attacks.",
          "Expired or mismatched certificates are the most common cause of 'not secure' banners and lost user trust at checkout."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is HSTS and why enable it?",
            "a": "HSTS forces HTTPS and blocks SSL-strip downgrade attacks after the first visit."
          },
          {
            "q": "What breaks the trust chain?",
            "a": "A missing intermediate certificate on the server, so the browser cannot link leaf to root."
          },
          {
            "q": "How early renew certificates?",
            "a": "Automate via ACME (Let's Encrypt) with renewal 30 days before expiry to avoid outages."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "html-encoder": {
      "name": "HTML Encoder",
      "description": "Encode and decode HTML entities",
      "metaDescription": "Encode and decode HTML special characters",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Paste HTML text needing escaping (with < > & etc.).",
          "Choose encode (escape special chars) or decode (restore).",
          "Copy the result for web code, form values, or docs."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What is HTML escaping and why",
        "formula": "map: & → &amp;  < → &lt;  > → &gt;  \" → &quot;  ' → &#39;",
        "explanation": [
          "HTML escaping replaces syntax-meaning chars with entity references so the browser won't treat them as tags or attributes. E.g. write < as &lt; to display a literal less-than.",
          "When echoing user input back to a page, correct escaping prevents XSS (cross-site scripting)—a Web-security basic. Frameworks often auto-escape; manual HTML concatenation needs care.",
          "This tool encodes/decodes both ways, handy to check what a snippet really renders as, or to restore someone else's escaped content."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Is escaping the same as encryption?",
            "a": "No. Escaping only strips syntactic meaning; anyone can decode it. It's not secret and doesn't replace encryption."
          },
          {
            "q": "When must I escape?",
            "a": "Whenever inserting data into HTML, attributes, URLs, or JS contexts. Riskiest is user input concatenated straight into a page—use context-correct escaping."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does decoding differ from the original?",
            "a": "If the source already had entities (e.g. &amp;), decoding restores &. Expected; watch idempotency on repeated round-trips."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "html-chars": {
      "name": "HTML Character Map",
      "description": "Browse and copy HTML character entities",
      "metaDescription": "Reference guide for HTML character entities",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Type or paste text with special characters.",
          "Choose convert to HTML entities (e.g. © → &copy;) or reverse.",
          "See each character's named and numeric entity, then copy."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "HTML character entities: names and numbers",
        "formula": "char → named (&copy;) or numeric (&#169; / &#xA9;)",
        "explanation": [
          "Entities let you insert characters you can't type or that would be misread as tags—copyright ©, trademark ™, non-breaking space. Forms: named (&copy;) and numeric (&#169; or hex &#xA9;).",
          "Named entities read better; some rare symbols exist only as numeric. Numeric entities are maximally compatible and cover any Unicode point.",
          "This tool lists each character's name and number, handy for precise insertion in HTML, email templates, and rich text without errors."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Named or numeric entity?",
            "a": "Named is more readable and maintainable; numeric has the best compatibility and covers any code point. Mixing is fine if your tooling supports it."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do some symbols lack a named entity?",
            "a": "HTML defines a limited set of named entities for standard chars; newer Unicode often only has numeric. Use &#xXXXX; to cover it."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do entities conflict with storing UTF-8 chars?",
            "a": "No. Modern sites mostly store UTF-8 directly; entities are for must-escape or semantics-clear cases."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "emoji-chars": {
      "name": "Emoji Character Map",
      "description": "Browse and copy emoji with codes",
      "metaDescription": "Browse and copy emoji characters",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Type or paste text with emoji / special symbols.",
          "See each character's Unicode code point (U+XXXX) and UTF-8 bytes.",
          "Copy code points or entity form for code and docs."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Emoji and Unicode: reading code points",
        "formula": "code point: U+1F600 (😀); UTF-8 bytes: 0xF0 0x9F 0x98 0x80",
        "explanation": [
          "Every emoji and character has a unique Unicode code point like U+1F600. Many emoji are actually combinations (skin tone, ZWJ), so string length ≠ visible count.",
          "UTF-8, the web's common encoding, turns a code point into 1–4 bytes. Surrogate pairs let beyond-BMP chars exist in UTF-16—why '😀'.length is 2 in JS.",
          "This tool splits text into characters with their points and bytes, great for debugging truncation, mojibake, or wrong length."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does an emoji count as two characters?",
            "a": "Chars beyond the BMP use a surrogate pair in UTF-16; JS counts UTF-16 units, so length is 2."
          },
          {
            "q": "Code point vs UTF-8 encoding?",
            "a": "Code point is the logical number in the Unicode table; UTF-8 is the on-wire byte form. One point maps to 1–4 bytes."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do some emoji show as boxes?",
            "a": "The system or font lacks that glyph and falls back to a box/question mark. It's a font-coverage issue, not encoding."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "seo-performance": {
      "name": "SEO Performance Analyzer",
      "description": "Analyze page SEO metrics and get recommendations",
      "metaDescription": "Analyze SEO performance and get optimization tips",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your page URL and the target keyword or phrase you want to rank for.",
          "Enter the main competitors or current ranking position if known.",
          "Read the on-page, speed and structure suggestions to improve visibility."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Assessing SEO performance",
        "formula": "visibility ∝ (contentRelevance × authority) / (loadTime × crawlErrors)",
        "explanation": [
          "SEO performance blends content relevance, site authority, page speed and clean crawlability; fixing one without the others yields limited gains.",
          "Core Web Vitals and a logical internal link structure often move rankings more than micro-optimizing meta tags once the basics are covered."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What are Core Web Vitals?",
            "a": "Google's LCP, CLS and INP metrics measuring loading, visual stability and interactivity."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does speed really affect ranking?",
            "a": "Yes, both directly via signals and indirectly via bounce rate and engagement."
          },
          {
            "q": "How long until changes show?",
            "a": "Crawl and re-index can take days to weeks; sustained improvement needs consistency."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "mortgage": {
      "name": "Mortgage",
      "description": "Calculate monthly payments, view amortization schedule, and visualize your loan",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate mortgage payments with amortization schedule",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the property price and the down payment percentage or amount; the loan principal is derived automatically.",
          "Fill in the term and the annual rate. Add property tax and home insurance if you know them, for a realistic monthly figure.",
          "See the payment breakdown, principal and interest plus taxes and insurance, and the total interest over the life of the loan."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What a mortgage payment is made of",
        "formula": "Payment = principal and interest + monthly tax + monthly insurance (+ mortgage insurance)",
        "explanation": [
          "Many people assume the payment is only principal and interest, but lenders usually collect property tax and home insurance monthly and bundle them into one figure, known as PITI: principal, interest, taxes and insurance. If the down payment is under 20%, private mortgage insurance is added on top, and it can normally be cancelled once the balance falls below 80% of the property value.",
          "Beyond the monthly payment, buying involves one-off costs: the down payment, closing costs, appraisal and origination fees, typically 2% to 5% of the price. To gauge how much house you can afford, lenders often apply the 28/36 rule: housing costs should stay under 28% of monthly income and total debt payments, including car loans and credit cards, under 36%. It is conservative, but it keeps you out of over-borrowing territory."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "How the down payment changes the payment (300,000 home, 30 years, 4.0%)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Down payment",
          "Principal",
          "Monthly (P&I)",
          "Mortgage insurance",
          "Total interest"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "10%",
            "270,000",
            "≈ 1,289",
            "Required",
            "≈ 194,000"
          ],
          [
            "20%",
            "240,000",
            "≈ 1,146",
            "Not required",
            "≈ 172,500"
          ],
          [
            "30%",
            "210,000",
            "≈ 1,003",
            "Not required",
            "≈ 151,000"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Is a bigger down payment always better?",
            "a": "Not always. More down means a smaller loan, less total interest and no mortgage insurance. But there is an opportunity cost: sinking every saving into the deposit wipes out your emergency buffer. If you have investments that reliably beat the mortgage rate after tax, or you need cash for risk, a smaller deposit can be the better call. Keep three to six months of living costs aside regardless."
          },
          {
            "q": "Fixed or variable rate?",
            "a": "A fixed rate never changes, so the payment is predictable, which suits anyone planning to stay put and valuing certainty. A variable rate usually starts lower but moves with the market and can rise, which suits someone who plans to move or refinance within a few years and can absorb higher payments. The deciding questions are how long you will stay and whether your cash flow survives a rate rise."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is paying the mortgage off early worth it?",
            "a": "Compare the mortgage rate with your alternative after-tax return. If the rate is higher, prepaying locks in a risk-free return at that rate. If not, your money may do better invested, or simply held as an emergency fund. In some countries mortgage interest is tax deductible, so prepaying also gives up that benefit, which belongs in the calculation."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the 28/36 rule?",
            "a": "A rule of thumb for debt capacity: housing costs should not exceed 28% of monthly income, and all debt payments combined, mortgage plus car loan plus minimum credit card payments, should not exceed 36%. Lenders use it as a first screen and it is deliberately conservative; final approval also weighs credit score, down payment and job stability."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "compound-interest": {
      "name": "Compound Interest",
      "description": "Calculate compound interest on investments with regular contributions",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate compound interest growth over time",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the starting principal, the amount you invest at the beginning.",
          "Fill in the annual return, the investment horizon and the compounding frequency (yearly, half-yearly, quarterly or monthly).",
          "If you add money regularly, enter the recurring contribution to see the total including those deposits."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "The compound interest formula",
        "formula": "A = P × (1 + r/n)^(n·t)",
        "explanation": [
          "A is the final amount, P the principal, r the annual rate, n how many times interest compounds per year, and t the number of years. Compounding means interest earns interest: each period's interest joins the principal and earns a return in the next period, so the growth curve steepens over time. Simple interest, by contrast, is paid only on the original principal and never earns anything itself.",
          "More frequent compounding yields a larger total, but with diminishing returns: moving from yearly to half-yearly adds about 1.1%, and moving on to monthly adds roughly another 1.1%. What really moves the needle is time and rate. A handy shortcut is the rule of 72: years to double ≈ 72 ÷ annual return in percent, so 6% doubles in about 12 years and 9% in about 8."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "How compounding frequency changes the total (100,000 at 6% for 20 years)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Frequency",
          "Times per year",
          "Total after 20 years",
          "Extra vs yearly"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Yearly",
            "1",
            "≈ 320,714",
            "—"
          ],
          [
            "Half-yearly",
            "2",
            "≈ 324,340",
            "≈ 3,626"
          ],
          [
            "Quarterly",
            "4",
            "≈ 326,204",
            "≈ 5,490"
          ],
          [
            "Monthly",
            "12",
            "≈ 327,758",
            "≈ 7,044"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between compound and simple interest?",
            "a": "Simple interest is paid only on the principal, so it never earns anything itself. Compound interest adds each period's interest to the principal so it earns a return too. On 100,000 at 6% for 20 years, simple interest totals 220,000 while yearly compounding reaches about 320,700, a gap of more than 100,000 that widens the longer you wait."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the rule of 72?",
            "a": "A quick way to estimate doubling time: years to double ≈ 72 ÷ annual return in percent. At 6% money doubles in about 12 years, at 9% in about 8, at 3% in about 24. It is most accurate between 6% and 10% and drifts at much higher rates."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the compounding frequency matter much?",
            "a": "It matters, but less than people expect. On 100,000 at 6% over 20 years, yearly compounding gives about 320,700 and monthly about 327,800, only about 2.2% more. Investing five years longer or earning one extra percentage point moves the result far more, so focus on time and rate rather than frequency."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is the annualised return what I actually receive?",
            "a": "Usually not. The annualised return is a nominal growth rate; your net result is reduced by management fees, entry and exit charges and tax. If you add or withdraw money along the way, your personal return differs from the headline rate and is better measured with an internal rate of return (IRR)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "loan": {
      "name": "Loan",
      "description": "Calculate monthly payments for personal, auto, or any loan type",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate loan payments and amortization",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the loan principal, meaning the amount you actually receive, excluding upfront fees.",
          "Fill in the annual interest rate and the loan term. Add a down payment or residual value if the fields apply.",
          "Calculate to see the monthly payment, total interest and total repayment, then adjust the inputs to compare options."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How amortised loan payments are calculated",
        "formula": "Payment = P × r × (1 + r)ⁿ ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]",
        "explanation": [
          "P is the principal, r the monthly rate (annual rate ÷ 12) and n the total number of payments. This is the standard amortising loan: interest for the month is charged on the outstanding balance, the rest of the fixed payment goes to principal, and the balance shrinks so next month's interest is lower.",
          "With this method the payment stays constant, which makes budgeting easy, but early payments are mostly interest. The alternative is a straight-line or equal-principal loan, where you repay a fixed slice of principal plus interest on the remaining balance: the first payment is the largest, each one after is smaller, and total interest is lower. On a 1,000,000 loan over 30 years at 4%, the equal-principal route saves roughly 117,000 in interest but starts about 1,300 per month higher. The right choice depends on your cash flow, not just the interest total."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Amortised vs equal-principal (1,000,000 over 30 years at 4.0%)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Method",
          "First payment",
          "Last payment",
          "Total interest",
          "Total repaid"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Amortised",
            "≈ 4,774",
            "≈ 4,774",
            "≈ 719,000",
            "≈ 1,719,000"
          ],
          [
            "Equal principal",
            "≈ 6,111",
            "≈ 2,790",
            "≈ 602,000",
            "≈ 1,602,000"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Which is better, amortised or equal-principal?",
            "a": "Equal-principal costs less interest but demands noticeably more in the early months. If cash flow is tight now or you expect income to grow, amortised fits better. If your income is stable and you want to minimise interest, equal-principal wins. Neither is universally better; it depends on your cash flow."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is it worth repaying early?",
            "a": "Compare your loan rate with what you could safely earn elsewhere. If the loan rate is higher, prepaying locks in a risk-free return at that rate. If you have better uses for the money or need an emergency buffer, do not rush. Also check whether your lender charges an early repayment fee or limits how often you can prepay."
          },
          {
            "q": "How is each payment split between principal and interest?",
            "a": "In an amortising loan, early payments are mostly interest and later ones mostly principal. On a 1,000,000 loan at 4% over 30 years, the first payment is roughly 3,333 interest and only about 1,441 principal; the two only even out around year 10."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I convert an annual rate to a monthly rate?",
            "a": "Dividing by 12 gives the nominal monthly rate, which is what most lenders quote. The exact effective monthly rate is (1 + annual)^(1/12) − 1. The difference is tiny, about 0.006 percentage points at 4%, so annual ÷ 12 is fine for everyday estimates."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "retirement": {
      "name": "Retirement",
      "description": "Plan your retirement with savings projections and income planning",
      "metaDescription": "Plan your retirement savings and income",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your current age and planned retirement age to set the accumulation period.",
          "Fill in existing savings, the monthly amount you can contribute and the expected annual return.",
          "See the projected total at retirement and the safe monthly withdrawal suggested by the 4% rule."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How retirement savings are projected",
        "formula": "Future value = current savings × (1+r)ⁿ + monthly × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]",
        "explanation": [
          "The formula has two parts: existing savings compounding to retirement, and regular contributions accumulating through the annuity formula. Here r is the monthly rate, the annual return divided by 12, and n is the number of months to retirement. It assumes a constant return, monthly compounding and contributions at period end, so treat the output as a scenario rather than a forecast, since real markets fluctuate.",
          "A useful rule of thumb is the 4% rule: withdraw 4% of the portfolio in the first year of retirement, then adjust the amount for inflation each year, and the money is unlikely to run out over a 30-year retirement. It comes from backtests of US stock and bond history and implies saving 25 times your annual spending. If you retire early or expect a longer retirement, lower the safe withdrawal rate to 3.0% to 3.5%. Results are extremely sensitive to the return assumption, so run optimistic, neutral and pessimistic cases."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Effect of monthly contributions (30 years at 6% a year)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Monthly",
          "Total contributed",
          "Value at retirement",
          "Investment growth"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "500",
            "180,000",
            "≈ 502,000",
            "≈ 322,000"
          ],
          [
            "1,000",
            "360,000",
            "≈ 1,004,000",
            "≈ 644,000"
          ],
          [
            "2,000",
            "720,000",
            "≈ 2,008,000",
            "≈ 1,288,000"
          ],
          [
            "3,000",
            "1,080,000",
            "≈ 3,012,000",
            "≈ 1,932,000"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Does the 4% rule still hold?",
            "a": "It is a useful starting point, not a law. The rule was backtested on US history, so if future returns are lower, your retirement lasts more than 30 years, or your local markets behave differently, the safe rate should come down. Recent research often suggests 3.0% to 3.5%, or a dynamic approach of withdrawing less in years when markets fall sharply."
          },
          {
            "q": "What share of income should I save?",
            "a": "A common target is 10% to 15% of pre-tax income, including any employer match. What matters more is your replacement rate: most people need 70% to 80% of pre-retirement income to maintain their standard of living. Starting at 30 usually requires 15% to 20% to catch up, while starting before 25 may be fine at around 10%."
          },
          {
            "q": "How should I handle inflation?",
            "a": "The calculator produces nominal amounts with inflation not deducted. To see real purchasing power, subtract expected inflation from your expected return before calculating, for instance using 4% real if you expect 7% nominal and 3% inflation. At 3% inflation, 1,000,000 in 30 years buys what about 410,000 buys today, so ignoring inflation badly overstates your position."
          },
          {
            "q": "How much does starting 10 years later cost?",
            "a": "A great deal. Investing 1,000 a month at 6% from age 25 to 60 produces roughly 1,430,000, while starting at 35 yields only about 700,000, less than half. You lose ten years of compounding, and compounding does its heaviest work late in the period. Starting earlier is usually more powerful than contributing more."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "tip": {
      "name": "Tip",
      "description": "Calculate tip amount and split bills between multiple people",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate tips and split bills easily",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick the bill currency and the common local tip rate (e.g. restaurant 10%–15%, delivery 0%–10%).",
          "Enter the bill amount, or enter the number of people so the tool splits the bill before tipping.",
          "See the tip due, the total with tip, and each person's share when split by headcount."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How to calculate a tip? Logic and common rates",
        "formula": "tip = bill × tip rate; total = bill + tip",
        "explanation": [
          "A tip is an extra gratuity for service staff, usually a percentage of the bill. Rates vary widely by country and setting: North American restaurants commonly use 15%–20%, while Japan and South Korea generally don't expect tips.",
          "The sensible approach is to fix the local custom rate first, then multiply by the pre- or post-tax amount. For group dining, split the bill per person first, then tip each share, so nobody overpays by averaging.",
          "This tool shows both the total-based and per-person results, handy for travel, team events, and dinners—you can settle who owes what in minutes."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Should I tip on pre-tax or post-tax?",
            "a": "Post-tax is more common in most regions, but some use the pre-tax subtotal. When unsure, base it on the subtotal shown and confirm with the establishment before paying."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I split a tip fairly among a group?",
            "a": "Fairest is each person tips on their own spend plus a share of shared items; simply dividing the total tip by headcount is easier but makes big spenders subsidize the rest."
          },
          {
            "q": "Where is tipping actually not expected?",
            "a": "Japan, South Korea, New Zealand and Australia often include service in the bill, so extra tipping is uncommon; some European countries add a service charge, removing the need to tip separately."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "savings-goal": {
      "name": "Savings Goal",
      "description": "Calculate how long to reach your savings goal with regular contributions",
      "metaDescription": "Plan your savings and reach your financial goals",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the target amount, such as a deposit, study fund or emergency reserve, plus what you already have saved.",
          "Fill in the time until the target, in months or years, and the expected annual return.",
          "See the monthly amount required, and how it changes if you adjust the deadline."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How much you need to save each month",
        "formula": "Monthly = (target − present × (1+r)ⁿ) × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]",
        "explanation": [
          "This is the annuity formula rearranged. First work out what your existing savings will grow to, present value times (1+r)ⁿ, subtract that from the target to get the shortfall, then solve for the monthly deposit that closes the gap over n months. Here r is the monthly rate and n the number of periods. If existing savings already exceed the target, the result is zero or negative, meaning no further saving is needed.",
          "The formula exposes three levers: extend the horizon, raise the return, or lower the target. Extending time is by far the strongest, because compounding accelerates later on. For a 300,000 target at 4% a year, reaching it in 10 years needs about 2,036 a month, while 20 years needs only about 819. Double the horizon and the monthly amount falls by more than half, which is the mathematical case for starting early."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Monthly saving needed for a 300,000 goal (at 4% a year)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Horizon",
          "Monthly",
          "Total contributed",
          "Growth"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "5 years",
            "≈ 4,527",
            "≈ 271,600",
            "≈ 28,400"
          ],
          [
            "10 years",
            "≈ 2,036",
            "≈ 244,300",
            "≈ 55,700"
          ],
          [
            "15 years",
            "≈ 1,220",
            "≈ 219,600",
            "≈ 80,400"
          ],
          [
            "20 years",
            "≈ 819",
            "≈ 196,600",
            "≈ 103,400"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What return should I assume?",
            "a": "It depends on the purpose and the horizon. Money needed within three years, such as a house deposit, should be assumed to earn little or nothing and sit in savings or a money market fund. Money you can leave for five years or more and can bear some volatility can be modelled at 4% to 6%. Do not inflate the return to make the numbers look comfortable, because underestimating the required contribution means missing the goal."
          },
          {
            "q": "How large should my emergency fund be?",
            "a": "Usually three to six months of living expenses, rising to nine to twelve if your income is irregular or your family depends on you. What matters most is liquidity and safety, so keep it in instant-access savings or a money market fund rather than chasing yield. Fill this bucket before saving for other goals."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should the target account for inflation?",
            "a": "Yes. A goal ten years out needs the purchasing power of 300,000 at that time, not today's 300,000. At 3% inflation, 300,000 in ten years buys what about 223,000 buys today, so the nominal target should be roughly 403,000. Alternatively, run the calculation with a real return, nominal minus inflation, which gives the same answer."
          },
          {
            "q": "What if I cannot save that much?",
            "a": "Three variables can move: extend the deadline, lower the target, or seek a higher return while accepting more risk. It also helps to review spending and find what can be cut. The 50/30/20 rule is a useful check, with 50% on needs, 30% on wants and 20% on saving and debt repayment. If you cannot reach 20%, trimming fixed costs such as subscriptions, insurance and commuting usually beats cutting small daily pleasures."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "inflation": {
      "name": "Inflation",
      "description": "Calculate the impact of inflation on purchasing power over time",
      "metaDescription": "Understand inflation's impact on your money",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter an amount together with the start and end year.",
          "Fill in the average annual inflation rate for that period, using a historical average or your own assumption.",
          "Read the inflation-adjusted value and how much purchasing power has been lost."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How inflation erodes purchasing power",
        "formula": "Real value = nominal amount ÷ (1 + inflation rate)ⁿ",
        "explanation": [
          "Inflation means the same sum of money buys progressively less. The conversion discounts the nominal amount back by the inflation rate: one unit n years from now is worth 1 ÷ (1 + i)ⁿ in today's terms. To go the other way and ask what today's 100 becomes in nominal terms, use 100 × (1 + i)ⁿ. Both directions use the same compounding formula, one multiplying and one dividing.",
          "The key insight is that the erosion compounds. Inflation of 3% sounds mild, yet purchasing power halves in about 24 years, since 1.03 to the power of 24 is roughly 2. At 5%, it halves in about 14 years. That is why long-term saving must be judged on real return, nominal return minus inflation: with a 2% deposit rate and 3% inflation, your money loses roughly 1% a year in real terms, and the longer you hold it, the more it costs you."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Purchasing power of 100 under different inflation rates",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Horizon",
          "2% inflation",
          "3% inflation",
          "5% inflation"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "After 10 years",
            "≈ 82.0",
            "≈ 74.4",
            "≈ 61.4"
          ],
          [
            "After 20 years",
            "≈ 67.3",
            "≈ 55.4",
            "≈ 37.7"
          ],
          [
            "After 30 years",
            "≈ 55.2",
            "≈ 41.2",
            "≈ 23.1"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does official CPI differ from the inflation I feel?",
            "a": "CPI is a weighted average across a basket of goods and services meant to represent a typical household. Your own spending mix differs, so if your money goes to rent, education or healthcare and those rise faster than average, your personal inflation will exceed headline CPI. CPI also adjusts for quality; a phone that costs more but does far more is not counted as pure inflation."
          },
          {
            "q": "What inflation rate should I assume long term?",
            "a": "For most advanced economies the long-run average sits between 2% and 3%, which is also the target band many central banks aim for. For retirement or education planning, use 3% as the neutral case and run a pessimistic 4% to 5% scenario as well. Inflation is among the least predictable inputs in long-range planning, so it is safer to overestimate than to underestimate."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is deflation, and does it happen?",
            "a": "Deflation is a broad, sustained fall in prices, meaning negative inflation. It sounds welcome but is dangerous: if people expect lower prices they delay purchases, firms cut prices and jobs, demand falls further, and the cycle feeds itself. Japan struggled with this from the 1990s onward, which is why central banks target modest inflation around 2%."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I calculate a real return?",
            "a": "Precisely, real return is (1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1, so 6% nominal with 3% inflation gives about 2.91% real. For quick work, subtracting inflation from the nominal rate is close enough, here 3%. What matters for judging whether an investment truly grows your wealth is always the real return, not the headline nominal figure."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "roi": {
      "name": "ROI",
      "description": "Calculate return on investment and annualized returns",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your investment returns",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the cost of the investment, the purchase price plus every fee and tax.",
          "Enter the amount returned, whether sale proceeds or current market value, net of selling costs.",
          "Read the return on investment, the net gain, and the annualised rate if you supply a holding period."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How return on investment is calculated",
        "formula": "ROI = (gain − cost) ÷ cost × 100%",
        "explanation": [
          "Return on investment measures efficiency: net gain divided by cost. It is intuitive and universal, letting you compare property, shares, equipment or an advertising campaign, provided you use the same conventions throughout. Its big weakness is that it ignores time. An investment that makes 30% over three years and one that makes 30% over ten years have identical ROI, yet they are nowhere near equivalent.",
          "So when horizons differ, use the annualised rate, also called CAGR: (ending value ÷ beginning value)^(1/years) − 1. In the example above, 30% over three years annualises to about 9.1%, while 30% over ten years is only about 2.7%. Another common omission is the cost basis: shares carry commissions and taxes, property carries transfer tax, agency fees, renovation and ongoing service charges, and leaving these out materially overstates the true return."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "ROI versus annualised return (CAGR)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Project",
          "Total ROI",
          "Holding period",
          "Annualised"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "A: short-term trade",
            "+30%",
            "3 years",
            "≈ 9.1%"
          ],
          [
            "B: long hold",
            "+30%",
            "10 years",
            "≈ 2.7%"
          ],
          [
            "C: property",
            "+45%",
            "5 years",
            "≈ 7.7%"
          ],
          [
            "D: index fund",
            "+80%",
            "10 years",
            "≈ 6.1%"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What counts as a good ROI?",
            "a": "There is no absolute number; it depends on the benchmark and the horizon. Long-term bank deposits yield roughly 1% to 2% a year, while major equity indices have historically returned about 7% to 10% nominal. A multi-year project annualising above 8% is therefore usually decent. Always weigh risk too, since high ROI often comes with volatility or leverage, so look at risk-adjusted measures such as the Sharpe ratio."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between ROI and IRR?",
            "a": "ROI looks only at the final total return, which suits a single investment with no intermediate cash flows. IRR accounts for the timing of every cash flow, so it fits staged contributions or interim income such as regular investing, rental income or corporate projects. Whenever cash flows are complex, IRR is the more accurate measure."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is my realised return below the headline ROI?",
            "a": "Three usual culprits: missed costs such as fees, taxes and currency losses; inflation, since nominal gains must be adjusted to show real purchasing power; and opportunity cost, what else that money could have earned. With leverage you must also deduct interest, which magnifies gains and losses alike."
          },
          {
            "q": "How is ROI calculated on a loss?",
            "a": "The formula is unchanged and the result is negative. Cost 10,000 and proceeds 7,000 gives ROI = (7,000 − 10,000) ÷ 10,000 = −30%. Note the asymmetry: after a 30% loss you need roughly a 42.9% gain to return to your starting point, since 1 ÷ 0.7 − 1 ≈ 0.429. The deeper the drawdown, the harder the recovery, which is why limiting losses matters more than chasing gains."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "break-even": {
      "name": "Break-Even",
      "description": "Calculate break-even point for your business",
      "metaDescription": "Find your business break-even point",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter fixed costs (rent, salaries—costs that don't vary with volume).",
          "Enter unit variable cost and unit price, or per-unit profit.",
          "See the break-even quantity, the matching revenue, and a safety-margin hint."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Break-even: fixed vs variable costs",
        "formula": "break-even qty = fixed cost ÷ (price − unit variable cost)",
        "explanation": [
          "The break-even point is the 'no profit, no loss' volume where total fixed costs are covered by per-unit contribution margin (price − variable cost).",
          "Higher fixed costs or lower per-unit profit raise the break-even point and the risk. Discounting boosts volume but raises the point, needing more sales to recover.",
          "This tool answers 'how many must I sell to not lose money'—useful before opening a shop, launching a product, or taking a project."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Fixed vs variable cost?",
            "a": "Fixed stays flat with output (rent, salary, subscriptions); variable moves with volume (materials, packaging, per-order shipping). Clear split gives accurate margin."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does discounting raise or lower break-even?",
            "a": "Usually raises it. Lower price → lower per-unit profit → more units needed to cover fixed costs."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is safety margin?",
            "a": "The amount actual sales exceed break-even. Bigger margin means more cushion against falling sales without loss."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "profit-margin": {
      "name": "Profit Margin",
      "description": "Calculate gross, operating, and net profit margins",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate business profit margins",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Choose 'cost-plus' or 'price-backward' mode.",
          "Enter cost and target margin (or enter price to solve for cost/profit).",
          "See profit, gross margin, and the minimum price to hit your target."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How margin works: cost vs price",
        "formula": "margin = (price − cost) ÷ price; price = cost ÷ (1 − margin)",
        "explanation": [
          "Margin is based on price, markup on cost—easy to confuse. A 50% 'markup' is NOT a 50% 'margin': markup puts half the cost as profit, while margin puts half the price as profit.",
          "To price, use 'cost ÷ (1 − target margin)'. E.g. cost 80, target margin 40% → price = 80 ÷ 0.6 ≈ 133.",
          "Useful for e-commerce, restaurants, and freelancers to cover costs and lock in real profit instead of 'selling but not earning'."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Is margin the same as markup?",
            "a": "No. Margin = profit÷price, markup = profit÷cost. At the same number, markup implies a lower margin."
          },
          {
            "q": "What price for 40% profit?",
            "a": "price = cost ÷ (1 − 40%). At cost 100, price ≈ 167, profit ≈ 67, margin exactly 40%."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is real profit lower than calculated?",
            "a": "Hidden costs like platform fees, shipping, refunds, and taxes are often missed. Fold them into cost before pricing back."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "discount": {
      "name": "Discount",
      "description": "Calculate discounts, sale prices, and savings",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate discounts and sale prices",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the original price and discount percent (e.g. 20%), or enter the sale price to back-calculate the discount.",
          "The tool instantly shows the saving, the final price, and the equivalent 'fold' discount.",
          "For stacked coupons or thresholds, chain two steps to get the final price."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How discounts work: logic and formula",
        "formula": "final = original × (1 − discount%); saving = original − final",
        "explanation": [
          "A discount is proportional subtraction: 20% off means multiplying by 0.8, same as 'pay 80%'. Don't be misled by wording—'X% off' and 'pay (100−X)%' are identical in amount.",
          "When stacking, 'discount then threshold' differs from 'threshold then discount'. Usually discounting first then checking a spend threshold favors the buyer, but platform rules vary.",
          "Converting to 'fold' is intuitive: 20% off ≈ 0.8× (8 折), 35% off ≈ 0.65×. This tool shows the net price and discount strength for easy price comparison."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Is 20% off the same as paying 80%?",
            "a": "Yes, exactly—both multiply the price by 0.8. The wording differs but the final amount is identical."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which comes first when stacking coupons and thresholds?",
            "a": "Order changes the result. Most stores discount first then test the threshold; if the threshold is on the original price, threshold-first is cheaper. Read the rules."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I gauge discount strength quickly?",
            "a": "Map percent off to fold: 30% off ≈ 0.7× (7 折), 50% off = 0.5×. Lower net price means stronger discount, but also check tax and category limits."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "currency": {
      "name": "Currency Converter",
      "description": "Convert between major world currencies",
      "metaDescription": "Convert currencies with live exchange rates",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the amount you want to convert and pick the source and target currencies.",
          "Check the rate source and the timestamp shown on the page. The default is the latest available mid-market rate.",
          "Read the converted amount. Remember that banks and payment providers add a margin, so the rate you actually get will be worse than this reference."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How exchange rates are calculated",
        "formula": "Target amount = amount × (target rate ÷ source rate)",
        "explanation": [
          "Everything rests on the exchange rate, the price of one currency expressed in another. This tool uses a USD-based cross rate: the amount is first converted into US dollars using the source currency rate, then out again using the target currency rate. The advantage is that any pair can be converted without maintaining a separate quote for every combination.",
          "In practice you meet several different prices. The mid-market rate is the average of buy and sell and is the reference used here. The telegraphic transfer rate applies to account-to-account transfers and carries a small margin. The cash rate applies to physical banknotes and carries the largest margin, because handling, storage and counterfeit risk all have to be paid for. Real transactions usually land 0.5% to 3% away from the mid-market rate, and airport or hotel bureaux can be 5% or more worse."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Mid-market versus what you actually get (converting 10,000 units)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Channel",
          "Rate type",
          "Typical margin",
          "What you receive"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Reference mid-market",
            "Mid-market",
            "0%",
            "Full reference value"
          ],
          [
            "Bank wire transfer",
            "TT selling rate",
            "≈ 0.3% – 0.8%",
            "Slightly less than reference"
          ],
          [
            "Bank counter cash",
            "Cash selling rate",
            "≈ 1% – 2%",
            "Less than reference"
          ],
          [
            "Airport / hotel bureau",
            "Cash rate",
            "≈ 3% – 8%",
            "Clearly less than reference"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How often is the rate updated?",
            "a": "The foreign exchange market trades around the clock on business days, and the reference rate used here is typically refreshed once a day. For a price you can trade on, use the live quote from your bank or payment provider."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is my bank's rate different from the one shown here?",
            "a": "Different price conventions. This page shows the mid-market rate, the midpoint between buy and sell. Your bank quotes a buying or selling rate that includes its operating cost and margin, the spread. That gap is normal, not a data error."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between mid-market, transfer and cash rates?",
            "a": "Mid-market is the buy/sell midpoint, used for reference and accounting. The transfer rate applies to electronic account-to-account moves, involves no physical cash and has the smallest spread. The cash rate applies to banknotes and is the widest, because transport, storage and counting must be paid for. For the same currency, the cash buying rate is usually noticeably below the transfer buying rate."
          },
          {
            "q": "What rate applies when I use my card abroad?",
            "a": "Usually the card network rate on the settlement date, plus a currency conversion fee of about 1% to 1.5%, and sometimes an extra cross-border fee from your bank. If the terminal offers to charge you in your home currency, that is dynamic currency conversion, and its rate is normally worse. Choose the local currency instead."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "auto-loan": {
      "name": "Auto Loan",
      "description": "Calculate car loan payments, interest, and total cost",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your auto loan monthly payments and total cost",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter car price, down payment (percent or amount), term, and annual rate.",
          "Pick equal-installment (flat monthly) or equal-principal (higher early, lower later).",
          "See monthly payment, total interest, total repaid, and early-payoff savings."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Auto loan payment: equal installment vs equal principal",
        "formula": "payment ≈ P×r×(1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1)",
        "explanation": [
          "Auto loans usually use equal installment: fixed monthly payment, with interest dominating early and principal later. Equal principal keeps principal flat and payments decline.",
          "Rate and term hugely affect total interest. On a 200k loan, 4%→8% can double the interest; shortening by a year also saves a lot.",
          "Compare down-payment, term, and rate combos to pick the plan with both manageable payment and lower total interest."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Which is better, equal installment or principal?",
            "a": "Equal principal pays less total interest but higher early payments. Choose installment if cash-flow tight; principal if you can afford early and want savings."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is 0% financing really free?",
            "a": "Often the rate is baked into a higher car price or fees, and 0% is short-term. Compare '0% price' vs 'normal loan price' for true cost."
          },
          {
            "q": "How much does early payoff save?",
            "a": "You pay principal early, so later interest stops. Savings depend on remaining principal and term—earlier pays off more (watch prepayment penalties)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "personal-loan": {
      "name": "Personal Loan",
      "description": "Calculate personal loan payments with fees and APR",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate personal loan payments including origination fees",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter loan amount, APR, and term (months or years).",
          "Pick repayment: equal installment is most common.",
          "See monthly payment, total interest, total repaid, and compare terms."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Personal loan payment: APR and term",
        "formula": "payment ≈ P×r×(1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1)",
        "explanation": [
          "Personal loans usually use equal installment with a fixed monthly payment. The key variables are APR (cost of money) and term (monthly pressure).",
          "On 100k, APR 6%→18% can multiply total interest several times. Longer term lowers the monthly but raises total interest—don't pick long just because it's easy.",
          "This tool puts 'payment comfort' and 'total cost' side by side so low monthly payments don't hide extra interest."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "APR to monthly rate?",
            "a": "monthly = APR ÷ 12. 12% APR → 1% monthly. Some ads use 'monthly fee rate' not real APR, which is costlier after conversion."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is a longer term better?",
            "a": "Lower payment but worse—more total interest. Choose long only if you need lower payments; otherwise prefer short to save."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the payout less than I borrowed?",
            "a": "Upfront fees, guarantee fees, or pre-deducted interest. Look at APR and total repaid, not just the monthly."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "student-loan": {
      "name": "Student Loan",
      "description": "Calculate student loan payments with grace period options",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate student loan payments and repayment schedules",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your total loan balance, the fixed interest rate, and the repayment term in years.",
          "Choose a plan type if your lender offers standard, extended, or income-driven options.",
          "Read your estimated monthly payment and total interest paid over the life of the loan."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How student loan interest works",
        "formula": "M = P * r(1+r)^n / ((1+r)^n - 1), where r = monthly rate, n = months",
        "explanation": [
          "Student loans accrue interest daily on the unpaid principal using the simple daily interest method: daily interest = balance x (annual rate / 365).",
          "Paying more than the minimum or making extra payments early reduces the principal faster, which cuts total interest because interest is always calculated on the remaining balance."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Should I pay off student loans early?",
            "a": "If the rate is high and you have no higher-interest debt, extra payments save interest. Keep an emergency fund and any employer match first."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is an income-driven plan?",
            "a": "It caps your monthly payment at a percentage of discretionary income and may forgive the balance after 20 to 25 years, though forgiven amounts can be taxable."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are payments tax deductible?",
            "a": "In some countries interest is deductible up to a limit; check your local rules, as treatment varies by jurisdiction."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "home-equity": {
      "name": "Home Equity",
      "description": "Calculate home equity loan or HELOC payments",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate home equity loan and HELOC payments",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your home's current market value and the total balances of any mortgages or liens.",
          "Pick the loan-to-value limit your lender allows, often 80 to 85 percent.",
          "Read the usable equity and the maximum home-equity loan or line of credit available."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What home equity means",
        "formula": "Equity = Home value - Outstanding mortgage; Usable = Equity x LTV limit",
        "explanation": [
          "Equity is the portion of the home you own outright. It grows as you repay the mortgage and as the property's market value rises.",
          "Lenders usually let you borrow only a fraction of equity to keep a safety buffer, and they require an appraisal to confirm the current value."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between a HELOC and a home-equity loan?",
            "a": "A HELOC is a revolving line you draw from as needed; a home-equity loan is a lump sum with fixed payments."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I lose my home?",
            "a": "Yes. Both are secured by the property, so missed payments can lead to foreclosure. Borrow only what you can repay."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is the interest tax deductible?",
            "a": "Interest on money used for substantial home improvements may be deductible in some jurisdictions; consult a local tax professional."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "debt-payoff": {
      "name": "Debt Payoff",
      "description": "Calculate how long to pay off debt with extra payments",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate debt payoff time and savings from extra payments",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "List all debts: balance, rate, minimum payment.",
          "Pick a strategy: avalanche (highest rate first) or snowball (smallest balance first).",
          "See total payoff time, total interest, and the boost from paying extra."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Pay off debt fastest: avalanche vs snowball",
        "formula": "each min payment = balance×r×(1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1)",
        "explanation": [
          "Both methods concentrate extra cash on one debt: avalanche goes high-rate first (least total interest); snowball goes small-balance first (more motivation, easier to stick).",
          "The wider the rate gap, the more avalanche saves; if rates are close, results are similar—pick the one you'll keep doing.",
          "This tool simulates both strategies' time and interest so you choose a plan that saves money and is sustainable, avoiding minimum-payment compound trap."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Avalanche or snowball?",
            "a": "Math favors avalanche (high rate first) for least interest; snowball (small first) uses early 'one paid off' wins to keep going. Pick what you'll finish."
          },
          {
            "q": "Risk of minimum-only payments?",
            "a": "Minimums mostly cover interest, principal drops slowly, and credit-card compounding balloons balance for years with huge total interest."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does paying extra help?",
            "a": "A lot. Any extra hits principal, later interest stops, shortening the cycle and saving interest (check prepayment allowance)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "credit-card": {
      "name": "Credit Card",
      "description": "Calculate credit card payoff time and interest",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate credit card payoff time and total interest cost",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the current balance, the card's annual percentage rate and the amount you plan to pay each month.",
          "If you only pay the minimum, tick that option to see how many years it would take to clear the balance.",
          "Read the number of months, the total interest, and how much a fixed monthly payment would save you."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How credit card interest builds up",
        "formula": "Monthly interest = average daily balance × APR ÷ 365 × days in cycle",
        "explanation": [
          "Most cards use the average daily balance method. The issuer adds up each day's balance in the cycle, divides by the number of days, then applies the daily rate, which is the APR divided by 365, multiplied by the days in the cycle. Paying early in the cycle therefore cuts interest noticeably, while paying on the last day leaves the full cycle to accrue.",
          "The minimum payment trap is that it is usually only 1% to 3% of the balance plus interest and fees. On a balance of 20,000 at 18% APR paying 2% minimum, about 300 of the first 400 payment is interest and only 100 reduces the balance, which takes more than 30 years to clear and costs nearly three times the original balance in interest. Paying a fixed 1,000 a month instead clears it in about 24 months for roughly 4,000 in interest. The gap is dramatic."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Repayment comparison (balance 20,000 at 18% APR)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Approach",
          "Monthly payment",
          "Time to clear",
          "Total interest"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Minimum only (2%)",
            "from ≈ 400",
            "over 30 years",
            "≈ 58,000"
          ],
          [
            "Fixed 800",
            "800",
            "≈ 32 months",
            "≈ 5,600"
          ],
          [
            "Fixed 1,500",
            "1,500",
            "≈ 16 months",
            "≈ 2,800"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Does paying only the minimum hurt my credit score?",
            "a": "As long as you pay at least the minimum, there is no late marker and the account still reports as current. But carrying a high balance keeps your credit utilisation, the ratio of balance to limit, elevated, and utilisation is a major scoring factor at roughly 30% of the score. Staying above 30% utilisation drags it down."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is there interest again after I paid?",
            "a": "Residual interest. Balance kept accruing between your payment date and the statement date, and that interest lands on the next statement. To stop interest entirely, pay the full statement balance before the statement date and stop using the card for a full cycle, then confirm the next statement shows zero interest."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is an instalment plan better than paying the minimum?",
            "a": "Usually yes. Instalment plans lock in a lower rate, often 7% to 12% a year against the 18% revolving rate, with a fixed payment and a clear end date. Watch for arrangement fees, and check whether paying early still leaves remaining charges due, so compare the true annualised cost before signing up."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the fastest way out of card debt?",
            "a": "Two common strategies. The snowball clears the smallest balance first for psychological momentum, while the avalanche targets the highest rate first and saves the most interest mathematically. Either way, stop adding new spending, fix your monthly payment, and consider moving the highest-rate balance to a cheaper facility."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "down-payment": {
      "name": "Down Payment",
      "description": "Calculate how much to save for a down payment",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate monthly savings needed for your down payment goal",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the home price you are considering and your target down-payment percentage.",
          "Add your available savings to compare against the required amount.",
          "See the dollar down payment needed and whether you meet common 20 percent thresholds that avoid mortgage insurance."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Why the down payment matters",
        "formula": "Down payment = Home price x percentage; Loan = Home price - Down payment",
        "explanation": [
          "A larger down payment lowers the loan amount, reduces monthly payments, and can remove the need for private mortgage insurance.",
          "Smaller down payments are allowed by many programs but usually cost more over time through insurance and interest on a bigger loan."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Is 20 percent always required?",
            "a": "No. Many loans allow 3 to 5 percent down, but below 20 percent you typically pay mortgage insurance until you build enough equity."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I drain savings for a bigger down payment?",
            "a": "Keep a reserve for closing costs and emergencies; an empty emergency fund is riskier than a slightly smaller down payment."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do gifts count toward the down payment?",
            "a": "Lenders often accept gifted funds but require a signed gift letter stating the money need not be repaid."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "retirement-401k": {
      "name": "401(k)",
      "description": "Calculate retirement savings with employer matching",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate 401k retirement savings with employer match and projections",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your current age, planned retirement age, salary, and current 401(k) balance.",
          "Add your contribution rate and any employer match percentage.",
          "Read the projected balance at retirement and the estimated annual income it could provide."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How compounding builds a 401(k)",
        "formula": "Future value = P(1+r)^n + C x (((1+r)^n - 1) / r)",
        "explanation": [
          "A 401(k) grows tax-deferred, so returns reinvest without annual tax drag, and employer matching is effectively free money added to your balance.",
          "Starting earlier matters more than the amount: contributions in your 20s compound for decades, often outweighing larger late-career contributions."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How much should I contribute?",
            "a": "Aim to capture the full employer match first, then raise toward 10 to 15 percent of salary as you are able."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the employer match?",
            "a": "Many employers match a percentage of your contributions up to a limit; not contributing leaves that money on the table."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I withdraw early?",
            "a": "Early withdrawals usually trigger taxes and a penalty; some plans allow loans or hardship exceptions with restrictions."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "ira-calculator": {
      "name": "IRA",
      "description": "Compare Traditional vs Roth IRA retirement accounts",
      "metaDescription": "Compare Traditional and Roth IRA retirement accounts and tax benefits",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Select whether you want to compare Traditional IRA, Roth IRA, or both.",
          "Enter your current age, planned retirement age, annual contribution, and expected rate of return.",
          "Review the estimated after-tax balance and compare which account may leave you with more spendable money."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Traditional IRA vs Roth IRA",
        "formula": "Future value = contribution × (((1 + r)^n - 1) / r) × (1 - tax_rate_or_0)",
        "explanation": [
          "A Traditional IRA gives you a tax deduction today, but withdrawals in retirement are taxed as ordinary income.",
          "A Roth IRA uses after-tax dollars, so qualified withdrawals in retirement are usually tax-free.",
          "The better choice depends on whether your current tax rate is higher or lower than the rate you expect in retirement."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can I contribute to both a Traditional and Roth IRA?",
            "a": "Yes, but your combined annual contribution cannot exceed the IRS limit for your filing status and income."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are IRA withdrawals always penalty-free after age 59½?",
            "a": "Traditional withdrawals are taxed; Roth qualified withdrawals are tax-free. Non-qualified withdrawals may trigger taxes and penalties."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the calculator include required minimum distributions?",
            "a": "No; it is a simplified projection. Use detailed planning software or a tax advisor for RMD and complex scenarios."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "bond-calculator": {
      "name": "Bond",
      "description": "Calculate bond prices, yields, and returns",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate bond prices, current yield, and yield to maturity",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the bond face value, coupon rate, years to maturity, and current market price.",
          "Add the payment frequency and any call or tax assumptions if applicable.",
          "Review the yield to maturity, current yield, and estimated total return."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How bond yields work",
        "formula": "Approx YTM = (annual coupon + (face - price) / years) / ((face + price) / 2)",
        "explanation": [
          "The coupon rate tells you the annual interest payment as a percentage of face value.",
          "Yield to maturity reflects the total return if you hold the bond until it matures, including price changes and reinvested coupons.",
          "When market interest rates rise, existing bond prices usually fall, and vice versa."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between coupon and yield?",
            "a": "Coupon is the fixed interest payment; yield is the effective return based on the price you actually paid."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does the bond price move opposite to interest rates?",
            "a": "New bonds pay the market rate, so an older bond with a lower coupon must trade at a discount to compete."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is this calculator suitable for zero-coupon bonds?",
            "a": "It works best for coupon bonds; zero-coupon bonds need a different reinvestment assumption."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "annuity-calculator": {
      "name": "Annuity",
      "description": "Calculate annuity payments and retirement income",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate annuity payments and retirement income projections",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Choose the annuity type: ordinary annuity (payments at end of period) or annuity due (payments at beginning).",
          "Enter the periodic payment, annual interest rate, number of periods, and starting principal if any.",
          "Review the future value, present value, and total interest earned."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Ordinary annuity vs annuity due",
        "formula": "FV ordinary = PMT × (((1 + r)^n - 1) / r); FV due = FV ordinary × (1 + r)",
        "explanation": [
          "An ordinary annuity assumes each payment occurs at the end of a period, like a typical loan payment.",
          "An annuity due assumes payments at the start of each period, which earns one extra period of interest.",
          "The same payment stream is worth more in an annuity due because each cash flow is invested sooner."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is a real-world example of an annuity due?",
            "a": "Apartment rent and gym memberships are common examples because payment is due at the beginning of each month."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I use this for retirement income planning?",
            "a": "Yes; it gives a first-order estimate for structured payouts, though real products may include fees and riders."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the calculator handle inflation?",
            "a": "No; the rate entered is nominal. For real value, subtract expected inflation from the rate."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "body-fat": {
      "name": "Body Fat",
      "description": "Calculate body fat percentage using the US Navy method",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your body fat percentage using the US Navy method with measurements",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Select your sex and unit system. Sex directly changes the formula coefficients.",
          "Enter height, weight and age. If you know your neck and waist circumference, add them for a noticeably better estimate.",
          "Read the estimated body fat percentage, see which band it falls into, and compare it with the table below."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How body fat percentage is estimated",
        "formula": "Body fat (%) = fat mass ÷ body weight × 100",
        "explanation": [
          "Body fat percentage is the share of your total weight that is fat, and it describes body composition more directly than BMI. This tool uses the US Navy method, which derives body fat from height, neck and waist circumference. When circumference data is missing it falls back to the Deurenberg equation: body fat = 1.20 × BMI + 0.23 × age − 10.8 × sex − 5.4, where sex is 1 for male and 0 for female.",
          "Results from different methods can differ by 3 to 5 percentage points. Home scales use bioelectrical impedance, which reacts strongly to hydration, food and exercise. Underwater weighing and DXA scans are more accurate but impractical for daily use. Treat body fat percentage as a long-term trend: measure under consistent conditions, ideally in the morning before eating, and compare changes over several weeks rather than a single reading."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Body fat reference ranges (ACE)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Group",
          "Essential",
          "Athlete",
          "Fitness",
          "Average",
          "Obese"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Male",
            "2–5%",
            "6–13%",
            "14–17%",
            "18–24%",
            "≥ 25%"
          ],
          [
            "Female",
            "10–13%",
            "14–20%",
            "21–24%",
            "25–31%",
            "≥ 32%"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Which is more accurate, a body fat scale or this calculator?",
            "a": "Both are estimates with different error sources. Impedance scales react strongly to hydration, so readings swing after drinking or exercising. The circumference method here depends on how carefully you measure. Pick one method, keep conditions consistent, and watch the long-term trend."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does my body fat reading disagree with my BMI?",
            "a": "That is exactly the point of measuring body fat. BMI only relates weight to height and cannot tell muscle from fat, while body fat estimates the fat share directly. Muscular people are often labelled overweight by BMI despite healthy body fat, and in that case body fat is the better guide."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is lower body fat always better?",
            "a": "No. Fat supports hormone production, temperature regulation and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Staying below roughly 5% for men and 13% for women over long periods can disrupt hormones and immunity. The fitness or average bands are usually the sustainable target."
          },
          {
            "q": "When is the best time to measure?",
            "a": "Measure in the morning, fasted and after using the bathroom, and avoid measuring right after hard training: dehydration pushes impedance readings up and temporary swelling inflates circumference. Averaging three consecutive days is far more reliable than one reading."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "lean-body-mass": {
      "name": "Lean Body Mass",
      "description": "Calculate lean body mass using multiple formulas",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your lean body mass (fat-free mass) using multiple medical formulas",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your weight, body fat percentage (or use a method like skinfold), and your sex.",
          "Choose the estimation equation if several are offered.",
          "Read your lean mass, the part of your body that is not fat."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Lean body mass explained",
        "formula": "Lean mass = Total weight x (1 - body fat fraction)",
        "explanation": [
          "Lean body mass includes muscle, bone, water, and organs; only the fat portion is excluded, so it is higher than just muscle mass.",
          "It is useful for setting protein targets, dosing some medications, and tracking body-composition changes over time."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How is body fat measured?",
            "a": "Methods range from skinfold calipers and bioimpedance scales to DEXA scans, each with different accuracy."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does lean mass matter?",
            "a": "It reflects metabolically active tissue and helps tailor nutrition and training, especially as you age."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is higher lean mass always better?",
            "a": "More is generally favorable for metabolism and strength, but context and overall health matter more than the number alone."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "body-surface-area": {
      "name": "Body Surface Area",
      "description": "Calculate BSA using multiple medical formulas",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate body surface area for medical dosing using multiple formulas",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your height and weight.",
          "Pick a formula such as Du Bois, Mosteller, or Haycock if available.",
          "Read your BSA in square metres, used for medical dosing."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Why body surface area matters",
        "formula": "Mosteller: BSA = sqrt( (height cm x weight kg) / 3600 )",
        "explanation": [
          "BSA estimates the total skin area and is used to dose certain drugs, especially chemotherapy, because it scales better than weight alone.",
          "Different formulas give slightly different results; clinicians pick one consistently. Never adjust prescribed doses yourself."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why use BSA instead of weight?",
            "a": "Many physiological processes scale with surface area, so BSA gives more consistent dosing across body sizes."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which formula is best?",
            "a": "They agree closely for average adults; Mosteller is common for its simplicity, Du Bois is a long-standing standard."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I use BSA to change my medication?",
            "a": "Never. BSA guides prescribers; do not alter prescribed doses without your clinician."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "ideal-weight": {
      "name": "Ideal Weight",
      "description": "Calculate ideal body weight using multiple formulas",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your ideal body weight using Robinson, Miller, Devine, and Hamwi formulas",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Select sex and unit system, since the coefficients differ by sex.",
          "Enter your height; some formulas also ask for age or frame size.",
          "Compare the range produced by several formulas and note the systematic gaps between them."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "The different ideal body weight formulas",
        "formula": "Devine (men) = 50 + 0.9 × (height cm − 152.4); Hamwi (women) = 45.5 + 0.9 × (height cm − 152.4)",
        "explanation": [
          "Ideal body weight is not a single standard but a family of historical equations. The Devine formula, from 1974, was originally designed to estimate anaesthetic and drug dosages and was later borrowed for everyday use. Hamwi and Robinson came slightly earlier and give similar results. All share the shape of a base value plus 0.9 kg for every centimetre above 152.4 cm, which is five feet.",
          "Two caveats matter. First, these equations were built decades ago mainly on Western samples and ignore age, frame size and muscle mass, so they tend to run 5% to 10% low for Asian populations. Second, they serve different purposes: Devine for dosing, BMI ranges for health screening, body fat for composition. Treat ideal weight as a reference band, roughly plus or minus 10% around the formula results, rather than a number you must hit."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Ideal weight by formula (man 175 cm, woman 165 cm)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Formula",
          "Man 175 cm",
          "Woman 165 cm",
          "Note"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Devine (1974)",
            "≈ 70.4 kg",
            "≈ 56.7 kg",
            "Most used for drug dosing"
          ],
          [
            "Hamwi (1964)",
            "≈ 70.3 kg",
            "≈ 56.7 kg",
            "Close to Devine"
          ],
          [
            "Robinson (1983)",
            "≈ 68.0 kg",
            "≈ 56.9 kg",
            "Slightly lower for men"
          ],
          [
            "Miller (1983)",
            "≈ 69.4 kg",
            "≈ 56.8 kg",
            "Between the two"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Does ideal weight match a normal BMI?",
            "a": "They correspond roughly but not exactly. Devine's output for most heights converts to a BMI around 21 to 23, the lower-middle part of the normal band, while the normal BMI range spans 18.5 to 24.9. At a given height, therefore, normal BMI is a much wider target than ideal weight; they are simply different reference frames."
          },
          {
            "q": "How should athletes estimate ideal weight?",
            "a": "These formulas do not apply. Muscle is denser than fat, so strength athletes often exceed the predicted weight by 15% to 25% while carrying low body fat and healthy metabolic markers. They should look at body fat percentage and waist circumference instead. Equally, older adults who have lost muscle may hit the target weight while carrying too much fat."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do the formulas disagree?",
            "a": "They were fitted to different samples in different eras for different purposes. Devine and Hamwi differ by under 1%, while Robinson and Miller run 2 to 3 kg lower for men. That spread is far smaller than natural variation between individuals, so arguing over which to use matters less than viewing the results as a band."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is being below ideal weight always healthier?",
            "a": "No. A BMI under 18.5 is associated with reduced immunity, bone loss, menstrual disruption and malnutrition risk. Low weight often comes with low muscle mass, and muscle underpins metabolic health and independence later in life. The aim is moderate body fat with adequate muscle, not the smallest number on the scale."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "bmr-calculator": {
      "name": "BMR",
      "description": "Calculate Basal Metabolic Rate for calorie planning",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your Basal Metabolic Rate using Harris-Benedict and Mifflin-St Jeor formulas",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Select sex and unit system. Sex sets the constant term in the equation.",
          "Enter age, height and weight. Age affects basal metabolism more than most people expect.",
          "Read your daily basal calorie need, then apply an activity factor to estimate total daily energy expenditure."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How basal metabolic rate is calculated",
        "formula": "Mifflin-St Jeor: men BMR = 10W + 6.25H − 5A + 5, women BMR = 10W + 6.25H − 5A − 161",
        "explanation": [
          "Basal metabolic rate is the energy needed to keep your heart beating, lungs working and body temperature stable while awake, fasted and resting in a neutral environment. It usually accounts for 60% to 75% of total daily energy expenditure. This tool uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation by default, which is among the most accurate predictive formulas for the general population. W is weight in kilograms, H height in centimetres and A age in years.",
          "Other common options include the revised Harris-Benedict equation, which dates back to 1919 and tends to overestimate modern adults by 5% to 10%, and Katch-McArdle, which is the most accurate when you know your body fat percentage. Prediction error is typically within plus or minus 10%, and individual variation comes mostly from muscle mass, genetics and hormones. Treat BMR as the starting point for a food plan rather than a laboratory measurement; indirect calorimetry or doubly labelled water is needed for that."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Activity factors and total daily energy expenditure",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Activity level",
          "Factor",
          "Description",
          "Example (BMR 1,500)"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Sedentary",
            "1.2",
            "Desk job, little or no exercise",
            "1,800 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Lightly active",
            "1.375",
            "Light exercise 1–3 days a week",
            "2,063 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Moderately active",
            "1.55",
            "Moderate exercise 3–5 days a week",
            "2,325 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Very active",
            "1.725",
            "Hard exercise 6–7 days a week",
            "2,588 kcal"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?",
            "a": "BMR is the minimum energy needed to stay alive at complete rest. TDEE adds everyday movement, exercise and the cost of digesting food: TDEE = BMR × activity factor. Plan your intake from TDEE, not BMR, or you will end up eating far too little."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do different formulas give different numbers?",
            "a": "They were fitted to different populations in different eras. Mifflin-St Jeor was derived in 1990 and suits modern adults well. Harris-Benedict dates from 1919, revised in 1984, and overestimates by roughly 5% to 10%. Katch-McArdle needs body fat percentage but is the most accurate when you have it. Differences are usually within 100 to 200 kcal, so pick one and stay consistent."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does dieting slow down metabolism?",
            "a": "It does. A sustained large deficit triggers adaptive thermogenesis: the body trims non-essential movement and thyroid output, and basal metabolism can drop 10% to 20%, with part of the drop persisting after you eat normally again. Keep the deficit to 300 to 500 kcal per day and combine it with resistance training and adequate protein to limit the effect."
          },
          {
            "q": "How much should I eat to lose fat?",
            "a": "Work out your TDEE and subtract 300 to 500 kcal, which corresponds to roughly 0.3 to 0.5 kg per week. Do not eat below your BMR, and stay above about 1,200 kcal a day for women and 1,500 for men. Two or three resistance sessions a week plus 1.6 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of bodyweight will help you keep muscle."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "tdee-calculator": {
      "name": "TDEE",
      "description": "Calculate Total Daily Energy Expenditure",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your Total Daily Energy Expenditure based on activity level",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter sex, age, height and weight to establish your basal metabolic rate.",
          "Choose the activity level that best matches a typical week, from sedentary up to athlete.",
          "Read your total daily energy expenditure and the intake suggested for fat loss, maintenance or muscle gain."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How total daily energy expenditure is calculated",
        "formula": "TDEE = BMR (Mifflin-St Jeor) × activity factor (1.2 – 1.9)",
        "explanation": [
          "Total daily energy expenditure has four components: basal metabolism at 60% to 75%, the thermic effect of food at about 10% because digestion itself costs energy, non-exercise activity thermogenesis at roughly 15% through walking, housework and fidgeting, and exercise at anywhere from 0% to 30%. The calculator derives BMR with Mifflin-St Jeor and multiplies it by the activity factor you pick.",
          "The component most often underestimated is NEAT. Two people who train identically, one commuting an hour and standing at a desk, the other seated all day, can differ by 400 to 600 kcal daily, a bigger gap than a moderate training session. Choose the factor that matches a typical week rather than your most active day, and if the result disagrees with how your weight actually moves, adjust by 10% to 15% from there."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Activity factors (for a BMR of 1,600 kcal)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Level",
          "Factor",
          "Typical",
          "TDEE"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Sedentary",
            "1.2",
            "Desk job, little extra exercise",
            "1,920 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Light",
            "1.375",
            "Easy exercise 1–3 days a week",
            "2,200 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Moderate",
            "1.55",
            "Moderate exercise 3–5 days a week",
            "2,480 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "High",
            "1.725",
            "Hard exercise 6–7 days a week",
            "2,760 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Very high",
            "1.9",
            "Physical job plus twice-daily training",
            "3,040 kcal"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between TDEE and BMR?",
            "a": "BMR is the minimum needed to stay alive at complete rest, while TDEE adds everyday movement, exercise and the cost of digesting food. Plan your intake from TDEE, because that reflects the life you actually live. Using BMR as your target badly underestimates requirements and leads to under-eating, metabolic adaptation and muscle loss."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is my weight unchanged if I eat at my TDEE?",
            "a": "The usual cause is picking the wrong activity factor. Most people overestimate their exercise, and a desk worker selecting moderately active adds 300 to 500 kcal that never happens. Start with sedentary or light, log your food and weight for two weeks, then adjust to what actually occurs. Stable weight means intake equals TDEE, which is the most accurate calibration available."
          },
          {
            "q": "How large should a fat loss deficit be?",
            "a": "Aim for 300 to 500 kcal below TDEE per day, which works out to about 0.3 to 0.5 kg per week. A bigger deficit accelerates muscle loss and metabolic adaptation, making long-term progress harder. Do not eat below your BMR, and generally stay above 1,200 kcal for women and 1,500 for men. Two or three resistance sessions a week plus enough protein will preserve muscle."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are the calorie readings on fitness devices accurate?",
            "a": "Not really. Errors of 20% to 40% are normal and usually skew high, because bands and treadmills rely on heart rate or generic formulas that cannot capture individual variation or the way people move less afterwards. Treat the numbers as a rough guide and judge progress by the two-week trend in weight and measurements instead."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "calorie-calculator": {
      "name": "Calorie",
      "description": "Calculate daily calorie needs for weight goals",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate daily calorie needs and macros for your weight loss or gain goals",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter sex, age, height and weight to establish your basal metabolic rate.",
          "Pick your everyday activity level so the tool can estimate your total daily energy expenditure.",
          "Choose a goal, fat loss, maintenance or muscle gain, and read the suggested intake and macronutrient split."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How daily calorie needs are worked out",
        "formula": "Total daily energy expenditure = BMR × activity factor (1.2 – 1.725)",
        "explanation": [
          "Total expenditure has four components. Basal metabolism accounts for 60% to 75%. The thermic effect of food is about 10%, since digesting and absorbing nutrients costs energy. Non-exercise activity thermogenesis, walking, housework and fidgeting, adds roughly 15%. Exercise contributes anywhere from 0% to 30% and is the most variable. The calculator derives basal metabolism with Mifflin-St Jeor and multiplies it by the activity factor you choose.",
          "Fat loss comes down to a sustained deficit against that total. A reduction of 300 to 500 kcal a day corresponds to roughly 0.3 to 0.5 kg per week, a pace you can maintain while keeping muscle. Too large a deficit triggers metabolic adaptation, muscle loss and hunger that is hard to live with, which is why crash diets rebound. For muscle gain, a surplus of 200 to 400 kcal is enough; anything beyond that is stored as fat rather than built into tissue."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Calorie targets by goal (for a TDEE of 2,200 kcal)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Goal",
          "Daily intake",
          "Expected pace",
          "Protein target"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Faster fat loss",
            "1,700 kcal",
            "≈ −0.5 kg per week",
            "1.6 – 2.2 g per kg"
          ],
          [
            "Gentle fat loss",
            "1,900 kcal",
            "≈ −0.3 kg per week",
            "1.6 – 2.2 g per kg"
          ],
          [
            "Maintenance",
            "2,200 kcal",
            "Roughly stable",
            "1.2 – 1.6 g per kg"
          ],
          [
            "Muscle gain",
            "2,500 kcal",
            "≈ +0.2 kg per week",
            "1.6 – 2.2 g per kg"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why am I not losing weight despite following the plan?",
            "a": "Three causes are common. Underestimating intake, because cooking oil, drinks and snacks easily add 300 to 500 kcal. Overestimating activity, because a desk worker selecting moderately active inflates expenditure. And early fat loss being masked by water retention. Log your food honestly for one or two weeks and adjust from there; that beats switching calculators."
          },
          {
            "q": "How much protein during fat loss?",
            "a": "Aim for 1.6 to 2.2 grams per kilogram of bodyweight, roughly 25% to 35% of calories. Higher protein protects muscle in a deficit, increases satiety and has a larger thermic effect, since digesting protein burns about 20% to 30% of its calories. Take the upper end if you train with weights, the lower end if you are sedentary. Anyone with kidney disease should check with a doctor first."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I trust the calories burned from exercise?",
            "a": "Only loosely. Treadmills, bands and apps are typically off by 20% to 40%, usually high, and the body partly compensates by moving less for the rest of the day. Treat exercise figures as a guide and judge progress by the two-week trend in bodyweight and measurements."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does metabolism slow down as I lose weight?",
            "a": "Yes. Every kilogram lost shaves roughly 20 to 30 kcal off basal metabolism, and rapid large losses add a further adaptive drop of 10% to 20%. Recalculate your needs after every 5 to 10 kilograms, keep lifting, keep protein high, and consider short maintenance phases along the way."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "calories-burned": {
      "name": "Calories Burned",
      "description": "Calculate calories burned during various activities",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate calories burned during exercise and daily activities using MET values",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your body weight, since a heavier person burns more doing the same activity.",
          "Choose the activity and how long you did it, as MET values differ greatly between exercises.",
          "Read the estimated burn and use it as a reference for diet and training planning."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How exercise calories are estimated",
        "formula": "Calories = MET × weight (kg) × duration (hours)",
        "explanation": [
          "MET, or metabolic equivalent, expresses an activity's metabolic rate as a multiple of resting metabolism. Sitting quietly is 1 MET, brisk walking roughly 4, jogging 8 to 9, and skipping rope can reach 12. Multiply MET by bodyweight in kilograms and duration in hours to estimate energy cost. The values come from the Compendium of Physical Activities, the most widely used reference.",
          "Two caveats matter. First, MET values are population averages and ignore individual differences: two people jogging for 30 minutes can differ by 10% to 15% depending on movement efficiency, with beginners typically burning more. Second, the formula gives gross calories, including the energy you would have burned anyway at rest. To find the net cost of the exercise, subtract resting metabolism for the same period, about 1 MET × weight × hours. For a 70 kg person jogging 30 minutes, gross is roughly 315 kcal and net around 280."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "MET values for common activities (70 kg, 30 minutes)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Activity",
          "MET",
          "Gross",
          "Net"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Walking 4 km/h",
            "3.0",
            "≈ 105 kcal",
            "≈ 70 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Brisk walking 6 km/h",
            "5.0",
            "≈ 175 kcal",
            "≈ 140 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Jogging 8 km/h",
            "8.3",
            "≈ 290 kcal",
            "≈ 255 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Cycling, moderate",
            "7.5",
            "≈ 263 kcal",
            "≈ 228 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Swimming, freestyle",
            "8.3",
            "≈ 290 kcal",
            "≈ 255 kcal"
          ],
          [
            "Skipping, moderate",
            "11.8",
            "≈ 413 kcal",
            "≈ 378 kcal"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why does my watch show more than this?",
            "a": "Most wearables overestimate by 20% to 40%. They rely on heart rate and generic algorithms, and heart rate shifts with caffeine, temperature, sleep and mood, while the algorithm cannot see how efficient your movement is. Some devices also fold part of your resting metabolism into the activity figure. Treat the reading as a trend rather than an accurate ledger."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does metabolism stay elevated after exercise?",
            "a": "Yes, through excess post-exercise oxygen consumption, or EPOC. High-intensity intervals and resistance training produce the largest effect, raising metabolism by roughly 5% to 15% for a few hours, but the total is usually only a few dozen calories, far below the popular claim of burning fat for 24 hours. Steady low-intensity cardio produces a negligible EPOC."
          },
          {
            "q": "For fat loss, is exercise or diet more important?",
            "a": "Diet does most of the work. Thirty minutes of jogging nets about 250 kcal, which a slice of cake or a sugary drink cancels out. Exercise earns its place by preserving muscle, improving metabolic markers and helping you keep the weight off, rather than by creating a large deficit on its own. Combining both works best."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does training fasted burn more fat?",
            "a": "Fasted cardio does use a higher proportion of fat during the session, but total expenditure is not necessarily greater, and it can lower intensity and total training volume. Long-term studies show little difference in fat loss; total calorie balance remains decisive. If fasting makes you dizzy or weakens your performance, eating beforehand is the better choice."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "target-heart-rate": {
      "name": "Target Heart Rate",
      "description": "Calculate target heart rate zones for exercise",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your target heart rate zones for fat burning and cardio exercise",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your age, since maximal heart rate is driven mainly by age.",
          "Optionally enter your resting heart rate to use the Karvonen method for better precision.",
          "Read the target zones and compare them against a chest strap or watch while training."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How target heart rate is calculated",
        "formula": "Max heart rate = 220 − age; target = max heart rate × intensity %",
        "explanation": [
          "The most common estimate for maximal heart rate is 220 minus age, a rough fit to several studies from the 1970s. It is convenient, but the standard deviation is about plus or minus 10 to 12 beats per minute, meaning your true maximum may sit more than 10 beats above or below the estimate. The Tanaka equation, 208 − 0.7 × age, is generally a closer fit, particularly for middle-aged and older adults.",
          "If you know your resting heart rate, the Karvonen method personalises things further: target = (max − resting) × intensity% + resting. It works from heart rate reserve and so reflects differences in fitness. Whichever method you use, remember that heart rate responds to caffeine, sleep, temperature, mood and medication, so swings of 5 to 10 beats at the same effort on different days are normal. Judge intensity by heart rate and perceived exertion together."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Training zones (for age 40, max heart rate 180)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Zone",
          "% of max",
          "Target heart rate",
          "How it feels"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Warm-up",
            "50–60%",
            "90–108",
            "Easy, full conversation"
          ],
          [
            "Fat burn / aerobic",
            "60–70%",
            "108–126",
            "Breathing harder, short sentences"
          ],
          [
            "Aerobic endurance",
            "70–80%",
            "126–144",
            "Clearly breathless, single words"
          ],
          [
            "Anaerobic / intervals",
            "80–90%",
            "144–162",
            "Very hard, cannot talk"
          ],
          [
            "Maximal",
            "90–100%",
            "162–180",
            "All-out, unsustainable"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How accurate is 220 minus age?",
            "a": "Only roughly. Individual variation is large, with a standard deviation around 10 to 12 beats per minute, and the formula tends to overestimate for younger people and underestimate for older ones. The Tanaka equation, 208 − 0.7 × age, is usually closer. For everyday training, either is good enough to set zones."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the fat-burning zone, and does it burn more fat?",
            "a": "It refers to the intensity where the proportion of energy from fat is highest, roughly 60% to 70% of maximum. The key clarification: at low intensity the proportion from fat is high but total calories are low, while at high intensity the fat share falls but total burn and excess post-exercise oxygen consumption rise. Fat loss depends on your overall calorie balance, not on the fuel mix during the session."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why will my heart rate not rise, or not come down?",
            "a": "A blunted response usually reflects fatigue, poor sleep or dehydration, and beta blockers suppress heart rate markedly, so anyone taking them should use perceived effort instead. An elevated rate can come from heat, caffeine, dehydration or overtraining. If your resting heart rate sits more than 10% above normal for several days, that is a signal to rest."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does a low resting heart rate mean a healthy heart?",
            "a": "Usually yes. Endurance training raises stroke volume, so resting rates of 40 to 60 beats per minute reflect a more efficient heart. Typical adult resting rates sit between 60 and 100, and a persistently lower value without dizziness or fatigue is a good adaptation. If it comes with dizziness, weakness or fainting, get it checked."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "one-rep-max": {
      "name": "One Rep Max",
      "description": "Calculate your one-rep max from submaximal lifts",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your 1RM using various formulas from your submaximal lifts",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Choose the lift you want to estimate, such as bench press, squat or deadlift.",
          "Enter a weight and the number of clean reps you can do, ideally between 2 and 10.",
          "Read your estimated one-rep max and the training loads at each percentage."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How a one-rep max is estimated",
        "formula": "Epley: 1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)",
        "explanation": [
          "A one-rep max is the heaviest weight you can lift once with good form, and it anchors most strength programming. Testing it directly demands a long warm-up and carries injury risk, so in practice it is estimated from a set of multiple reps. The widely used Epley formula assumes each additional rep corresponds to roughly 3.33% less weight, which is one thirtieth.",
          "Other formulas such as Brzycki, Lombardi and O'Conner agree closely between 1 and 10 reps, usually within 2% to 5%, but accuracy collapses beyond about 10 reps because endurance, lactate tolerance and conditioning start to dominate rather than pure strength. Base your estimate on a set of 2 to 10 reps. Remember too that the result is a programming reference, not a competition number: your true max on a given day depends heavily on fatigue, technique and how thoroughly you warmed up."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Formula comparison (bench press 80 kg × 5)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Formula",
          "Estimated 1RM",
          "Note"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Epley",
            "≈ 93.3 kg",
            "Most common, good for 1–10 reps"
          ],
          [
            "Brzycki",
            "≈ 90.3 kg",
            "Slightly conservative"
          ],
          [
            "Lombardi",
            "≈ 92.8 kg",
            "Exponential relationship"
          ],
          [
            "O'Conner",
            "≈ 92.0 kg",
            "More stable at higher reps"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can I just attempt the estimated max?",
            "a": "Not recommended, especially if you are relatively new to training. Estimates can sit 5% to 10% away from your true max, so going straight for the number carries real risk. A safer route is to test 90% of the estimate, confirm the technique holds, then add 2.5 to 5 kg per attempt until form breaks, always with a spotter or safety bars."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does the estimate get worse with more reps?",
            "a": "Because the limiting factor changes. Sets of 1 to 5 reps reflect maximal strength, but beyond roughly 10 to 12 reps, muscular endurance, lactate tolerance and conditioning take over, and those are not a linear extension of pure strength. Every formula therefore states a valid range, usually 2 to 10 reps, and results distort badly outside it."
          },
          {
            "q": "How should I set training loads from a 1RM percentage?",
            "a": "A common structure is 85% to 100% for 1 to 5 reps for strength, 65% to 80% for 6 to 12 reps for hypertrophy, and 60% to 70% for 15 to 20 reps for endurance. Beginners do well starting at 60% to 70% while they groove technique, then raising intensity gradually. Exact percentages shift with training age and recovery capacity."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is a normal ratio between lifts?",
            "a": "Individual variation is large, but rough benchmarks for trained lifters are a squat around 1.5 times bodyweight, a bench around 1.0 times and a deadlift around 1.75 to 2.0 times. Female lifters typically reach about 60% to 70% of the numbers of a male of the same bodyweight, largely because of differences in muscle distribution and hormones. Treat these as context, not targets."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "pace-calculator": {
      "name": "Pace",
      "description": "Calculate running pace, speed, and race times",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your running pace, predict race times, and plan your training",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Choose: distance+time → pace, or pace+distance → finish time.",
          "Enter distance (m/km/mi) and time, or your target pace.",
          "See pace (min:sec per km), finish time, and split suggestions."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Running pace: pace vs time",
        "formula": "pace = time ÷ distance; time = pace × distance",
        "explanation": [
          "Pace is time per unit distance, shown as 'min:sec per km'. It's the yardstick of training intensity: at the same heart rate, slower pace means lower intensity.",
          "Deriving pace from distance+time tells if a run met its goal; pace+distance previews finish time for race or training planning.",
          "Switch metric/imperial to turn a goal like 'sub-30 5K' into a daily executable pace range."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Pace vs speed?",
            "a": "Speed is distance per time (12 km/h); pace is time per distance (5:00/km). Runners use pace because it maps to 'how hard each km feels'."
          },
          {
            "q": "How to plan training with pace?",
            "a": "Use easy/tempo/interval pace zones. After this tool gives target pace, fine-tune by heart rate or feel."
          },
          {
            "q": "Calculated time far from actual?",
            "a": "Terrain, wind, fatigue slow you. Pace is the ideal-flat reference; add margin for hills or headwind."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "macro-calculator": {
      "name": "Macro",
      "description": "Calculate daily macronutrient targets",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your optimal protein, carbs, and fat intake for your fitness goals",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter sex, age, height and weight so the basal metabolic rate can be calculated.",
          "Choose your activity level and goal, whether fat loss, maintenance or muscle gain, to set the calorie target.",
          "Read the gram splits for protein, carbohydrate and fat, and adjust carbs and fats to taste."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How macronutrients are split",
        "formula": "Protein g = weight kg × factor; fat g = calories × fat share ÷ 9; carbs g = remaining ÷ 4",
        "explanation": [
          "Macronutrients differ in energy density: protein and carbohydrate provide about 4 kcal per gram, fat about 9, and alcohol about 7. The usual order is to set protein first, then fat, and let carbohydrate fill the remainder. The reason is that protein and fat have minimum requirements, protein for muscle retention and satiety, fat for hormone production and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, while carbohydrate has no strict floor and is the most flexible.",
          "Protein factors shift with the goal: 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg while cutting to protect muscle in a deficit, 1.2 to 1.6 at maintenance, and 1.6 to 2.2 when building. Fat typically covers 20% to 35% of calories and should not sit below 20% for long, since hormones can suffer. Carbs absorb what is left and can vary with training, higher on training days and lower on rest days. Total energy drives weight change; the macro split shapes body composition, hunger and performance."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Macro splits by goal (70 kg, TDEE 2,200 kcal)",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Goal",
          "Calories",
          "Protein",
          "Fat",
          "Carbs"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Fat loss",
            "1,750 kcal",
            "140 g (32%)",
            "58 g (30%)",
            "166 g (38%)"
          ],
          [
            "Maintenance",
            "2,200 kcal",
            "126 g (23%)",
            "73 g (30%)",
            "259 g (47%)"
          ],
          [
            "Muscle gain",
            "2,500 kcal",
            "154 g (25%)",
            "83 g (30%)",
            "281 g (45%)"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can too much protein damage the kidneys?",
            "a": "For healthy people, current evidence does not show harm from intakes up to about 2 grams per kilogram of bodyweight. Anyone diagnosed with chronic kidney disease does need to limit protein and should follow medical advice. Higher intakes also call for more water and enough fibre, so do not let meat crowd out vegetables and fruit."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do carbs or fat cause more weight gain?",
            "a": "Neither is the culprit on its own; weight gain comes from a sustained calorie surplus. Fat is energy dense at 9 kcal per gram and easy to overeat unnoticed, while refined carbohydrate raises blood sugar quickly and satisfies poorly, so it is easy to overdo as well. In practice, controlling total calories while favouring filling foods such as protein, vegetables and whole grains works better than demonising one macro."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is a ketogenic diet suitable for most people?",
            "a": "Keto, usually under 50 grams of carbohydrate a day, was developed to treat refractory epilepsy. Any fat loss advantage comes mainly from appetite suppression and easier calorie control rather than a metabolic edge. It is hard to sustain, often causes headache and fatigue at first, and long-term safety and adherence data are limited. Without a specific medical reason, most people do better with a moderate deficit and balanced nutrition."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do I need to hit the numbers exactly every day?",
            "a": "No, and trying to is counterproductive. Macros average out over a day or a week, so a single day off changes nothing. Aim to hit your protein total across the week and let carbohydrate flex around training. Obsessing over grams tends to destroy adherence, and consistency over months matters far more than precision on any one day."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "protein-calculator": {
      "name": "Protein",
      "description": "Calculate daily protein requirements",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your daily protein needs based on your weight and goals",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter body weight (kg) and activity or fitness goal.",
          "Pick goal: maintain / cut / bulk; protein scales accordingly.",
          "See daily protein (g) and how many common-food servings that equals."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How much protein per day? Weight and goal",
        "formula": "protein(g) ≈ weight(kg) × factor (maintain 0.8–1.2, bulk 1.6–2.2, cut 1.8–2.4)",
        "explanation": [
          "Protein need is based on body weight, not total calories. Sedentary ~0.8 g/kg; regular lifters need 1.6–2.2 g/kg for synthesis and recovery.",
          "During a cut, aim higher (1.8–2.4 g/kg): in a deficit, more protein preserves lean mass and boosts satiety. Older adults should also nudge it up.",
          "The tool converts grams into servings of chicken, eggs, Greek yogurt, etc., turning an abstract number into a plate you can build."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Does too much protein hurt kidneys?",
            "a": "For normal kidney function, evidence doesn't support harm from high protein; those with chronic kidney disease should follow medical limits. For most, weight-based intake is safe."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is plant protein enough?",
            "a": "Yes, but vary sources (legumes + grains) to cover essential amino acids. If fully vegan, aim slightly above the suggestion."
          },
          {
            "q": "How much per meal absorbs best?",
            "a": "About 20–40 g quality protein per meal uses well; 3–4 meals beats one large dose for synthesis."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "carb-calculator": {
      "name": "Carbohydrate",
      "description": "Calculate daily carbohydrate needs",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your optimal daily carbohydrate intake for energy and performance",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your daily calorie target and your chosen carbohydrate percentage.",
          "Add your activity level or goal if the tool uses it to suggest calories.",
          "Read the grams of carbohydrates to aim for each day."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Carbohydrates in your daily intake",
        "formula": "Carb grams = (Calories x carb%) / 4  (carbs = 4 kcal per gram)",
        "explanation": [
          "Carbohydrates supply about 4 kilocalories per gram; setting a percentage turns your calorie goal into a gram target.",
          "The right share depends on your goal, training, and preferences; many people do well across a wide range of carb intakes."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How many carbs should I eat?",
            "a": "It varies; a common range is 45 to 65 percent of calories, but athletes or low-carb dieters may differ."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are all carbs the same?",
            "a": "No. Whole grains, fruit, and vegetables add fibre and nutrients, while added sugars give quick energy with little else."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why 4 calories per gram?",
            "a": "That is the average energy yield of carbohydrate; protein is also 4, fat is 9 per gram."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "fat-intake": {
      "name": "Fat Intake",
      "description": "Calculate daily fat intake requirements",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your daily fat intake needs including healthy fat sources",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your daily calorie goal and a target fat percentage.",
          "Choose whether to see grams of total fat or split into saturated and unsaturated.",
          "Read the grams of fat that fit your plan."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Dietary fat in context",
        "formula": "Fat grams = (Calories x fat%) / 9  (fat = 9 kcal per gram)",
        "explanation": [
          "Fat provides about 9 kilocalories per gram, more than twice carbs or protein, so small amounts contribute a lot of energy.",
          "The type of fat matters: unsaturated fats from plants and fish are generally favorable, while excess saturated and trans fats are not."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What fat percentage is healthy?",
            "a": "Many guidelines suggest 20 to 35 percent of calories from fat, emphasising unsaturated sources."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does fat have more calories?",
            "a": "Its chemical structure stores more energy per gram, which is why 9 kcal per gram versus 4 for carbs and protein."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I avoid all fat?",
            "a": "No. Fat supports hormones, nerves, and vitamin absorption; choose quality sources and watch total amounts."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "water-intake": {
      "name": "Water Intake",
      "description": "Calculate daily water intake needs",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate how much water you should drink daily based on your lifestyle",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your body weight and choose kilograms or pounds.",
          "Fill in how long and how hard you train, since sweat losses raise requirements substantially.",
          "If you are in heat or at altitude, tick those options to account for the extra loss."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How daily water needs are estimated",
        "formula": "Base need = weight (kg) × 30–35 ml; exercise = training minutes × extra factor",
        "explanation": [
          "The most common rule is body weight times 30 to 35 millilitres, so a 60 kg person needs roughly 1.8 to 2.1 litres a day. That coefficient comes from measured daily losses through urine, breathing, skin evaporation and stool. For exercise, add roughly 350 to 700 ml per hour depending on intensity and how much you sweat.",
          "One widespread misunderstanding deserves clearing up: the famous eight glasses, about two litres, is a memorable approximation that ignores weight, climate and activity. Just as importantly, that total does not all have to come from drinking, because food, especially fruit, vegetables and soups, supplies 20% to 30% of daily water, and metabolism generates around 300 ml. That means plain drinking water usually accounts for 500 to 800 ml less than the headline total. The simplest check is urine colour: pale straw means you are well hydrated, dark amber means drink more."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Daily water suggestions by weight and activity",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Weight",
          "Sedentary",
          "Moderately active",
          "Hard training 1h+"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "50 kg",
            "≈ 1.5–1.8 L",
            "≈ 1.8–2.0 L",
            "≈ 2.3–2.5 L"
          ],
          [
            "70 kg",
            "≈ 2.1–2.5 L",
            "≈ 2.5–2.8 L",
            "≈ 3.0–3.2 L"
          ],
          [
            "90 kg",
            "≈ 2.7–3.2 L",
            "≈ 3.2–3.5 L",
            "≈ 3.7–4.0 L"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Do coffee and tea count towards hydration?",
            "a": "Yes. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, but at normal intakes the net effect is still hydrating, and research generally counts coffee and tea towards daily fluid intake. What deserves attention are sugary drinks and alcohol: the first add calories, and the second is clearly diuretic and increases fluid loss."
          },
          {
            "q": "If I feel thirsty, am I already dehydrated?",
            "a": "Not necessarily. Thirst is a reliable signal for most healthy adults and typically kicks in at about 1% to 2% fluid loss, a level that does not harm performance or health. Thirst does blunt with age, however, and children often forget to drink when absorbed in play, so both groups benefit from drinking on a schedule rather than waiting."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is more water always better?",
            "a": "No. The kidneys can excrete only about 800 to 1,000 ml per hour, so drinking very large volumes quickly can cause hyponatraemia, with headache, nausea and confusion, and in extreme cases it is life threatening. It is most often seen after endurance events when people replace sweat with plain water and no electrolytes. Drinking steadily to thirst is enough for everyday life."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do I need a sports drink when exercising?",
            "a": "It depends on duration and intensity. Up to about 60 minutes of easy to moderate work, water suffices. Beyond 60 to 90 minutes, or when you sweat heavily, a drink with sodium, around 300 to 600 mg per litre, and 4% to 8% carbohydrate helps maintain electrolyte balance and glycogen. Weighing yourself before and after gives a practical target: replace roughly 1.2 to 1.5 litres for every kilogram lost."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "body-type": {
      "name": "Body Type",
      "description": "Determine your body type (ectomorph, mesomorph, endomorph)",
      "metaDescription": "Discover your body type and get personalized training and nutrition advice",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Answer a few questions about your build, or enter simple measurements.",
          "Let the tool classify you into a somatotype or a blend.",
          "Read the description and how it may relate to training and diet."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Body types, or somatotypes",
        "formula": "Descriptive classification, not a single formula",
        "explanation": [
          "Somatotypes group builds into ectomorph (lean), mesomorph (muscular), and endomorph (rounder), though most people are a mix.",
          "The model is a rough frame for training and nutrition, not a fixed destiny; genetics, diet, and exercise shape your actual results."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can my body type change?",
            "a": "Yes. Training and diet shift your composition; the label is a starting point, not a life sentence."
          },
          {
            "q": "Which body type is best?",
            "a": "None is superior; each has trade-offs, and mixed types are common and completely normal."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I train only for my type?",
            "a": "Use it as a guide, then follow evidence-based training and nutrition that fit your goals."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "healthy-weight": {
      "name": "Healthy Weight",
      "description": "Calculate your healthy weight range based on height",
      "metaDescription": "Find your healthy weight range based on your height, age, and frame size",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your height, weight, age, and sex assigned at birth.",
          "Choose the formula set if multiple are offered for your region.",
          "Read your healthy weight range and where you currently fall within it."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Healthy weight ranges, not single numbers",
        "formula": "Based on BMI 18.5 to 24.9 mapped back to weight = BMI x height^2",
        "explanation": [
          "Healthy weight is shown as a range because a single number ignores body composition; muscle, bone, and frame size all shift what is right for you.",
          "The range is a screening guide, not a diagnosis. Athletes and older adults especially should also consider body fat and waist circumference."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why a range and not one weight?",
            "a": "People of the same height differ in muscle and bone, so a band captures normal variation better than a point."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is BMI enough?",
            "a": "It is a useful screen but misses body composition; pair it with waist measurement and fitness."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the range differ by ethnicity?",
            "a": "Some guidelines use lower cut-offs for certain populations; check local public-health advice."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "army-body-fat": {
      "name": "Army Body Fat",
      "description": "Calculate body fat using US Army standards",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your body fat percentage using the official US Army tape test method",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the required measurements such as height, neck, and waist (and hips for some).",
          "Choose the correct sex-specific formula.",
          "Read your estimated body fat percentage and whether it meets the standard."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "The Army body-fat method",
        "formula": "Uses circumference-based equations that differ for men and women",
        "explanation": [
          "The method estimates body fat from tape measurements at the neck and waist, with hip circumference added for women, using sex-specific equations.",
          "It is a field method: quick and repeatable, but less precise than DEXA or hydrostatic weighing, so treat results as estimates."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why measure the neck?",
            "a": "The neck helps account for upper-body lean tissue, improving the estimate alongside waist size."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is it accurate?",
            "a": "It is good for screening and tracking, but not a laboratory measurement; small tape errors change the result."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does it apply outside the military?",
            "a": "The math works for anyone, but pass/fail standards are military-specific; consult your own guidelines."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "bac-calculator": {
      "name": "BAC",
      "description": "Calculate blood alcohol content",
      "metaDescription": "Estimate your blood alcohol content based on drinks consumed",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your sex, weight, number of standard drinks, and the hours spent drinking.",
          "Add the drink strength and duration if a detailed mode is available.",
          "Read your estimated blood alcohol concentration and the hours to sober up."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Estimating blood alcohol concentration",
        "formula": "BAC = (A x 5.14 / W x r) - 0.015 x H  (A=oz alcohol, W=lb, r=0.73/0.66)",
        "explanation": [
          "BAC estimates how much alcohol is in the bloodstream using the amount consumed, body water, and time for metabolism, which removes about 0.015 percent per hour.",
          "It is only an estimate; food, medications, sleep, and tolerance change the real effect. Never use a number to decide if you can drive."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is a standard drink?",
            "a": "Roughly 14 g of pure alcohol, about 350 ml beer, 150 ml wine, or 45 ml spirits, varying by strength."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I drive at 0.02 percent?",
            "a": "Laws differ and impairment begins with the first drink; if you have consumed alcohol, the safe choice is not to drive."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does sex change the result?",
            "a": "Average body water differs, so the same drinks produce a higher concentration for those with less water per kilogram."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "gfr-calculator": {
      "name": "GFR",
      "description": "Calculate glomerular filtration rate for kidney function",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your eGFR to assess kidney function using CKD-EPI formula",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your serum creatinine, age, and sex (and race coefficient if the older equation uses it).",
          "Choose the equation, such as CKD-EPI or MDRD.",
          "Read your estimated GFR and the corresponding kidney stage."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What eGFR tells you",
        "formula": "CKD-EPI and MDRD equations estimate GFR from creatinine, age, sex (and historically race)",
        "explanation": [
          "eGFR estimates how well your kidneys filter waste; lower numbers mean reduced kidney function, grouped into stages 1 to 5.",
          "It is a screening estimate from a blood test, not a diagnosis; only a clinician can interpret it alongside other tests."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is a normal eGFR?",
            "a": "Around 90 or above is generally normal for adults, though it naturally declines a little with age."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does race appear in some formulas?",
            "a": "Older equations included a race coefficient, but current guidelines favour race-free versions to reduce bias."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I worry about one low result?",
            "a": "A single value can vary; your clinician looks at trends and other tests before drawing conclusions."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "sleep-calculator": {
      "name": "Sleep",
      "description": "Calculate optimal sleep and wake times",
      "metaDescription": "Find the best times to sleep and wake up based on sleep cycles",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick the time you plan to fall asleep, or the time you need to wake up.",
          "Set how long you usually take to fall asleep, most people need 10 to 20 minutes, and the tool subtracts it.",
          "Read the suggested times, all aligned to the end of complete sleep cycles."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How sleep cycles are calculated",
        "formula": "Wake time = bedtime + time to fall asleep + cycles × 90 minutes (4–6 cycles)",
        "explanation": [
          "Sleep unfolds in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, each containing light sleep (N1 and N2), deep sleep (N3) and rapid eye movement. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night and handles physical repair and growth hormone release, while REM increases later and supports memory consolidation and emotional regulation. Waking at the end of a cycle, close to the surface, leaves you clear-headed, whereas being woken from deep sleep produces heavy sleep inertia and sluggishness.",
          "So the calculator works from the moment you fall asleep, after subtracting the time it takes you to drift off, and steps forward in 90-minute blocks, listing the wake times for four, five and six cycles, which is 6, 7.5 and 9 hours. One caveat: 90 minutes is a population average, individual cycles run anywhere from 70 to 120 minutes and shorten slightly with age. Treat this as a practical scheduling aid rather than a precise read on your body clock."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Cycle counts and total sleep",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Cycles",
          "Sleep",
          "Typical use",
          "Suits"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "4",
            "6 hours",
            "Short-term, emergencies",
            "Not for the long run"
          ],
          [
            "5",
            "7.5 hours",
            "Everyday for most adults",
            "Recommended"
          ],
          [
            "6",
            "9 hours",
            "Recovery, after hard training",
            "Teenagers or catching up"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Do I really need eight hours?",
            "a": "Not necessarily. Eight is a round number that is easy to remember; real needs vary, and most adults fall somewhere between seven and nine hours. What matters more is completing whole cycles and how you feel on waking. If 7.5 hours leaves you sharp, there is no reason to force eight. The test is whether you feel sleepy during the day and struggle to get up."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why am I still tired after a long night?",
            "a": "Often because you woke from deep sleep, or because quality was poor. Frequent awakenings, snoring with pauses in breathing, alcohol before bed, and a bedroom that is too bright or warm all reduce deep and REM sleep. If you consistently wake unrefreshed despite adequate hours, especially with loud snoring and morning headaches, ask about a sleep study."
          },
          {
            "q": "How long should a nap be?",
            "a": "Ten to twenty minutes is ideal: it restores alertness without reaching deep sleep, so you wake clear-headed. Thirty to sixty minutes tends to end in deep sleep and produces noticeable grogginess. If you want a full cycle, allow 90 minutes. It also helps to nap before about 3 pm so night-time sleep is not affected."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does sleeping in at the weekend help?",
            "a": "It repays part of the debt but cannot fully offset chronic short sleep, and it shifts your body clock, making Monday harder, the so-called social jetlag. Keeping bedtime and wake time within about an hour every day works better. If you are genuinely short, going to bed an hour earlier beats sleeping three hours later."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "due-date": {
      "name": "Due Date",
      "description": "Calculate pregnancy due date and milestones",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your pregnancy due date and track important milestones",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP).",
          "The tool adds the standard 280 days (40 weeks) to estimate the due date.",
          "See due date, current week, and key prenatal checkup milestones."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How due date is calculated: LMP and weeks",
        "formula": "due = LMP + 280 days (Naegele: month+9, day+7)",
        "explanation": [
          "The common method is Naegele's rule: from the first day of LMP, add 280 days (40 weeks). The shortcut is 'month+9, day+7'.",
          "Only about 5% deliver exactly on the due date; within two weeks either side is normal. Early ultrasound (esp. CRL) refines the week better.",
          "The tool also shows current gestational week and key screenings (NT, anatomy, glucose) so you can map the whole pregnancy timeline."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Is the due date accurate?",
            "a": "It's a midpoint estimate. ~80% are born within two weeks of it; only ~5% on the exact day. Treat it as a window, not a date."
          },
          {
            "q": "Irregular cycles?",
            "a": "Long or irregular cycles make LMP-based dating early. First-trimester ultrasound (CRL) is more accurate."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why weeks, not months?",
            "a": "Weeks (7-day) are finer; screenings are scheduled by week (NT 11–13, glucose 24–28), so weeks avoid missing windows."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "ovulation-calculator": {
      "name": "Ovulation",
      "description": "Calculate ovulation date and fertile window",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate your ovulation date and most fertile days",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter first day of last period and average cycle length (default 28).",
          "The tool estimates ovulation (~14 days before next period) and the fertile window.",
          "See estimated ovulation for the next 6 cycles to plan or avoid."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How to find ovulation: cycle and ovulation day",
        "formula": "ovulation ≈ next period − 14 days; fertile ≈ 5 days before to 1 day after",
        "explanation": [
          "Ovulation usually happens ~14 days before the next period (luteal phase is fairly fixed), while follicular length varies, so 'cycle−14' beats 'cycle÷2'.",
          "Sperm survive ~3–5 days, egg ~12–24 h, so the fertile window spans ~5 days before to 1 day after ovulation—about a week.",
          "Good for regular cycles as an estimate; for precision use basal temperature, OPKs, or an app. Irregular cycles widen the error."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can I calculate with irregular cycles?",
            "a": "You can estimate but with larger error. The more variable the cycle, the harder ovulation is to predict—verify with OPK + temperature."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the fertile window so long?",
            "a": "Sperm live for days and ovulation can shift, so covering the days around ovulation is safest."
          },
          {
            "q": "Used for both trying and avoiding?",
            "a": "Yes, same principle: time intercourse for conception, or use protection to avoid—ovulation tracking alone isn't reliable birth control."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "period-calculator": {
      "name": "Period",
      "description": "Track menstrual cycle and predict next period",
      "metaDescription": "Track your menstrual cycle and predict your next period",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter first day of last period and average cycle length.",
          "The tool estimates next period and upcoming cycle dates.",
          "Also estimates the current phase (follicular / luteal)."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Predicting periods: cycle math",
        "formula": "next = last first day + cycle length; phase ≈ days since last first day",
        "explanation": [
          "The core is cycle length: add one cycle to the last first day for the next expected date. Most people fall in 21–35 days; 28 is common, not a standard.",
          "Knowing length also hints at phase: ovulation ~14 days before next period, follicular before, luteal after—helping explain mood/energy swings.",
          "Good for regular-cycle reminders; if cycles are chaotic, very long, or painful, see a doctor for PCOS/thyroid rather than relying on math."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What cycle length is normal?",
            "a": "21–35 days is common and varies by person. As long as yours is stable, you needn't hit 28."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is mine never on time?",
            "a": "Stress, big weight changes, poor sleep, illness all disrupt it. Occasional shifts are normal; chronic irregularity deserves a check."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can this detect pregnancy?",
            "a": "No. A late period is only one possible sign; confirm with a test. This tool only schedules, not diagnoses."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "pregnancy-weight-gain": {
      "name": "Pregnancy Weight Gain",
      "description": "Calculate recommended weight gain during pregnancy",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate healthy weight gain recommendations during pregnancy",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter your pre-pregnancy weight and height to compute your starting BMI.",
          "Select the recommended gain range for your BMI category.",
          "Track your current gain against the suggested total for each trimester."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Recommended weight gain in pregnancy",
        "formula": "Total by BMI: underweight 12.5 to 18 kg, normal 11.5 to 16 kg, overweight 7 to 11.5 kg, obese 5 to 9 kg",
        "explanation": [
          "Weight gain supports the baby, placenta, amniotic fluid, and maternal tissue stores; the right amount depends mainly on starting BMI.",
          "Gaining too little or too much is linked to delivery and infant-health risks, so ranges are guidance, not strict rules, and your clinician individualizes them."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What if I am expecting twins?",
            "a": "Recommended totals are higher for multiples; follow your provider's specific schedule."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I diet to lose weight while pregnant?",
            "a": "Pregnancy is not the time for weight-loss dieting; focus on steady, adequate gain with good nutrition."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does BMI at the start matter?",
            "a": "It sets the safest gain band because risk patterns differ by starting weight."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "basic-calculator": {
      "name": "Basic",
      "description": "Perform arithmetic calculations with expression support",
      "metaDescription": "Free online basic calculator for arithmetic operations and expressions",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Type numbers using the on-screen pad or your keyboard.",
          "Use + - x / and parentheses for the order of operations.",
          "Read the result, and use clear or backspace to start a new calculation."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Order of operations",
        "formula": "PEMDAS: Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication/Division, Addition/Subtraction",
        "explanation": [
          "The calculator follows standard precedence, so 2 + 3 x 4 equals 14, not 20; parentheses force a different grouping.",
          "It is exact for the digits shown, but very long numbers are rounded for display; use scientific mode for high precision."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why is my answer different from expected?",
            "a": "Check parentheses and operation order; multiplication runs before addition unless you group it."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does it keep history?",
            "a": "This simple view shows the current line; use the expression history if your version includes it."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can it handle fractions?",
            "a": "It works in decimals; convert fractions to decimals first for accurate results."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "fraction-calculator": {
      "name": "Fraction",
      "description": "Add, subtract, multiply, divide, and simplify fractions",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate fractions with step-by-step solutions and mixed numbers",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick an operation: add, subtract, multiply, divide, or just simplify a mixed/improper fraction.",
          "Enter the numerators and denominators (multiple fractions supported); the tool validates that no denominator is zero.",
          "Read the simplified result and toggle between improper and mixed-number display."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What are fraction operations?",
        "formula": "a/b ± c/d = (ad ± bc)/bd; (a/b) × (c/d) = ac/bd",
        "explanation": [
          "A fraction has a numerator and a denominator: the denominator is how many equal parts the whole is split into, the numerator is how many of those parts you take. To add or subtract you must first rewrite both with a common denominator; multiply top-by-top and bottom-by-bottom; divide by multiplying by the reciprocal.",
          "Results are usually reduced: divide numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor to get the simplest form, and turn an improper fraction into a mixed number when the top is larger. Fraction arithmetic underpins ratios, probability, recipes and engineering measurement."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can the denominator be zero?",
            "a": "No. Division by zero is undefined, so the calculator warns you the moment a denominator is zero to prevent invalid output."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I enter a mixed number?",
            "a": "Enter the whole part first, then the numerator and denominator; results can also be toggled between improper and mixed form for easier reading."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the result sometimes a repeating decimal?",
            "a": "A fraction becomes a repeating decimal when its denominator has prime factors other than 2 or 5. That is normal, and you can keep as many digits as you need."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "percentage-calculator": {
      "name": "Percentage",
      "description": "Calculate percentages, percent change, and percent difference",
      "metaDescription": "Free online percentage calculator. Find X% of Y, what percent one number is of another, and increases or discounts fast, no signup.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick the percentage operation you need (X% of Y, X is what % of Y, or increase/decrease).",
          "Enter the numbers into the input fields.",
          "Read the result instantly below the inputs."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How Percentage Calculations Work",
        "formula": "Part = (Percentage / 100) × Whole",
        "explanation": [
          "A percentage is simply a fraction of 100. Calculating 'X% of Y' means multiplying Y by X divided by 100.",
          "Percentages are used daily for discounts, tax, tips, interest rates, and comparing changes between two values."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "How do I find what percent one number is of another?",
            "a": "Divide the part by the whole and multiply by 100. For example, 25 of 200 is (25 / 200) × 100 = 12.5%."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I calculate a percentage increase?",
            "a": "Subtract the original from the new value, divide by the original, then multiply by 100."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I use it for discounts?",
            "a": "Yes. To find a sale price after a discount, calculate the percent of the original price and subtract it."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "percent-error": {
      "name": "Percent Error",
      "description": "Calculate percent error between experimental and actual values",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate percent error for scientific experiments and measurements",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the measured (experimental) value and the accepted (true) value.",
          "Choose whether to see the absolute error as well as the percentage.",
          "Read the percent error, which tells you how far the measurement is from the reference."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What percent error measures",
        "formula": "Percent error = |measured - accepted| / |accepted| x 100%",
        "explanation": [
          "Percent error expresses the size of a mistake relative to the true value, so a 5 percent error means the same proportion whether you measured grams or kilometres.",
          "A smaller percent error means higher accuracy. Systematic errors from a faulty instrument show up as consistently biased results."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Can percent error be negative?",
            "a": "The formula uses absolute value, so it is reported as a positive magnitude; the sign is dropped to show size, not direction."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is a good percent error?",
            "a": "It depends on the field; school labs often target under 5 percent, while precision engineering expects far less."
          },
          {
            "q": "Percent error vs percent difference?",
            "a": "Error compares a measurement to a known true value; difference compares two measurements to each other."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "rounding-calculator": {
      "name": "Rounding",
      "description": "Round numbers using different methods and precision",
      "metaDescription": "Round numbers to decimal places, significant figures, or custom precision",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the number you want to round and choose the target decimal places or significant figures.",
          "Pick a rounding rule such as round half up or round to even if offered.",
          "Read the rounded result and the digits that were kept."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Why rounding rules matter",
        "formula": "Round to n decimals: keep n digits after the point, adjust the last kept digit by the next",
        "explanation": [
          "Rounding reduces noise from measurements that are not exact, but repeated rounding can drift results, so round only at the final step.",
          "Different rules (half up versus round to even) handle the exact .5 case differently, which matters in statistics and finance."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is round half to even?",
            "a": "When the dropped part is exactly .5, round to the nearest even digit; this avoids bias when many .5 cases occur."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I round during each step?",
            "a": "No. Keep full precision while calculating and round only the final answer to avoid accumulating error."
          },
          {
            "q": "What are significant figures?",
            "a": "They are the meaningful digits carried by a measurement's precision, counting from the first non-zero digit."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "average-calculator": {
      "name": "Average",
      "description": "Calculate mean, median, mode, and other averages",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate arithmetic mean, weighted average, geometric mean, and harmonic mean",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the numbers line by line or comma separated; add or remove any time.",
          "The tool ignores blank cells and flags obvious outliers (you can keep them if needed).",
          "Read the arithmetic mean, the sum and the count, and toggle to see the median."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How is the average calculated?",
        "formula": "mean = (x₁ + x₂ + … + xₙ) ÷ n",
        "explanation": [
          "The arithmetic mean adds up a set of values and divides by how many there are. It is a simple, intuitive measure of the 'centre' of the data, but it is very sensitive to extremes — a single very large or very small value can pull the mean noticeably up or down.",
          "When a distribution is skewed or contains outliers, the median usually describes the typical value better than the mean. For example, a few very high incomes pull the average income up so most people appear 'below average'. Check both mean and median together."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between mean and median?",
            "a": "The mean is the sum divided by the count; the median is the middle value after sorting. They diverge a lot in skewed data, and the median resists extreme values."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do empty cells affect the result?",
            "a": "No. Blank cells and unrecognised text are ignored; only valid numbers are used."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can it do a weighted average?",
            "a": "This tool computes the simple arithmetic mean. If each item has a different weight (e.g. exam weights), use a weighted average calculator."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "long-division": {
      "name": "Long Division",
      "description": "Perform long division with step-by-step solution",
      "metaDescription": "Learn long division with detailed step-by-step solutions and remainders",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the dividend (the number being divided) and the divisor (the number you divide by).",
          "Run the calculator to see the quotient and the remainder step by step.",
          "Read the full decimal result and, if it does not divide evenly, the remainder."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How long division works",
        "formula": "dividend = divisor × quotient + remainder; 0 ≤ remainder < divisor",
        "explanation": [
          "Long division repeatedly subtracts the largest multiple of the divisor that fits, bringing down digits until no remainder or a repeating decimal appears.",
          "The remainder is what is left after the whole-number part of the quotient; it is always smaller than the divisor."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the remainder?",
            "a": "The leftover amount when the dividend is not a whole multiple of the divisor."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why does division sometimes repeat?",
            "a": "Some fractions have denominators with prime factors other than 2 and 5, forcing a repeating decimal."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can the divisor be zero?",
            "a": "No, division by zero is undefined and the calculator will reject it."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "exponent-calculator": {
      "name": "Exponent",
      "description": "Calculate powers and exponential expressions",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate powers, exponents, and exponential expressions",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the bottom number and the index to be applied.",
          "Select the index as positive, negative or fraction.",
          "View the results, note that the negative index is divided by the bottom."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How the index works",
        "formula": "a^n = a x a x ...(n); a^-n = 1/ a^n; a^(1/n) = n + root",
        "explanation": [
          "The index indicates the number of times the bottom is multiplied; the negative index translates into a fraction, which is equal to the opening.",
          "The index compresses the repetitive multiplication into a compact swarm, which is why it appears in growth, decay and scientific algorithms.",
          "An an for n a multiplication. Core law: Add a multiplication index to the base (am x an = am+n), multiplication of the multiplier index for aluminum ((am)n = amn), negative index is the last (a-n = 1/an) and fraction index is the opening (a^(1/2) = a). These laws make it possible to simplify the majority of algebra expressions.",
          "The chicken soup \"a 1% improvement per day\" is actually a mathematical fact: 1.01365 ≈ 37.78, 37 times the original 37 a year later; in turn 0.99365 ≈ 0.026, only 2.6% a year later. ** The difference between the two is not two times, but more than 1,400 times **, which is the terrible thing about index growth. Understanding the growth of the index is critical for the management of finance (recontribution), the prevention of disease (transmission rate), and the development of technology (Moor law)."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What does negative mean?",
            "a": "is 1 divided by the base number of the timebar, so that 2^3 equals 1/8."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do you fit in?",
            "a": "Keep the bottom unchanged, with an index added: a^m x a^n = a^ (m+n)."
          },
          {
            "q": "What's the score index?",
            "a": "The denominator is the number of roots; for example, x^(1/2) is the square root of x."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why equal 1 (a ≠ 0)?",
            "a": "It can be derived from the index law: am ÷an = am-n. When m = n, the left is am ÷ am = 1, the right is a0, so a0 = 1. It can also be seen from a diminishing perspective: 23 = 8,22 = 4,21 = 2, divided by 2, so 20 = 1,2−1 = 0.5. Note that 00 is undefined."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do you use the scientific algorithm?",
            "a": "Write a number in a form of 10n (1 ≤a < 10). For example, 3,000 000 = 3 x 106, 00045 = 4.5 x 10-4. Index n is positive for large and negative for decimal. Calculator displays 3E6 or 3e6. When processing astronomical numbers or microscales, the scientific algorithm is much more reliable than a long string of 0."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Common calculator results quick check",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Bottom",
          "Index",
          "Result",
          "Apply"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "2",
            "10",
            "1,024",
            "1 KB = 1024 bytes"
          ],
          [
            "2",
            "16",
            "65,536",
            "16 bit Depth / port cap"
          ],
          [
            "2",
            "32",
            "About 4.29 billion",
            "32-bit address space"
          ],
          [
            "10",
            "3",
            "1,000",
            "kg = 1000 g"
          ],
          [
            "10",
            "9",
            "1 billion",
            "Giga prefix"
          ],
          [
            "e",
            "1",
            "2.71828",
            "Natural constant"
          ],
          [
            "1.01",
            "365",
            "37.78",
            "One percent of the year's profit per day."
          ],
          [
            "0.99",
            "365",
            "0.026",
            "A daily retreat of 1% of annual interest"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "root-calculator": {
      "name": "Root",
      "description": "Calculate square roots, cube roots, and nth roots",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate square roots, cube roots, and any nth root of numbers",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the radicand (the number under the root) and the index, such as 2 for square root.",
          "Choose whether to allow complex results for negative inputs if offered.",
          "Read the principal root and, for squares, the positive and negative values."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Understanding roots",
        "formula": "n-th root of x = y where y^n = x; square root means index 2",
        "explanation": [
          "A root answers the question 'which number, raised to this power, gives the radicand'; the square root is the most common case.",
          "Even roots of negative numbers are not real, so calculators either report an error or return a complex number depending on mode."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why are there two square roots?",
            "a": "Both a positive and a negative number square to the same value; the principal root shown is the non-negative one."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I take a root of a negative number?",
            "a": "Odd roots (cube, etc.) of negatives are real, but even roots are not real unless complex mode is on."
          },
          {
            "q": "What is the index?",
            "a": "It is the small number that says which root; 2 is square root, 3 is cube root, and so on."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "logarithm-calculator": {
      "name": "Logarithm",
      "description": "Calculate logarithms with any base",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate logarithms with base 10, natural log, or any custom base",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the number whose logarithm you need and pick the base, such as 10, e, or 2.",
          "Choose natural log, common log, or a custom base if available.",
          "Read the result, which is the exponent that reaches your number."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "What a logarithm is",
        "formula": "log_b(x) = y means b^y = x",
        "explanation": [
          "A logarithm is the inverse of exponentiation: it tells you what power the base must be raised to in order to equal the number.",
          "Logarithms compress huge ranges into manageable numbers, which is why they appear in pH, decibels, and earthquake magnitude."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between ln and log?",
            "a": "ln uses base e (about 2.718); log usually means base 10 in many contexts, though some fields use log for base e."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can I take the log of zero or a negative?",
            "a": "No real logarithm exists for zero or negatives; the result would require an impossible power."
          },
          {
            "q": "How do I change the base?",
            "a": "Use the change-of-base rule: log_b(x) = log_k(x) / log_k(b) for any convenient base k."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "quadratic-calculator": {
      "name": "Quadratic Formula",
      "description": "Solve quadratic equations and find roots",
      "metaDescription": "Solve quadratic equations using the quadratic formula with step-by-step solutions",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the coefficients a, b, and c from your quadratic equation a x^2 + b x + c = 0.",
          "Make sure a is not zero, otherwise the equation is linear, not quadratic.",
          "Read the roots, the discriminant, and whether the solutions are real or complex."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Solving quadratics",
        "formula": "x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a); discriminant D = b² - 4ac",
        "explanation": [
          "The quadratic formula finds the two values of x where the parabola crosses the axis; the discriminant tells you how many real solutions exist.",
          "If the discriminant is positive there are two real roots, zero gives one repeated root, and negative gives two complex conjugate roots."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What does the discriminant tell me?",
            "a": "Its sign reveals the nature of the roots: positive for two real, zero for one, negative for two complex."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why are there two answers?",
            "a": "A squared term generally yields two symmetric solutions; the plus and minus in the formula captures both."
          },
          {
            "q": "What if a is zero?",
            "a": "Then it is a linear equation with one solution; enter it into a linear solver instead."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "factor-calculator": {
      "name": "Factor",
      "description": "Find all factors of a number",
      "metaDescription": "Find all factors, factor pairs, and prime factors of any number",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter a positive integer you want to factor.",
          "Run the calculator to list all factors or prime factors.",
          "Read the factor pairs and, for prime factorization, the exponent form."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Finding factors of a number",
        "formula": "if n mod d = 0 then d is a factor; prime factors: n = p1^a1 × p2^a2 × ...",
        "explanation": [
          "A factor divides a number with no remainder; the calculator tests divisors up to the square root to find them efficiently.",
          "Prime factorization rewrites the number as a product of primes, which is the basis for GCD, LCM and simplifying fractions."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is the difference between factors and prime factors?",
            "a": "Factors include composites like 6; prime factors are only the irreducible primes such as 2 and 3."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does 1 count as a factor?",
            "a": "Yes, 1 and the number itself are always factors, but neither is prime."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why stop at the square root?",
            "a": "Any factor larger than √n pairs with one smaller than √n, so testing beyond is redundant."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "gcd-calculator": {
      "name": "GCD",
      "description": "Calculate Greatest Common Divisor of multiple numbers",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate the greatest common divisor (GCD/GCF) of two or more numbers",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter two or more integers.",
          "Runs the calculator for the maximum number of conventions.",
          "View the largest number of conventions and, if necessary, the steps of the Euro-Culture algorithm."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Maximum number of conventions",
        "formula": "gcd(a,b) = gcd(b,a mod b) until the balance is 0; last non-zero balance is the maximum number of conventions",
        "explanation": [
          "The maximum number of conventions is the maximum number of integers capable of dividing each input by an integer that allows the fractions to be approximately the simplest.",
          "Euclid algorithms continue to replace larger numbers with the rest, much faster than the one-by-one elimination.",
          "The core insinuation of the transverse dichotomy is gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b). In other words, the number of conventions with the two largest numbers equals the number of conventions with the smaller and the remaining number. The relationship is applied repeatedly until the balance is zero, and the final division is the answer. It is extremely efficient — even for hundreds — the number of calculations is only a small number of digits, which is why it has been in use for over 2,000 years.",
          "There are more practical scenarios for the maximum number of conventions than would have been imagined: approximately fractions (molecule denominators and GCDs received the simplest form directly), brick-laying problems (refining the rectangular floor with square bricks of the largest size, with the brick edge being a wide GCD), and ciphers (RSA algorithms need to calculate the modulus, using the extension of the Euclid algorithm). Two numbers of GCDs are called \"mutuality\" at 1 and this is of particular significance in fractional computing and cryptography."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What use is the largest convention?",
            "a": "An approximation, and as a step towards a minimum common multiple and a simulation."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do negative numbers have the largest number of conventions?",
            "a": "Yes, the maximum number of conventions is absolute and the symbol does not affect the result."
          },
          {
            "q": "If there's no public factor?",
            "a": "The largest number of conventions is 1 and 2 are referred to as interoperability."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why do you call it \"Swipe\"?",
            "a": "The term \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl\" and \"twirl\" means \"twirl.\" This method is recorded in the Geometry Original in Euclid (about 300 B.C.), one of the first algorithms in human history, more than a thousand years before many subsequent mathematical discoveries."
          },
          {
            "q": "What about the three-digit maximum number of conventions?",
            "a": "(c) = gcd(a, b, c). Go first to the first two GCDs, then to the third and then to the third GCDs, which can be extended to any number. gcd(12, 18, 30), for example: gcd(12,18) = 6, then gcd(6,30) = 6, the answer is 6. Similarly, the minimum common multiple of multiple numbers can also be pushed by lcm(a, b, c) = lcm(a, b), c)."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "To maximize the number of conventions by twirling one another.",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Match",
          "Calculator",
          "GCD",
          "Annotations"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "12, 18",
            "18 = 12×1 + 6 → 12 = 6×2 + 0",
            "6",
            "Division of the remaining 0 hours"
          ],
          [
            "48, 18",
            "48 = 18×2 + 12 → 18 = 12×1 + 6 → 12 = 6×2 + 0",
            "6",
            "Three steps."
          ],
          [
            "100, 75",
            "100 = 75×1 + 25 → 75 = 25×3 + 0",
            "25",
            "Two steps."
          ],
          [
            "17, 5",
            "17 = 5×3 + 2 → 5 = 2×2 + 1 → 2 = 1×2 + 0",
            "1",
            "Reciprocity"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "lcm-calculator": {
      "name": "LCM",
      "description": "Calculate Least Common Multiple of multiple numbers",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate the least common multiple (LCM) of two or more numbers",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter two or more integers.",
          "Runs the calculator for a minimum common multiple.",
          "It is useful to see the minimum common multiple, which is added to the heterogeneity."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Minimal common multiple",
        "formula": "lcm(a,b) = |a x b|/ gcd(a,b); extension by pair",
        "explanation": [
          "The minimum common multiple is the minimum positive integer that can be divided by each input integrator and is commonly used when matching cycles or points add points.",
          "It is calculated by the maximum number of conventions and even large numbers are efficient and does not need to list multiples.",
          "There is an elegant relationship between the lowest common multiple and the largest number of conventions:** LCM(a, b) x GCD(a, b) = a x b**. This means that as long as GCDs are sought, LCM can be calculated directly without a separate list of multiples. This method is particularly efficient for large numbers - it is easier to list multiples of 15 and 20, but it is painful to list multiples of 1,234 and 5,678.",
          "The most classic application is the fair fraction of fractions plus minus 1/4 + 1/6 requiring the lowest common multiple of denominators 4 and 6, which becomes 3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12. The use of LCM instead of the direct product (24) as the common denominator can make the calculation smaller and the result easier to measure. Other scenes include the problem of synchronisation of cycles (two vehicles are maintained every 4 and 6 days, respectively, with a space of 12 days) and the need for `symmetrical cycles' in order to schedule, assemble, etc."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "When is the minimum common multiple required?",
            "a": "The difference is added to the public cycle."
          },
          {
            "q": "What if one of them is zero?",
            "a": "The minimum common multiple with 0 is defined as 0, as 0 is a multiple of any number."
          },
          {
            "q": "How does the minimum common multiple relate to the maximum number of conventions?",
            "a": "The two multipliers are equal to a two-digit product, i.e. lcm = |axb| / gcd."
          },
          {
            "q": "How quickly do you judge the number of people who interact?",
            "a": "Two numbers of GCDs are one, i.e., each other. Quick-judging techniques: If both numbers are prime and not equal, they are bound to interact; two integers adjacent to each other are bound to interact (e.g. 8 and 9); one number is prime and is not divided by another. But the answer is to judge whether the arbitrary two numbers (e.g. 1,234 and 5,678) are of a quality, and whether the most reliable or the GCD — a few steps in the spin-off — is possible."
          },
          {
            "q": "What about the minimum number of three?",
            "a": "(a, b, c) = lcm (a, b), c). Note that **can't ** be extended directly to three — axbxc ÷ gcd(a,b,c) by `multiplications'. For example, lcm (2, 3, 4): lcm (2, 3) = 6, lcm (6, 4) = 12, the answer is 12. 2x3x4 ÷gcd (2,3,4) = 24 ÷ 1 = 24, the result of the error."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Calculate the minimum common multiple using formula LCM(a, b) = axb ÷ GCD(a,b)",
        "tableHeaders": [
