    "hex-calculator": {
      "name": "Hexadecimal",
      "description": "Convert and calculate in hexadecimal",
      "metaDescription": "Convert and perform arithmetic operations in hexadecimal",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter a hexadecimal value (using 0-9 and A-F) or other anode.",
          "Select the target digit, such as decimal or binary.",
          "View the conversion results and check the numerical groups."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Concise Hexadecimal Guide",
        "formula": "Each hexadecimal bit = 4 bit; value = thallium (digit i x 16^i)",
        "explanation": [
          "The hexadecimal base is 16 and each number corresponds to 4 binary bits, so it is a compact way to write memory addresses and colours (e.g. #FF8800).",
          "When hexadecimal to decimal, multiplied by 16 bits each; A-F stands for 10 to 15.",
          "Hexadecimal is 0-9 plus A-F for 0-15,** a hexadecimal corresponds to 4-bit binary** (known as a nibble). So 2-bit hexadecimal is a byte (0x00-0xFF = 0-255). This whole correspondence makes hexadecimal the de facto criterion for the binary data: memory address, colour value, MAC address, machine code.",
          "The colour of the web page #RRRGGBB is three groups of hexadecimals: #FF000 is pure red (red channel 255, green 0, blue 0), #FF white, #000000 black, #808080 ash. ** Hexadecimal-todecimal-to-decimal-to-chromosomal ** #336699 plus 2 each to #5588BB, overall brightness raised and the relative relationship between the corridors remained unchanged."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why hexadecimal rather than binary?",
            "a": "It is shorter, less prone to error, and corresponds to half a byte for each number."
          },
          {
            "q": "What's an A-F?",
            "a": "They express Decimal 10-15 as a single hexadecimal number."
          },
          {
            "q": "Where's the hexadecimal routine?",
            "a": "CSS colour, MAC address and memory storage are expressed on hexadecimal."
          },
          {
            "q": "What do you mean, \"0x prefix\"?",
            "a": "It is a programming language that indicates a hexadecimal prefix (C, Java, JavaScript, Python). Similarly, 0b for binary, 0o for octal. So 0x10 is 10 in hexadecimal, equal to 16 in Decimal, not 10 -- this is the easiest pit for beginners."
          },
          {
            "q": "How does hexadecimal translate to decimal?",
            "a": "Right-to-left, n-weight is 16n. For example, 0x2A = 2x161 + 10x160 = 32 + 10 = 42. Remember the weight sequences 1, 16, 256, 4096 (160 to 163) to handle most scenarios. In turn, the decimal hexadecimal is in the same way as the binary."
          }
        ],
