    "tire-sizing": {
      "name": "Tire Size Calculator",
      "description": "Calculate tire dimensions, compare sizes, and check speedometer error",
      "metaDescription": "Free tire size calculator - dimensions, comparison, speedometer error. Supports European metric notation (205/55R16).",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the current tyre code (e.g. 205/55R16) and the candidate size.",
          "If the wheel is different in diameter, enter.",
          "View the diameter of the new size, the height of the tire wall and the error of the code table."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Compare tyre sizes",
        "formula": "diameter = wheel + 2 x (brack width x flat ratio / 100 / 25.4) (in)",
        "explanation": [
          "Tire code coded the width, flat ratio and the diameter of the wheel; changes change the height of the body, gap and code reading.",
          "The rolling diameter should be kept within approximately 3 per cent of the original plant in order to avoid errors in ABS, tooth ratio and mileage.",
          "Tyre diameter = height of + 2 x tire wall Take the example of 205/55 R16: foetal wall height = 205 x 55% = 11275 mm, wheel 毂 16 inch = 406.4 mm, total diameter = 406.4 + 2 x 11275 = 631.9 mm. ** The retrofitting of the wheel radon must be done in a total diameter close to the original plant (the error is within > 3 per cent)**, otherwise it will result in an incorrect speed schedule (lower diameter, high velocity), accelerated slowness (equivalent to a change of moving ratio), tweak eyebrow, ABS/ESP calibration error.",
          "Larger rims (e.g. 16 inches to 18 inches) are ** synchronized to lower flat ratio** to maintain total diameter: 205/55 R16 (631.9 mm) to 18 inches. The cost is: a thin tire wall, a bad shock reduction, a hard road sense, and an easy to drum through a pit; a wide tire, a higher grip, but an increase in fuel consumption and tire noise; and an increase in the cost of both tyres and wheels. ** The day-to-day step proposal is to maintain the original specifications and to seek to manipulate the upgraded tyre brand instead of the size** (the same size of the Michelin PS4 and the original plant ' s energy-efficient tires, the control gap is much greater than the change of the wheel)."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What is 55 of 205/55R16?",
            "a": "The wall height is 55% of the 205 mm break."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why is the diameter approaching the plant?",
            "a": "The deviation from the General Assembly resulted in the incorrect performance of the watch, the tooth ratio and the brake."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can you get a wider baby?",
            "a": "Only if there is a gap in the arc and the width of the arc supports the new fracture."
          },
          {
            "q": "When should we change tires?",
            "a": "Three hard indicators: 1 **write depth below 1.6 mm** (statutory limits, wear marks in tyre gutters, as long as they are flat; area with more than 3 mm recommended for rain; 2 ** birth age above 6 years** (rubber age becomes hard, scratches lower, impotent, even when the gratuity is deep, and production dates are four below DOT on the side of the tyre, like \"2523\" = 25 weeks in 2023); 3 ** injury** (beam-side drums, crack-up deep visible curtains, multiple patching and patching positions on the side of the tyre). ** The four tyres should be replaced at the same time, at least in line with the two axes** (striped, branded, worn, etc.), otherwise different grips on the right or the left will affect brake and manipulation stability."
          },
          {
            "q": "How much is it?",
            "a": "Based on** vehicle identification**, not the number on the tyre. The marking is usually on the side of the driver ' s door frame, the oil tank cap or a user ' s manual with a common value of 2.2–2.5 bar (cold tires). N.B.: 1 Tires sidemarks are ** maximum stress **, not recommended value; 2 ** cold tire measurements** (stopping for more than 3 hours or not exceeding 2 km), heat tires are high 0.2-0.3 bar; 3 full load and high-speed travel can be appropriately raised by 0.1-0.2 bar; 4 Seasonal changes: temperature is about 0.07 bar at 10°C and cell pressure is about 0.07 bar at winter. ** The birth pressure is much lower than the risk ** — Low birth pressure increases the stenching of the side and is the main cause of high-speed flats."
          }
        ],
