    "unit-converter": {
      "name": "Unit Converter",
      "description": "Convert between units of length, mass, temperature, area, volume, speed, time, data, energy, power and pressure.",
      "metaDescription": "Free online unit converter for length, mass, temperature, area, volume, speed, time, data, energy, power and pressure.",
      "category": "Category",
      "value": "Value",
      "from": "From",
      "to": "To",
      "swap": "Swap",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick a category: length, weight, temperature, area, volume, speed, time and more.",
          "Enter the value and choose the source and target units. If a unit is missing, switch the category first.",
          "Read the result along with the same value expressed in other common units."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How unit conversion works under the hood",
        "formula": "Target = value × (source factor ÷ target factor)",
        "explanation": [
          "Each category has one international base unit: metres for length, kilograms for mass, kelvin for temperature, litres for volume. Every other unit is stored as a single factor saying how many base units it equals. A conversion first turns the input into base units, then divides by the target factor. Adding a unit means registering one factor rather than writing a rule for every pair, and it avoids the rounding drift you get from chaining factors together.",
          "Temperature is the one category that cannot be handled by multiplication alone. Celsius and kelvin differ by an offset (K = °C + 273.15), and Celsius and Fahrenheit use a linear transform with an intercept (°F = °C × 9/5 + 32), so temperature needs its own branch. That is exactly where simple converters go wrong. Watch out too for units that share a name across regions: a US gallon is about 3.785 litres, an imperial gallon about 4.546, a gap of roughly 20%."
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Common conversions at a glance",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Category",
          "Relationship",
          "Example"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Length",
            "1 inch = 2.54 cm",
            "10 in = 25.4 cm"
          ],
          [
            "Length",
            "1 foot = 30.48 cm",
            "6 ft = 182.9 cm"
          ],
          [
            "Mass",
            "1 pound = 0.4536 kg",
            "150 lb = 68.04 kg"
          ],
          [
            "Volume",
            "1 US gallon = 3.785 L",
            "5 gal = 18.93 L"
          ],
          [
            "Temperature",
            "°F = °C × 9/5 + 32",
            "25 °C = 77 °F"
          ],
          [
            "Speed",
            "1 mph = 1.609 km/h",
            "60 mph = 96.6 km/h"
          ]
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why is temperature different from other units?",
            "a": "Because zero is not nothing. Length and mass have absolute zeros, so a single multiplier suffices. Celsius and Fahrenheit define zero differently, with the freezing point of water at 0 and 32, so a conversion needs both a multiplier and an intercept. Multiply 25 by 1.8 without adding 32 and you get 45 instead of the correct 77 °F."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are US and imperial gallons the same?",
            "a": "No, and the gap is significant. A US gallon is about 3.785 litres while an imperial gallon is about 4.546, roughly 20% more. The same applies to fluid ounces: about 29.57 ml in the US and 28.41 ml in the UK. Always check which system your source uses, because swapping an American recipe into British measures will be visibly off."
          },
          {
            "q": "How many decimal places should I keep?",
            "a": "This tool keeps enough significant digits for you to round as you like. For everyday use, one decimal is plenty: height to 0.1 cm, weight to 0.1 kg. In engineering or scientific work, match the significant figures of your input data, and do not let an exact defined constant such as 2.54 become the thing that limits your precision."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is an ounce a weight or a volume?",
            "a": "It can be either, depending on context. The avoirdupois ounce is a mass of about 28.35 grams. The fluid ounce is a volume of about 29.57 ml in the US and 28.41 ml in the UK. Recipes usually mean mass when they write oz, while drink labels mean volume, so read the abbreviation carefully."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "set-calculator": {
      "name": "Set Calculator",
      "description": "Perform set operations: union, intersection, difference, symmetric difference, cartesian product and set relations.",
      "metaDescription": "Free online set calculator for union, intersection, difference, symmetric difference, cartesian product and subset checks.",
      "setA": "Set A",
      "setB": "Set B",
      "operation": "Operation",
      "hint": "Enter elements separated by commas (e.g. 1, 2, 3).",
      "cardinality": "Cardinality",
      "resultSet": "Result set",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter a set of A and B elements, separated by commas.",
          "Select the operation: combined, intersecting, differential or symmetrical.",
          "View the results of the collection to list the members after they are heavy."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Collapse Operations",
        "formula": "A-B, a-B, a-B, a-B, symmetry (A-B) ∪ (B-A)",
        "explanation": [
          "The aggregation is a collection of different elements; the aggregation combines or compares the collections without double counting.",
          "All reservations are kept together, only the same items are retained at the intersection, and the different reservations belong to one but not the other.",
          "A-B is \"in A but not in B\" and B-A is \"in B but not in A\", which is not the same. Using the example of the above table, A -B = {1, 2}, while B -A = {4, 5}. Adjudication techniques: First look at the assembly in front of the minus sign and remove from it the elements in the back.",
          "The principle of rebuke is the most important practical formula for aggregate calculations: |A =B= = |B+ − B−. Direct additions count the intersections twice, so they are reduced once. In the previous example, =A = 3, =B = 3, =A ∩ B = 1, so |A ∪B = 3 + 3 = 1 = 5, which corresponds to the actual {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. This rationale, which can be extended to three or more clusters, is the basic tool for probabilistic and counting questions."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why is there no repetition?",
            "a": "Each element will appear at most once by definition and will be merged."
          },
          {
            "q": "What's a symmetry gap?",
            "a": "It happens to be an element of one of the two pools, excluding shared members."
          },
          {
            "q": "Are you in order?",
            "a": "No, {1,2, 3} equals {3,2,}; only concerned about membership."
          },
          {
            "q": "Can the elements in the collection repeat?",
            "a": "No, it's the core difference between a collection and a `list/multiset'. Each element of the assembly can only appear once, and {1, 1, 2} and {1, 2} are the same. Also ** There is no order**, {1, 2, 3} and {3, 2,} exactly the same. If the business needs to retain the number or order of repetitions, multisets (multisets) or lists/arrays should be used. The DISTINCT operation in the database is essentially about weighting the results as a grouping process."
          },
          {
            "q": "How does a pool of operations work in programming?",
            "a": "Most languages have built-in collection types: Python uses set (support for & cross-section, t-coding, − differential, ^ symmetry), JavaScript has Set (need to run or expand) and SQL uses UNION/INTERSECT/EXCEPT. The bottom of the collection is usually a Hashi table, so the average complexity of finding and inserting is O (1), weighting and member judgement are highly efficient. When dealing with tasks such as \"discovering the difference between the two lists\", the collection is much faster than the embedded loop."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Basic calculations of the collection",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Operations",
          "Symbol",
          "Meaning",
          "Example (A = {1, 2, 3}, B = {3, 4, 5})"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "Group",
            "A ∪ B",
            "All elements belonging to A or B",
            "{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}"
          ],
          [
            "Intersection",
            "A ∩ B",
            "Elements of both A and B",
            "{3}"
          ],
          [
            "Gap",
            "A − B",
            "Elements belonging to A but not B",
            "{1, 2}"
          ],
          [
            "Complement",
            "A′",
            "All elements that do not belong to A",
            "Depends on the full set definition"
          ],
          [
            "Symmetry difference",
            "A △ B",
            "It's just one of the elements.",
            "{1, 2, 4, 5}"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "programmer-calculator": {
      "name": "Programmer Calculator",
      "description": "Convert between decimal, hexadecimal, octal and binary and apply bitwise and shift operations.",
      "metaDescription": "Free online programmer calculator: base conversion (dec/hex/oct/bin) with bitwise AND/OR/XOR and shifts.",
      "valueA": "Value A",
      "valueB": "Value B",
      "bitwise": "Bitwise operation",
      "shift": "Shift",
      "none": "None",
      "left": "Left (<<)",
      "right": "Right (>>)",
      "reprA": "A representations",
      "reprB": "B representations",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the value (two, ten, sixteen or eight) below the current evolution.",
          "Switches to the target digit that you want to see.",
          "View the value after conversion and, if necessary, the by-bit calculation (AND, OR, XOR, migration)."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Programr Calculator",
        "formula": "Bitwork: a AND b to keep both 1 bits; a XOR b to keep exactly 1 bits",
        "explanation": [
          "The programmer calculator works between binary, octadecimal, decimal and hexadecimal and exposes the bit logic used in the bottom code.",
          "Bits and/or bits or transpositions are the basis for tagging, masking and embedded controls.",
          "Bitwork directly handles binary bits, which are extremely fast and are used extensively in system programming, permission control, graphic processing. Some of the most practical techniques: ** to judge the eccentricity of n & 1** (quicker than n %2); ** to multiply the ecliptic shift** (n < 3 = n × 8, n > > 2 = n ÷ 4 rounded); ** to exchange two numbers without a temporary variable** (a ^ = b; b ^ = a; a ^ = b); ** to eliminate the lowest 1** (n & (n-1) to match the number of 1 in the repertoipherical binary, which is the high frequency of interviews).",
          "The permission system prefers a bit sign: read = 1, write = 2, execute = 4, so the \"read & write\" permission is 1 |2 = 3; determine if write permission is available for perm & 2; revoke write permission for perm & ~2. Unix file permission 755 is a combination of three groups (owner rwx=7, group rx=5, other rx=5). ** The advantage of a bit of storage sign is that any combination requires only one integer** and storage and transmission is saved."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "What's the point?",
            "a": "It hides the value and keeps only the bit in the mask, which is also 1."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why move instead of multiplying?",
            "a": "Move n bit left equals 2^n, often faster in the hardware layer."
          },
          {
            "q": "What's a patch?",
            "a": "A symbol integer is the standard way to indicate a negative number: a bit inverse."
          },
          {
            "q": "What do you want to notice when you move left?",
            "a": "The left moves the height and may spill a variable (number of symbols). Shift right in two ways: the right of arithmetic (reserved sign bit, negative number added 1, Java/C > ) and the right of logic (filled 0, Java > >, unsigned number of C). The right shift of negative numbers must be especially careful. The other digit cannot equal or exceed the width of the data, otherwise the behaviour is not defined."
          },
          {
            "q": "Why ~12 equals 13 instead of 3?",
            "a": "Because it's a patch. 12 is 00001100, reverse 11110011, which corresponds to the number of symbols -13 (certification: 11110011 against 00001100, plus 1 plus 000011011 = 13, so the original value is -13). The result depends on whether there are symbols or not, and the same bit mode explains different results."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Bitwork True Table and Usual Techniques",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Operations",
          "Symbol",
          "Example:",
          "Result",
          "Usual uses"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "By Bit and",
            "&",
            "12 & 10",
            "8",
            "Extract a given location/ mask"
          ],
          [
            "By Bit or",
            "|",
            "12 | 10",
            "14",
            "Place (open sign)"
          ],
          [
            "Bitwise or differently",
            "^",
            "12 ^ 10",
            "6",
            "Flip / Swap Variable"
          ],
          [
            "Against",
            "~",
            "~12",
            "-13",
            "Bit Invert (compensation)"
          ],
          [
            "Shift left",
            "<<",
            "3 << 2",
            "12",
            "Multiply 2n"
          ],
          [
            "Shift right",
            ">>",
            "12 >> 2",
            "3",
            "divided by 2n"
          ],
          [
            "I'm judging the doll.",
            "& 1",
            "7 & 1",
            "1",
            "Result is 1 or odd"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    "debt-snowball-avalanche": {
      "name": "Debt Payoff Planner",
      "description": "Compare the snowball and avalanche debt payoff strategies and see which clears your debt faster.",
      "metaDescription": "Free debt payoff planner comparing snowball vs avalanche methods with total interest and payoff order.",
      "debts": "Debts",
      "hint": "One debt per line: Name, Balance, Annual Rate %, Minimum Payment.",
      "extra": "Extra monthly payment",
      "snowball": "Snowball (smallest balance first)",
      "avalanche": "Avalanche (highest rate first)",
      "months": "Months",
      "totalInterest": "Total interest",
      "totalPaid": "Total paid",
      "order": "Payoff order",
      "savings": "Avalanche saves {months} months and {interest} in interest versus snowball.",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "List each debt with its current balance, minimum payment, and interest rate.",
          "Choose a strategy: snowball (pay smallest balance first) or avalanche (pay highest interest first).",
          "Enter any extra monthly amount you can add above the minimums, then review the estimated payoff date and total interest."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "Snowball vs avalanche payoff strategy",
        "formula": "Monthly surplus = extra_payment + minimums_of_paid_off_debts",
        "explanation": [
          "The snowball method gives quick wins by clearing the smallest debt first, which can help maintain motivation.",
          "The avalanche method minimizes total interest by attacking the highest-rate debt first, saving money mathematically.",
          "Both methods use the same core idea: once a debt is gone, redirect its payment to the next target debt."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Which method saves more money?",
            "a": "Avalanche usually saves more interest overall, but snowball may be easier to stick with if motivation is a challenge."
          },
          {
            "q": "Should I stop saving while paying off debt?",
            "a": "Keep a small emergency fund first; then direct extra cash toward high-interest debt before long-term investing."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does this calculator include fees or penalties?",
            "a": "No; it uses balance, rate, and payment only. Check your loan terms for prepayment penalties."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "income-tax": {
      "name": "Income Tax Calculator",
      "description": "Calculate progressive income tax with presets or your own custom tax brackets.",
      "metaDescription": "Free progressive income tax calculator with US, UK and custom bracket support.",
      "system": "Tax system",
      "income": "Taxable income",
      "brackets": "Custom brackets",
      "hint": "One bracket per line: upper limit, rate%. Use 0 as the top limit for an open-ended bracket.",
      "tax": "Tax owed",
      "effectiveRate": "Effective rate",
      "marginalRate": "Marginal rate",
      "net": "Net income",
      "breakdown": "Per-bracket breakdown",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Pick the region's tax brackets or a simplified schedule (e.g. US federal, etc.).",
          "Enter gross income and deductible items (social insurance, itemized, etc.).",
          "See tax due, effective rate, and take-home pay."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "How income tax works: progressive brackets",
        "formula": "tax = Σ(bracket income × bracket rate) − quick deduction",
        "explanation": [
          "Most countries use progressive rates: income is split into brackets, lower parts taxed low and higher parts higher—not all at the top rate.",
          "The gap between gross and net comes from three sources: mandatory insurance, allowances/standard deduction, and the progressive tax itself. Using deductions fully lowers the base legally.",
          "This tool uses simplified brackets for quick planning—great for yearly estimates and 'how much more I keep after a raise'. For filing, follow local tax law."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why isn't all income taxed at the top rate?",
            "a": "Progressive tax only applies the higher rate to the portion above each threshold; lower portions stay low, so effective rate is below the top."
          },
          {
            "q": "What do itemized deductions do?",
            "a": "They directly reduce taxable income, lowering tax. Mortgage interest, donations, insurance—using them fully saves legally."
          },
          {
            "q": "Is this estimate accurate?",
            "a": "It uses simplified brackets for speed. Real filing involves credits, capital gains, and special rules—consult local tax authority."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "gcd-lcm": {
      "name": "GCD & LCM",
      "description": "Calculate GCD and LCM of multiple numbers",
      "metaDescription": "Calculate both greatest common divisor and least common multiple of two or more numbers",
      "guide": {
        "steps": [
          "Enter the integer of two values.",
          "The running calculator returns the maximum number of conventions and the minimum common multiple at the same time.",
          "View two results and confirm that a x b = gcd x lcm is satisfied."
        ],
        "explanationTitle": "The maximum number of conventions together with the minimum common number Yeah.",
        "formula": "gcd(a,b) × lcm(a,b) = |a × b|",
        "explanation": [
          "When the two are calculated at a time, many points are to be matched by the maximum number of Convention points and the minimum common number of times.",
          "The constant equation gcd x lcm = |axb| is the quick way to verify that both are consistent.",
          "gcd: divide the balance by a larger number divided by a smaller number, divide the remainder by a fraction until the remaining number is 0, and the last non-zero balance is gcd; when gcd is acquired, the constant equation gcd x lcm = |axb| is launched with lcm without having to do a full mass factor breakdown.",
          "Practical applications are common: 12/18 fractions divided into about 2/3 with gcd = 6; two things are repeated every 12 days with lcm = 12. This \"approximate + alignment\" pattern is used in schedule, gear ratio, music festival analysis."
        ],
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Why do we count together?",
            "a": "The approximate points require the maximum number of conventions; the average points require the minimum common number."
          },
          {
            "q": "Does the constant equation always work?",
            "a": "Two positive integers; two applications for more than two."
          },
          {
            "q": "What if two?",
            "a": "The maximum number of conventions and the minimum common number are equal to that number per se."
          },
          {
            "q": "What about negative or zero?",
            "a": "The number of conventions and the number of commons is usually defined as a non-negative integer; zero is taken as an absolute value, and the gcd of the positive is equal to that positive, lcm (0, a) = 0."
          },
          {
            "q": "What about three or more?",
            "a": "Two pushes: gcd(a,b,c) = gcd(gcd(a,b), c), lcm the same; note that the constant gcd x lcm = |axb| is valid only for two digits."
          }
        ],
        "tableTitle": "Example value pair: gcd x lcm = |a x b|",
        "tableHeaders": [
          "Values vs. a, b",
          "Maximum number of conventions gcd",
          "Minimal male factor lcm",
          "Validate a x b = gcd x lcm"
        ],
        "tableRows": [
          [
            "12, 18",
            "6",
            "36",
            "216 = 216"
          ],
          [
            "15, 25",
            "5",
            "75",
            "375 = 375"
          ],
          [
            "8, 9",
            "1",
            "72",
            "72 = 72"
          ],
          [
            "24, 36",
            "12",
            "72",
            "864 = 864"
          ],
          [
            "100, 75",
            "25",
            "300",
            "7500 = 7500"
          ],
          [
            "7, 13",
            "1",
            "91",
            "91 = 91"
          ]
        ]
      }
    }
  },
