  "blog": {
    "title": "Articles & Guides",
    "subtitle": "Practical explainers on money, health, and everyday math, paired with our free calculators.",
    "readMore": "Read article",
    "publishedOn": "Published",
    "minRead": "min read",
    "backToBlog": "Back to all articles",
    "all": "All",
    "category": {
      "finance": "Finance",
      "health": "Health",
      "math": "Math",
      "cooking": "Cooking"
    },
    "relatedTitle": "Related articles",
    "heroBadge": "Practical guides & calculators",
    "posts": {
      "50-30-20-budget": {
        "title": "The 50/30/20 Budget: A Simple Plan That Actually Sticks",
        "excerpt": "A budget only works if you can keep it up. The 50/30/20 rule is popular because it is simple: split your after-tax income into needs, wants, and savings.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Most budgets fail not because they are wrong, but because they are exhausting. The 50/30/20 rule works because it asks for awareness, not perfection. You divide your take-home pay into three buckets and adjust from there."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The three buckets"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Needs (50%): rent or mortgage, groceries, utilities, insurance, minimum debt payments.",
              "Wants (30%): dining out, streaming, hobbies, travel, the things that make life feel good.",
              "Savings (20%): emergency fund, retirement, and any extra debt payments."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Start by calculating your after-tax income. If you are not sure what lands in your account after deductions, our [Income Tax Calculator](/finance/income-tax) can estimate your net pay first."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why it works"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The rule forces the savings habit before spending, not with whatever is left over. Even a steady 20% rate builds a real cushion surprisingly fast, and the structure makes overspending obvious the moment a bucket overflows."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Automate the 20% transfer on payday so saving happens before you can spend it. Out of sight is the whole point."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When to adjust"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In high-cost cities the split might need to be 60/20/20 for a while, and that is fine. The point is direction, not a rigid number. Use our [Savings Goal Calculator](/finance/savings-goal) to see how your 20% compounds over time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "compound-interest-early": {
        "title": "Compound Interest: Why Starting Early Beats Saving More Later",
        "excerpt": "Albert Einstein supposedly called compound interest the eighth wonder of the world. Whether or not he said it, the math is real: your money earns returns, and then those returns earn returns.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Compounding is the quiet engine behind nearly every long-term financial goal. You invest money, it grows, and the growth itself starts earning more. The longer the runway, the more dramatic the effect."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The snowball effect"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Run the numbers in our [Compound Interest Calculator](/finance/compound-interest): put 200 per month at 7% for 30 years and you will see that most of the final balance came from growth, not from your contributions."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Time beats amount"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Investing 100 a month from age 25 often beats investing 300 a month from age 40. The early money compounds for an extra 15 years, and those years do the heavy lifting. This is why procrastination is the most expensive habit in personal finance."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The single biggest factor in compounding is starting time. You cannot recover lost years, no matter how much you add later."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Make it automatic"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Pair compounding with consistency using a [Savings Goal Calculator](/finance/savings-goal). Set the target, set the date, and let regular contributions do the rest."
          }
        ]
      },
      "bmi-body-fat-explained": {
        "title": "BMI, Body Fat, and What Your Weight Number Really Means",
        "excerpt": "Step on a scale and the number feels like a verdict. But weight alone hides more than it reveals. BMI is a starting point, not a finish line.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "We tend to treat a single weight number as the whole story. It is not. Two people can weigh the same and have completely different bodies. The useful question is not just how much you weigh, but what that weight is made of."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What BMI actually measures"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Body Mass Index is weight divided by height squared. It groups you as underweight, normal, overweight, or obese. It is a cheap, fast screen, and you can find your number with the [BMI Calculator](/health/bmi)."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Where BMI falls short"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Muscle weighs more than fat. A trained athlete can read as overweight while having very low body fat. That is why [Body Fat Calculator](/health/body-fat) and lean-mass estimates give a fuller picture than BMI alone."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Use BMI as a screen, not a diagnosis. Waist size and body composition usually tell the more honest story."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "A healthier lens"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Instead of fixating on the scale, combine your [BMR Calculator](/health/bmr-calculator) and [TDEE Calculator](/health/tdee-calculator) to understand your energy needs. Weight is a lagging indicator; energy balance is the thing you can actually steer."
          }
        ]
      },
      "extra-mortgage-payments": {
        "title": "How Extra Mortgage Payments Save You Years of Interest",
        "excerpt": "A 30-year mortgage is mostly interest in the early years. Small extra payments can shave years, and thousands of dollars, off the loan.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The first few years of a mortgage can feel discouraging: a large payment, but the balance barely moves. That is because early payments go mostly to interest. The good news is that extra payments hit the principal hardest exactly when it matters most."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How amortization works"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Amortization front-loads interest. Our [Mortgage Calculator](/finance/mortgage) shows the split between principal and interest for every month, so you can see how slowly the balance falls at first."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The power of a small extra amount"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "On a typical loan, an extra 100 per month can cut four to six years off the term and save tens of thousands in interest. The sooner you start, the bigger the effect."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Always tell your lender the extra payment should go to principal, not toward future scheduled payments."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Compare the trade-off"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Before throwing everything at the mortgage, weigh it against other goals using the [ROI Calculator](/finance/roi) and [Savings Goal Calculator](/finance/savings-goal). Sometimes a higher-return investment beats paying the loan early."
          }
        ]
      },
      "percentages-in-everyday-life": {
        "title": "Percentages in Real Life: Discounts, Tips, and Tax Made Easy",
        "excerpt": "Percentages run the everyday math of money, from sales and tips to tax and interest. Once the pattern clicks, you can do most of it in your head.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Percent means per hundred. Anchor every percentage problem to 100 and most of them stop being intimidating. The same idea powers discounts, tips, tax, and growth."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Discounts"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A 30% discount on an 80 item saves 24, leaving 56. The shortcut is price times (1 minus the rate). Our [Discount Calculator](/math/discount) does it instantly, but the mental version is handy at the register."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Tips"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "An 18% tip on a 50 bill is 9. The [Tip Calculator](/math/tip) handles groups and rounding, but here is the trick: 10% is easy to find, and 20% is simply double that."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Tax and growth"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The same percentage logic describes interest and inflation. See how a rate compounds over time with the [Percentage Calculator](/math/percentage-calculator)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Percent literally means per hundred. Anchor every percentage problem to 100 and it gets simple fast."
          }
        ]
      },
      "calories-bmr-tdee": {
        "title": "Calories, BMR, and TDEE: Planning a Diet That Actually Works",
        "excerpt": "Eat less, move more is true but useless without numbers. Your calorie needs are personal, and two calculators explain why.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Generic diet advice ignores the fact that everyone burns a different amount of energy. The fix is to estimate your own numbers first, then build a plan around them."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "BMR: your resting burn"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Basal Metabolic Rate is the calories you burn at complete rest, just keeping you alive. Find yours with the [BMR Calculator](/health/bmr-calculator)."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "TDEE: your real daily need"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Total Daily Energy Expenditure adds your activity on top of BMR. The [TDEE Calculator](/health/tdee-calculator) turns that into a daily target you can actually eat to."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Losing or gaining weight"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A deficit of about 500 kcal per day is roughly one pound per week. Track your food with the [Calorie Calculator](/health/calorie-calculator) and your macros with the [Macro Calculator](/health/macro-calculator)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Crash diets drop water, not fat. Aim for a modest, sustainable deficit you can keep for months."
          }
        ]
      },
      "cooking-unit-conversions": {
        "title": "Cooking Unit Conversions: Cups, Grams, and Ounces Without the Guesswork",
        "excerpt": "A recipe calls for 200 g of flour but your cups are imperial. Converting cooking units should not ruin dinner.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Cooking across recipes from different countries means juggling cups, grams, ounces, and milliliters. The reliable move is to stop measuring by volume and start measuring by weight."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Weight beats volume"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A cup of flour can vary by 20% depending on how you scoop it. Our [Cooking Units Converter](/cooking/cooking-units) handles cups, grams, ounces, and milliliters so your results stay consistent."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Scaling recipes"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Doubling a recipe by hand is error-prone. The [Recipe Scaler](/cooking/recipe-scaler) adjusts every ingredient automatically, and the [Food Cost Calculator](/cooking/food-cost) prices it out if you are cooking for a crowd."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Flour and sugar are not interchangeable by volume. Always convert by weight when you bake."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Beyond the kitchen"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "For general measurements, the [Unit Converter](/math/unit-converter) covers length, temperature, and more when a recipe strays into non-cooking territory."
          }
        ]
      },
      "debt-snowball-vs-avalanche": {
        "title": "Debt Snowball vs. Avalanche: Which Payoff Method Wins?",
        "excerpt": "Two popular methods promise to crush debt. They differ on one question: do you pay by emotion, or by math?",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "If you carry several debts, the order you pay them off matters as much as the amount. The two best-known strategies attack that order very differently."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Snowball: motivation first"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "List debts smallest balance to largest. Pay the minimum on all, then throw every extra dollar at the smallest. Quick wins build momentum. Model the whole thing in our [Debt Snowball and Avalanche Calculator](/finance/debt-snowball-avalanche)."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Avalanche: math first"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Pay the highest interest rate first. You save the most money and finish fastest, but the early progress can feel slow because big balances linger."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Mathematically, avalanche wins. Behaviorally, snowball often sticks. Pick the one you will actually finish."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Do not forget the rate"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Before choosing, compare the cost of your debt to what you might earn investing with the [ROI Calculator](/finance/roi). Sometimes it pays to invest while making minimum payments."
          }
        ]
      },
      "equal-installment-vs-principal": {
        "title": "Equal Installment vs Equal Principal: Which Mortgage Saves More",
        "excerpt": "Same loan amount, same rate — yet the two repayment methods can differ by tens of thousands in total interest. Here's how to choose.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When you take out a mortgage, the bank usually asks you to pick between an equal-installment plan and an equal-principal plan. Monthly payments, total interest, and early-years burden all differ — yet many people sign without understanding the trade-off."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The core difference"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Equal installment: a fixed monthly payment. Early payments are mostly interest; later payments shift toward principal.",
              "Equal principal: a fixed monthly principal portion. Interest is charged on the remaining balance, so the payment shrinks every month."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Our [Loan Calculator](/finance/loan) makes it concrete: on a $1,000,000 loan over 30 years at 4%, equal installment costs about $4,774/month. Equal principal starts near $6,111/month and drops by roughly $11 each month."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How much interest is at stake"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In that same example, equal installment totals about $719,000 in interest while equal principal totals about $602,000 — a gap of roughly $117,000. The difference comes from how fast the balance shrinks: equal principal cuts the interest-bearing base sooner."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "So is equal principal always better?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Not necessarily. Its early payments run about 28% higher, which is real pressure for a household that just bought a home. If the extra monthly cost squeezes your lifestyle or pushes you toward credit-card debt, the interest you save isn't worth it."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A simple rule: if you can comfortably afford the higher early payments, choose equal principal. Otherwise take equal installment and use the savings to prepay the loan."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What if you plan to prepay?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "If you expect to clear the loan within 5 to 10 years, the gap between the two methods narrows sharply, because much of the interest never accrues. Focus instead on prepayment penalties and on shortening the term rather than lowering the monthly payment. Compare scenarios with the [Mortgage Calculator](/finance/mortgage)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "credit-card-minimum-payment-trap": {
        "title": "The Real Cost of Paying Only the Credit Card Minimum",
        "excerpt": "Paying the minimum feels manageable, but it's one of the most expensive ways to borrow. A few thousand in debt can take over a decade to clear.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When the statement arrives, the minimum is usually just 5% or 10% of the balance — it looks like a friendly buffer. In reality it's one of the card issuer's most profitable designs: the rest accrues daily interest, and the grace period usually disappears."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "A concrete example"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Say you owe $10,000 at 18% APR (about 0.05% per day) with a 10% minimum. You pay $1,000 the first month; the remaining $9,000 keeps accruing interest. Next month the minimum drops to $900, so you pay even less while interest keeps running."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Run it through the [Credit Card Calculator](/finance/credit-card): paying only the minimum, clearing that $10,000 can take more than 14 years and cost over $8,000 in interest."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why the minimum is so expensive"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "The grace period vanishes: if you don't pay in full, interest is charged from the posting date of each purchase, not after 20–50 interest-free days.",
              "Daily interest, monthly compounding: interest is added to the balance and then earns interest itself.",
              "The minimum keeps shrinking: as the balance falls, so does your payment, so principal barely moves."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Three cheaper alternatives"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Pay in full: the only option with zero interest, and it should be your default.",
              "Installment plans: they carry fees, but the rate is usually below revolving interest, with a fixed, predictable term.",
              "Balance transfer or a low-rate loan: for large balances, compare the annual cost of a [personal loan](/finance/personal-loan)."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The minimum should be an emergency stopgap, never a habit. Even $100 extra per month cuts years off the payoff."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to get out fast"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Stop adding new charges, then use the [Debt Snowball vs Avalanche Calculator](/finance/debt-snowball-avalanche) to set your order: avalanche (highest rate first) costs least overall, while snowball (smallest balance first) is easier to stick with."
          }
        ]
      },
      "emergency-fund-how-many-months": {
        "title": "How Many Months of Expenses Should Your Emergency Fund Cover?",
        "excerpt": "“Three to six months” is the standard advice, but that range hides the real question: your job stability and family structure decide the number.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Nearly every personal-finance book says to keep three to six months of expenses in reserve. The range is reasonable, but it glosses over the key issue: whether you need three months or six depends on your exposure to risk, not on an average."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What drives the number"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Income stability: tenured or salaried roles vary little; freelancing, commission-based sales, and entrepreneurship vary a lot.",
              "Job market: if your industry is hiring and you could re-employ quickly, you can hold less; if it's contracting, hold more.",
              "Household structure: two incomes with no children is lowest risk; single-income households with children or dependents are higher.",
              "Fixed obligations: the more you have in mortgage, car loans, and insurance that can't be paused, the bigger the buffer you need.",
              "Existing safety nets: health insurance, unemployment benefits, and private coverage absorb part of the risk."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "A workable rule of thumb"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "3 months: two incomes, no children, healthy industry, stable social insurance.",
              "6 months: single-income household, has a mortgage, average industry.",
              "9–12 months: freelancer, founder, single parent, weak industry, or income heavily dependent on commission."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to compute your number"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The fund covers essential spending, not your full lifestyle. Add rent or mortgage, food, utilities, transport, insurance, and minimum debt payments to get your monthly baseline, then multiply by your target months. Use the [Savings Goal Calculator](/finance/savings-goal) to work out the monthly deposit needed."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "An emergency fund is optimised for access, not return. Keep it in a savings account or money-market fund — not locked in a term deposit or volatile assets."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When to actually use it"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Only genuine surprises qualify: job loss, serious illness, essential major repairs. Planned expenses — a new car, a holiday, a renovation — should be saved for separately and never eat into the buffer. After you dip in, refilling it becomes priority one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "inflation-purchasing-power": {
        "title": "What Will Your $100 Buy in 10 Years? Inflation and Purchasing Power",
        "excerpt": "Inflation doesn't shrink your money — it shrinks what your money can buy. Understanding that erosion is lesson one in why you invest.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Cash in a drawer doesn't lose its number — but ten years later it buys less. That is inflation eroding purchasing power: the most overlooked yet most certain risk in personal finance."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to compute purchasing power"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The formula is simple: future purchasing power = present amount ÷ (1 + inflation rate) ^ years. At 3% inflation, $100 today has the buying power of about $74 in ten years, and about $55 in twenty."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The [Inflation Calculator](/finance/inflation) shows the erosion across different rates and horizons."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why this forces you to invest"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Savings-account rates usually sit below inflation, so real returns are negative.",
              "Even if your balance grows in nominal terms, if the return trails inflation your real purchasing power still falls.",
              "Judge an investment by its real return: nominal return minus inflation."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "3% inflation sounds mild, but it means purchasing power halves in roughly 24 years. Simple arithmetic — and rarely done."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "That said, fear of inflation is no reason to gamble blindly. Build your [emergency fund](/blog/emergency-fund-how-many-months) first, then put long-horizon money into assets with a chance of beating inflation, and model the outcome with the [Compound Interest Calculator](/finance/compound-interest)."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Your income needs to keep up too"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The income side is equally overlooked. If your salary hasn't moved in years, your real income has been shrinking. When weighing a job's long-term value, whether raises outpace inflation matters more than the current number."
          }
        ]
      },
      "apr-vs-apy-interest-rate-basics": {
        "title": "Nominal Rate, Real Rate, and EAR: Don't Let the Numbers Fool You",
        "excerpt": "Is “12% per year” the same as “1% per month”? Not once compounding enters the picture — and the gap can be costly.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Loan ads love “0.5% per month.” Deposit products love “4% annualized.” Both look reasonable, but if you don't understand how interest is compounded, you'll underestimate true cost and overestimate true return."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Three terms you must separate"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Nominal rate: the stated annual rate in the contract, ignoring compounding frequency.",
              "Periodic rate: the rate per compounding period — monthly rate = nominal annual rate ÷ 12.",
              "Effective annual rate (EAR/APY): the true annual cost or return once compounding is included."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How compounding frequency changes things"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A 12% nominal rate compounded annually gives an EAR of 12%. Compounded monthly, the periodic rate is 1% and the true annual cost is (1 + 1%) ^ 12 − 1 ≈ 12.68%. Daily compounding pushes it higher still."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The gap looks small, but on a large balance over decades it is real money. Always convert products to a common basis before comparing."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "One line to remember: compare loans by EAR and deposits by APY. A nominal rate is only directly comparable when compounding frequency matches."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Practical steps"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When you get a quote, ask three things: what is the nominal annual rate, how often does it compound, and are there fees? Many “low-rate” products hide their true cost in fees. Enter different parameters in the [Loan Calculator](/finance/loan) to compare monthly payments and total cost."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "For savings, use the [Compound Interest Calculator](/finance/compound-interest) to estimate actual returns at different compounding frequencies, so marketing language doesn't mislead you."
          }
        ]
      },
      "rent-vs-buy-decision": {
        "title": "Rent or Buy? Run the Numbers Before You Decide",
        "excerpt": "“Buying always beats renting” is a popular claim, but the real answer depends on the price-to-rent ratio, how long you'll stay, and your city.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Few financial decisions are as emotionally loaded as renting versus buying. Elders say renting is “paying your landlord's mortgage”; agents say “buy early, gain early.” But this question can be settled with numbers instead of slogans."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Two metrics that matter"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Price-to-rent ratio: home price ÷ annual rent. The lower, the better buying looks. Roughly, below 15 favors buying; above 25 favors renting.",
              "Holding period: how long you plan to stay in the city. Transaction costs — taxes, agent fees, renovation — need years to amortize."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The true cost of buying is more than the mortgage"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Many people compare only “mortgage vs rent,” which is incomplete. Buying also carries the opportunity cost of the down payment, maintenance fees, repairs, property taxes, and eventual selling costs. Renting costs the rent itself plus the opportunity cost of the deposit."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Use the [Mortgage Calculator](/finance/mortgage) for payments and total interest, then the [ROI Calculator](/finance/roi) to estimate what the down payment could earn if invested — and compare the two together."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When renting makes more sense"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "You'll stay in the city fewer than 3 to 5 years.",
              "Your job or income is unstable and you need flexibility.",
              "The local price-to-rent ratio is clearly high.",
              "You can't afford a down payment without draining your emergency fund."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Remember: buying is not an investment; it's consumption plus forced savings. It gives you housing and discipline, but very poor liquidity and high transaction costs. Expecting investment-grade returns usually disappoints."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "A middle path"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Renting first is no disgrace. Accumulate a down payment, learn the city and neighborhood, confirm job stability — better than rushing in under pressure. Once you're sure you'll put down roots, model it carefully with the [Mortgage Calculator](/finance/mortgage)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "bmi-limitations-better-metrics": {
        "title": "BMI Isn't Enough: Why You Also Need Body Fat and Waist",
        "excerpt": "BMI summarizes everyone with one height-weight formula, so it inevitably misjudges muscular people and older adults. Know its blind spots to use it well.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "BMI is a screening tool, not a diagnostic one. Its value is that it's cheap, fast, and works across large populations; its cost is that it is crude at the individual level."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Three main blind spots"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "It can't tell muscle from fat: two people at 80 kg — a bodybuilder and a sedentary office worker — can share the same BMI with completely different bodies.",
              "It ignores fat distribution: visceral fat around the abdomen carries far more risk than subcutaneous fat in the limbs, and BMI sees none of this.",
              "It overlooks age and sex: at the same BMI, older adults usually carry more fat than younger people, and women naturally carry more than men."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Two better additions"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "First, body fat percentage. It directly reflects how much of your body is fat, a far better health signal than weight. Estimate it with the [Body Fat Calculator](/health/body-fat). Home scales have error, but the long-term trend matters more than any single reading."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Second, waist circumference. It reflects abdominal fat and is a key warning sign for metabolic syndrome. Generally, risk rises above roughly 90 cm in men and 80 cm in women (China guidelines)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Treat BMI as a starting point, not a conclusion. If BMI says overweight but body fat and waist are fine, you probably just carry muscle. If BMI is normal but your waist is large, don't be complacent."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to use it properly"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Get an initial read from the [BMI Calculator](/health/bmi), then combine it with [body fat](/health/body-fat) and waist. Better still, watch the trend: whether these numbers rise or fall over six months says far more than one measurement."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "One caveat: every online calculator is an estimate. If your numbers look abnormal or you have a medical condition, rely on an in-person doctor and clinical testing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "bmr-tdee-how-many-calories": {
        "title": "BMR and TDEE: How Many Calories Should You Actually Eat?",
        "excerpt": "Fat loss comes down to a calorie deficit — but a deficit relative to what you actually burn. BMR and TDEE answer that question.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "There is no universal answer to “how much should I eat” — it depends on your body and your activity. BMR and TDEE are the two concepts that turn that question into numbers."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "BMR: the calories you burn lying still"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Basal metabolic rate is the minimum energy your body needs at complete rest, fasting, in a neutral-temperature environment. It accounts for 60–75% of daily burn. Estimate it with the [BMR Calculator](/health/bmr-calculator), which uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "TDEE: your true burn once activity counts"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Total daily energy expenditure multiplies BMR by an activity factor: about 1.2 sedentary, 1.375 light, 1.55 moderate, 1.725 intense. The [TDEE Calculator](/health/tdee-calculator) gives you the number directly."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Sedentary (desk job, little exercise): BMR × 1.2",
              "Light (training 1–3 days/week): BMR × 1.375",
              "Moderate (training 3–5 days/week): BMR × 1.55",
              "Intense (training 6–7 days/week): BMR × 1.725"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to plan your intake"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "To lose fat: subtract 300–500 kcal from TDEE daily, which theoretically yields about 0.3–0.5 kg per week. To build muscle: add 200–400 kcal above TDEE with adequate protein."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Don't cut intake below BMR for long periods. Severe restriction slows metabolism and breaks down muscle, making fat loss harder and rebound very likely."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Use the [Macro Calculator](/health/macro-calculator) to split protein, carbs, and fat — easier to sustain and healthier than chasing calories alone."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The calculator is a starting point"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Equations carry individual error, sometimes around 10%. The practical method: start with the estimate, run it for two weeks, then adjust based on the scale. If weight hasn't moved, trim another 100–200 kcal."
          }
        ]
      },
      "daily-water-intake-guide": {
        "title": "How Much Water Should You Drink? Calculate by Weight, Not by “8 Glasses”",
        "excerpt": "“Eight glasses a day” is catchy, but a 50 kg person and a 90 kg person clearly don't need the same. Weight and activity are better guides.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "“Drink eight glasses (about 2 liters) a day” is everywhere, but it's a rounded, rough guideline that ignores the variables that matter most: body weight, climate, and activity."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Calculate from body weight"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A common estimate: daily water in ml = body weight in kg × 30 to 35. A 60 kg adult needs roughly 1,800–2,100 ml per day. The [Water Intake Calculator](/health/water-intake) gives you a personalized number."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When you need more"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Exercise and sweat: add roughly 500–700 ml per hour of activity.",
              "Heat or humidity: sweat losses rise sharply, so intake should follow.",
              "High altitude or dry aircraft cabins: water is lost faster through breathing and skin.",
              "Fever, diarrhea, or vomiting: losses accelerate, so replace fluids and electrolytes promptly.",
              "Pregnancy and breastfeeding: needs rise clearly — confirm the amount with your doctor."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Water isn't only plain water"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Many assume only plain water counts. In reality food counts too — fruits and vegetables are often over 90% water — as do soup, milk, tea, and coffee. Coffee's mild diuretic effect offsets part of its fluid, but it doesn't cause a net loss."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The simplest adequacy check is urine color: pale yellow means well hydrated, dark yellow means drink more, and completely clear may mean too much."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "More isn't always better"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Water is not the more the better. Drinking very large amounts quickly can cause hyponatremia, which is rare but can be life-threatening. People with kidney failure or heart failure need doctor-set fluid limits and shouldn't apply a generic formula."
          }
        ]
      },
      "waist-circumference-health-risk": {
        "title": "Waist Circumference: A Better Risk Predictor Than Weight",
        "excerpt": "Where your fat sits matters more than how much you carry. Abdominal fat is directly tied to heart disease and diabetes, and waist is the easiest way to measure it.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Two people can have identical weight and BMI yet vastly different risk profiles — the difference is where the fat is stored. Visceral fat around the abdomen releases inflammatory signals and disrupts metabolism, making it far riskier than fat under the skin of the limbs."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to measure it correctly"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Timing: in the morning, fasting, after emptying your bladder, standing and breathing normally.",
              "Position: a common method is the midpoint between the lower rib margin and the top of the hip bone (usually navel level); the WHO suggests the midpoint between the lower rib and the iliac crest.",
              "Technique: the tape sits on the skin without compressing it; read at the end of a normal exhalation, to the nearest 0.1 cm.",
              "Cautions: don't suck in your stomach, and don't measure right after a large meal."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Reference thresholds"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Men: ≥ 90 cm indicates abdominal (central) obesity.",
              "Women: ≥ 80 cm indicates abdominal obesity.",
              "Above these levels, the risk of type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular disease rises markedly."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "These are commonly used thresholds; different countries and bodies vary slightly. Pair the reading with the [BMI Calculator](/health/bmi) — using both together gives a more reliable picture."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Waist measurement's advantage: no equipment, a few seconds, and it often predicts risk better than BMI."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What to do if your waist is high"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The good news is that visceral fat responds readily to lifestyle change. Cutting refined carbohydrates and added sugar, adding aerobic exercise, protecting sleep, and drinking less alcohol all shrink abdominal fat. Note that spot reduction is a myth — the body mobilizes fat overall, and the abdomen usually changes first."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Tracking with the [Body Fat Calculator](/health/body-fat) and waist-to-hip measures such as those in [body type](/health/body-type) is far more informative than watching the scale alone."
          }
        ]
      },
      "percentage-increase-decrease-guide": {
        "title": "Percent Increase and Decrease: Mind the Base",
        "excerpt": "Up 50% then down 50% doesn't bring you back to the start. Understanding the base-rate trap prevents a lot of mistakes in investing and shopping.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Percentages are among the most commonly miscalculated things in daily life, and the cause is almost always the same: using the wrong base."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The basic formulas"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Change = (new − original) ÷ original × 100%",
              "New = original × (1 + increase%)",
              "Original = new ÷ (1 + increase%)"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The second line is the crux: a percentage is always relative to the original value. Verify quickly with the [Percentage Calculator](/math/percentage-calculator)."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The classic trap: gains and losses aren't symmetric"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Say a stock goes from $100 to $150 — a 50% gain. It then falls from $150 back to $100. That drop is (100 − 150) ÷ 150 = −33.3%, not −50%. Same $50 move, but the base shifted from 100 to 150."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Up 50% then down 50% leaves you at 100 × 1.5 × 0.5 = 75 — a 25% loss. That asymmetry is the cost."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Another trap: percentage points vs percent"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "If a rate rises from 4% to 5%, you can say it rose “1 percentage point” or that it rose 25% — (5 − 4) ÷ 4. Both are correct but mean different things, and news coverage mixes them to exaggerate. Always check which one is meant."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Practical cases"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Discounts: 20% off means paying 80% of the original price.",
              "Tipping: a 15% tip on a $200 bill comes to 200 × 1.15 = $230.",
              "Tax-inclusive prices: at a 5% tax rate, pre-tax = tax-inclusive ÷ 1.05 — not multiplied by 0.95.",
              "Investment return: (sale price − purchase price + dividends) ÷ purchase price × 100%."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "For more tax and discount scenarios, use the [Discount Calculator](/finance/discount) and the [Percentage Calculator](/math/percentage-calculator)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "common-unit-conversion-mistakes": {
        "title": "Where Unit Conversions Most Often Go Wrong",
        "excerpt": "Feet and inches to centimeters? Pounds to kilos? How many gallons are there? Most conversion errors aren't arithmetic — they're the wrong definition.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Unit conversion looks like plain multiplication and division, but the vast majority of mistakes aren't arithmetic slips. They come from using the wrong factor or confusing units that share a name."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Trap 1: feet and inches aren't decimal"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "5 feet 8 inches is often misread as 5.8 feet. The correct approach is to divide the inches by 12 and add: 5 + 8 ÷ 12 = 5.667 feet, then multiply by 30.48 to get about 172.7 cm. A conversion tool avoids the manual arithmetic entirely."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Trap 2: there are three gallons"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "US gallon ≈ 3.785 L",
              "Imperial (UK) gallon ≈ 4.546 L",
              "US dry gallon ≈ 4.405 L"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Whenever you see “gallon,” establish whether it's US or imperial first — otherwise the error can reach 20%."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Trap 3: an ounce is both weight and volume"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A fluid ounce (fl oz) is volume: 1 US fl oz ≈ 29.57 mL. An avoirdupois ounce (oz) is weight: 1 oz ≈ 28.35 g. The numbers are close but the meanings differ, and they aren't interchangeable across substances."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Trap 4: Fahrenheit and Celsius have different zeros"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Temperature isn't a simple ratio conversion because the two scales have different zero points. The formula is °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9, and conversely °F = °C × 9/5 + 32. The usual error is multiplying without adding or subtracting 32."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "For any unfamiliar unit, confirm three things: which system it belongs to, whether it's weight or volume, and whether identically named variants exist. Those three checks prevent most conversion errors."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Practical advice"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Use a tool rather than mental math. Our unit converter covers length, weight, volume, and temperature, and cooking has a dedicated [cooking units converter](/cooking/cooking-units) better suited to real recipe quantities."
          }
        ]
      },
      "recipe-scaling-guide": {
        "title": "How to Scale a Recipe Down from 8 Servings to 3",
        "excerpt": "Ingredients scale linearly, but seasoning, spices, and baking time don't. Know the exceptions and your resized dish still tastes right.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "To cut a recipe from 8 servings to 3, most people just multiply every ingredient by 3/8. That works most of the time — but for some ingredients it's a disaster."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What scales linearly"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Main ingredients: meat, fish, vegetables, rice, and noodles scale directly.",
              "Liquids: water, stock, and milk scale directly.",
              "Sugar and oil: usually fine to scale proportionally."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Compute the base factor: target servings ÷ original servings. 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375, so multiply each item by 0.375. The [Recipe Scaler](/cooking/recipe-scaler) does the arithmetic for you."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What does not scale simply"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Salt and strong seasonings: taste isn't linear, so scale to about 70–80% first, then taste and adjust.",
              "Spices and chili: also non-linear — start low, since you can always add more later.",
              "Leaveners (baking powder, baking soda): they depend on pan geometry and chemical reactions; extreme scaling fails, so cook in batches.",
              "Wine and vinegar: their flavor is potent, so scale to 50–75% and then adjust."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A practical rule: scale everything by the factor, but start salt, chili, and spices at 70%, then taste before serving. Add at the end if needed — never overshoot at the start."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Timing and pan size"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "With a smaller portion, baking time usually shortens, but not proportionally — check at about two-thirds of the original time, then extend. Less liquid also evaporates faster, so braises reduce sooner."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Match the pan to the volume: the same amount overflows a pan that's too small and scorches in one that's too large. Both depth and diameter affect heat transfer."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "A unit-conversion shortcut"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "For awkward measures like “1/3 cup” or “2 tablespoons,” don't grind through the math. 1 cup = 16 tablespoons = 48 teaspoons: convert everything to the smallest unit, apply the factor, then convert back. The [cooking units converter](/cooking/cooking-units) handles this fastest."
          }
        ]
      },
      "cooking-measurement-standards": {
        "title": "How Much Does a Cup of Flour Weigh? Why Baking Needs a Scale",
        "excerpt": "One cook's cup of flour is 120 g, another's is 160 g. That uncertainty in volume measures is a classic cause of baking failures.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "If you've ever followed a recipe and produced a brick-like cake, the recipe probably wasn't wrong — the question is how much “one cup” really was."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Where volume measurement goes wrong"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "How you fill it: flour scooped straight from the bag is denser than flour sifted and leveled — often by more than 20%.",
              "Ingredient state: whether flour is sifted, sugar is packed, or butter is softened all change the volume.",
              "Different cups: a “cup” isn't universal — about 240 mL in the US, about 200 mL in Japan."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Reference conversions"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "All-purpose flour: 1 cup ≈ 120–130 g (sifted and leveled)",
              "Granulated sugar: 1 cup ≈ 200 g",
              "Butter: 1 cup ≈ 227 g; 1 tablespoon ≈ 14 g",
              "Milk/water: 1 cup ≈ 240 mL ≈ 240 g",
              "Honey/syrup: 1 cup ≈ 340 g (far denser)"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Treat these as approximations; brands and technique shift them. For precise conversion, use the [cooking units converter](/cooking/cooking-units)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Baking is chemistry, not improvisation. A 10% error in flour or liquid is enough to turn a tender cake dense."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why weighing is better"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A digital scale costs little and usually reads to 1 g. Weights are fully repeatable, unaffected by how you scoop, and let you share a recipe without ambiguity — which is why professional formulas are written in grams."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When cups are fine"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Not every dish needs weighing. Stir-fries, stews, and soups are forgiving: volume measures are plenty and adjusting by feel is more flexible. Reserve the scale for baking, sauces, and desserts where ratios matter."
          }
        ]
      },
      "ratio-and-proportion-practical": {
        "title": "Ratios and Proportions: From Mixing Drinks to Reading Maps",
        "excerpt": "How do you dilute a 1:4 concentrate? What does a 1:10000 map mean? Ratios are the most practical — and most misread — math in daily life.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A ratio expresses the relationship between two quantities. It's simple enough to be overlooked, yet misusing it has real consequences, from undrinkable cordial to a wrong dose."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Two common notations"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "a:b — a relationship between two amounts, e.g. concentrate:water = 1:4.",
              "a:b:c — a continued ratio across three or more amounts, e.g. concrete at 1:2:3."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The key point: 1:4 means 1 part concentrate to 4 parts water — a total of 5 parts, not 4. That is the most common mistake."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Everyday examples"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Mixing drinks: for 500 mL at 1:4, concentrate = 500 ÷ 5 × 1 = 100 mL and water = 400 mL.",
              "Map scale: 1:10000 means 1 cm on the map equals 10,000 cm — i.e. 100 m — on the ground.",
              "Aspect ratio: 16:9 means the width is 16/9 of the height.",
              "Diluting cleaner: 1:10 means 1 part product plus 10 parts water, giving 11 parts total."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A reliable check: add the parts of the ratio together. The total parts should match the final total quantity. If it doesn't, one term is wrong."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Converting ratios to percentages"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A ratio converts to a percentage: in a:b, a's share is a ÷ (a + b). With a male-to-female ratio of 3:2, men are 3/5 = 60%. Conversely, 60% can be written as 3:2 or 60:40."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "For complex cases, let the [Ratio Calculator](/math/ratio-calculator) handle the arithmetic, especially with three or more terms or when solving backwards."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "One thing people mix up"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A ratio is not the same as a rate. A ratio compares two quantities of the same kind and has no unit; a rate usually compares different kinds and carries a unit, like speed (km/h) or population density (people/km²). Confusing them produces dimensional errors."
          }
        ]
      },
      "how-much-house-can-you-afford": {
        "title": "How Much House Can You Afford? Three Numbers the Bank Won't Volunteer",
        "excerpt": "The amount a bank approves and the amount you can truly sustain are usually far apart. Three ratios tell you the safe number.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When a bank approves a mortgage it asks “can they repay?”, using income multiples and debt ratios. That's a ceiling, not the figure you should use. A sustainable home budget is something you calculate yourself."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Number one: the 28/36 rule"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Housing costs under 28% of monthly income: mortgage payment, HOA fees, property tax, insurance.",
              "Total debt under 36% of monthly income: add car loans, credit cards, consumer loans, and support obligations."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Example: with $30,000 monthly household income, housing is capped at $8,400 and total debt at $10,800. If you already pay $3,000 for a car, keep the mortgage under $7,800."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Number two: down payment plus hidden costs"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A down payment is typically 20–30%, but that's far from your total cash need. Also budget for transfer tax (about 1–3%), a maintenance reserve, agent fees, renovation, appliances, and at least six months of payments in reserve."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A common mistake is pouring every saving into the down payment, leaving nothing for renovation or emergencies. Keep at least 10–15% of the purchase price in cash beyond the down payment."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Number three: the stress test"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Before signing, recalculate the payment with rates 1–2 percentage points higher and see if you can still cope. If your budget only works at today's rate, it has no resistance to rate moves."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Compare payments with the [Mortgage Calculator](/finance/mortgage) across rates and terms, then model prepayment with the [Loan Calculator](/finance/loan)."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Don't forget holding costs"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "HOA or management fees: charged by area and a real long-term expense.",
              "Maintenance and renovation: homes need continuous investment as they age.",
              "Commuting: a cheaper suburb carries time and transport costs worth pricing in.",
              "Opportunity cost: what the down payment might have earned if invested."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The figure you reach after all of this is what you can genuinely afford. It usually sits 20–30% below what the bank would lend — and it's the budget you can live with comfortably."
          }
        ]
      },
      "protein-intake-how-much": {
        "title": "How Much Protein Do You Need? Calculate by Body Weight",
        "excerpt": "Sedentary people, lifters, and older adults need very different amounts. Multiplying body weight by a factor is the most practical estimate.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Protein requirements vary more between individuals than almost any other nutrient. A sedentary office worker and someone who lifts five days a week may differ by a factor of two."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Reference ranges by body weight"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Sedentary, no training: 0.8 g per kg — the floor for basic needs.",
              "Moderately active: 1.0–1.2 g per kg.",
              "Regular resistance training: 1.4–1.8 g per kg.",
              "Intense training or cutting: 1.8–2.2 g per kg."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A 70 kg person who lifts three times a week needs roughly 98–126 g per day. The [Protein Calculator](/health/protein-calculator) gives you the number directly."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why intake should rise during a cut"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In a calorie deficit the body tends to break down muscle for energy. Higher protein protects muscle, increases satiety, and raises the thermic effect of food — digesting protein itself burns more calories. That's the core reason to raise protein while cutting."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Spreading it across meals"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "How well protein is used depends partly on the dose per meal. Roughly 25–40 g per meal is considered optimal for stimulating muscle protein synthesis. Spreading 120 g across three meals beats eating 100 g at dinner."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Rather than obsess over total grams, make sure each meal contains a quality protein source: eggs, lean meat, fish, soy products, or dairy."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When to be careful"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "People with impaired kidney function must limit protein under medical supervision and can't apply a generic factor. Source quality matters too: prefer complete animal proteins or well-combined plant proteins."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The [Macro Calculator](/health/macro-calculator) splits protein, carbs, and fat in one step, which is simpler than calculating each separately."
          }
        ]
      },
      "average-mean-median-mode": {
        "title": "Averages Mislead: Choosing Between Mean, Median, and Mode",
        "excerpt": "Why does “average salary” always feel inflated? Because extreme values pull the mean. Pick the right statistic to see the data clearly.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Under any story about “average salary,” someone always says they feel averaged away. That feeling is often justified — the mean is extremely sensitive to outliers."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What the three statistics are"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Mean: the sum of all values divided by the count. The most common, but heavily influenced by extremes.",
              "Median: the middle value once everything is sorted. Unaffected by extremes.",
              "Mode: the most frequent value. Best for categorical data."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "A clear example"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A company has 10 employees: nine earn $8,000 a month and the CEO earns $92,000. The mean is (9 × 8,000 + 92,000) ÷ 10 = $16,400, which sounds comfortable. The median is $8,000 — and that's the reality for most people there."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Whenever you read “average income,” ask: mean or median? For heavily right-skewed data like income or house prices, the median is almost always the more representative figure."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Which to use when"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Mean: when the distribution is fairly even, with no extremes, and you want to use every value (e.g. average test scores).",
              "Median: when there are outliers or clear skew (income, house prices, price growth).",
              "Mode: for categorical data, such as the most popular color or the most common size."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Putting it into practice"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The [Average Calculator](/math/average-calculator) computes these quickly. For larger sets or to see the shape of the distribution, pair it with the [Standard Deviation Calculator](/math/standard-deviation-calculator) — the same mean with a different standard deviation can describe completely different situations."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Build one habit with every statistic you read: ask how it was calculated, who was sampled, and whether outliers exist. Those three questions defuse most misleading numbers."
          }
        ]
      },
      "car-loan-total-cost": {
        "title": "The True Cost of a Car Loan: Four Costs Beyond the Monthly Payment",
        "excerpt": "“Zero-interest financing” sounds like a bargain, but once you add fees, insurance, taxes, and depreciation, the real cost often exceeds expectations.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Car-loan advertising focuses almost entirely on the monthly payment — “just $80 a day.” But what decides affordability is total outlay, and the monthly payment is only part of it."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Four costs besides the monthly payment"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Down payment: usually 20–30% of the price, and the largest single cash outlay.",
              "Sales tax or registration tax: a one-off payment, often a meaningful share of the price.",
              "Insurance: priciest in the first year, easing later, but recurring every year.",
              "Arrangement or financing fees: charged by the lender, sometimes 2–3% of the loan."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The truth about “zero interest”"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Zero- or low-interest offers usually recover the cost in one of two ways: higher arrangement fees, or forfeiting the cash rebate — you typically can't have both. Sometimes taking “a big cash rebate plus a normal-rate loan” is cheaper overall."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The only reliable way to compare two offers is to compute total outlay: down payment + all monthly payments + fees + interest − cash rebate. Don't let the monthly figure lead you."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Compare with a calculator"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Enter both scenarios into the [Auto Loan Calculator](/finance/auto-loan) and compare total interest and total outlay. If a dealer gives you only the monthly figure and not the total, treat that as a warning sign."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Don't ignore depreciation"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A car is a depreciating asset. A new one typically loses 10–20% of its value the moment it leaves the lot, with the steepest drop in the first three years. Finance a fast-depreciating car over five years and you can easily owe more than it's worth."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Keep the term short to avoid mismatching the depreciation curve.",
              "Consider a one-to-three-year-old car that has passed the steepest depreciation.",
              "Don't set the down payment too low, or you risk negative equity."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Finally, confirm with the [Loan Calculator](/finance/loan) that the payment sits within your debt ceiling — see the 36% total-debt rule in [How Much House Can You Afford](/blog/how-much-house-can-you-afford)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "food-cost-per-serving": {
        "title": "Cost per Serving: Is Cooking at Home Really Cheaper?",
        "excerpt": "Cooking at home is usually cheaper than eating out, but the gap depends on how you count. Ingredients, seasonings, and energy all belong in the total.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "“Cooking at home saves money” is near-universal advice, but once you add seasonings, gas, electricity, and your time, the conclusion may surprise you. That doesn't mean cooking isn't worth it — it means the arithmetic deserves a look."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What a dish actually costs"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Main ingredients: meat, vegetables, staples — priced by the amount used, not the whole package.",
              "Seasonings: oil, salt, soy sauce, spices — small per use, but they add up over time.",
              "Energy: gas or electricity, usually a small amount per cooking session.",
              "Trim loss: peeling and trimming typically remove 10–30% of what you bought."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The [Food Cost Calculator](/cooking/food-cost) adds these up to show the true cost per serving."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A common mistake: counting only main ingredients and ignoring seasonings and energy. For a simple home-cooked dish, those can be 15–25% of the cost."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The bulk-buying trap"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Larger packs have a lower unit price, but consider two things: will you finish it before it spoils, and what does storage cost? Saving money on a 5 kg bag of rice doesn't offset throwing half of it away."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When cooking really is cheaper"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Cooking for several people: costs spread out, so more portions means better value.",
              "Using seasonal produce: out-of-season vegetables can cost two to three times as much.",
              "Dishes with simple seasoning: recipes built on expensive sauces and imported spices escalate fast.",
              "Reusing leftovers: a whole chicken across two meals beats buying breast alone."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The value of your time"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Strictly speaking, cooking time has a cost, though most people don't count it — for many, cooking is relaxation or family time. Whether to include it is a personal call. If your time is expensive, partly prepared ingredients such as pre-cut vegetables may cost more per kilo yet still be cheaper overall."
          }
        ]
      },
      "rounding-and-significant-figures": {
        "title": "Why Rounding Is Sometimes Wrong: Significant Figures and Error Accumulation",
        "excerpt": "Round at every step and the final result drifts. Understanding error accumulation and banker's rounding makes your calculations trustworthy.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Rounding is the first rule taught in school, yet in chained calculations or large datasets, mechanical rounding introduces systematic bias and accumulated error."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Problem one: don't round intermediate steps"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Compute 1.4 × 2.6 × 3.8, rounding to one decimal each step: 1.4 × 2.6 = 3.64 → 3.6, then 3.6 × 3.8 = 13.68 → 13.7. Computed directly, 1.4 × 2.6 × 3.8 = 13.832 → 13.8. Rounding mid-way shifted the result by 0.1."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A basic principle: round only at the final step. Keep at least one or two extra significant figures throughout."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Problem two: rounding has systematic bias"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Digits 1–4 round down and 6–9 round up, which looks fair, but the treatment of 5 gives one extra case to rounding up. Across large datasets that makes results systematically too high."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Statistics more often uses “round half to even” (banker's rounding): below 5 rounds down, above 5 rounds up, and exactly 5 goes to the nearest even number. Over many values, positive and negative biases cancel out."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "2.5 → 2 (toward the even number)",
              "3.5 → 4 (toward the even number)",
              "1.24 → 1.2",
              "1.26 → 1.3"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What significant figures are"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Significant figures are the digits counted from the first non-zero digit, and they express the precision of a measurement or calculation. 0.0052 has two significant figures; 5.20 has three. A trailing zero isn't decorative — it says that zero was actually measured."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Rules of thumb: for addition and subtraction, match the fewest decimal places; for multiplication and division, match the fewest significant figures."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Using it day to day"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The [Rounding Calculator](/math/rounding-calculator) handles a variety of rounding needs. Be especially careful with financial data: currency calculations typically specify two decimals, and you should round the final result rather than every intermediate amount."
          }
        ]
      },
      "sleep-cycles-when-to-wake": {
        "title": "Sleep Cycles and Wake Times: Why 8 Hours Can Still Leave You Groggy",
        "excerpt": "Sleep runs in roughly 90-minute cycles. Waking from deep sleep leaves you foggy even after enough hours. Timing your wake-up helps.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Sometimes eight hours leaves you groggy, while six and a half leaves you sharp. The difference is usually not total hours but which stage of the sleep cycle you wake from."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What a sleep cycle is"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Sleep isn't a uniform state; it cycles in roughly 90-minute blocks containing light sleep, deep sleep, and REM sleep. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night, REM the second. Being woken from deep sleep produces noticeable sleep inertia and grogginess."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to time your wake-up"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Since a cycle lasts about 90 minutes, aim for a total close to a whole number of cycles and wake at a cycle boundary. Targeting 7.5 hours (5 cycles) or 6 hours (4 cycles) usually leaves you clearer than 8 hours."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "6 hours = 4 cycles",
              "7.5 hours = 5 cycles",
              "9 hours = 6 cycles"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Falling asleep usually takes another 10–20 minutes, so allow for that when counting back from your wake time. The [Sleep Calculator](/health/sleep-calculator) works out suitable bedtimes and wake times directly."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Cycle length varies between people; 90 minutes is an average. If 7.5 hours still leaves you tired, adjust by 15 minutes to find your own rhythm."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Matters more than duration"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Regularity: sleeping and waking at roughly the same times daily beats occasional catch-up sleep.",
              "Sleep pressure: enough waking time and physical activity during the day improves sleep quality.",
              "Light: natural light in the morning and dimmer light before bed stabilize your circadian rhythm.",
              "Alcohol: it speeds sleep onset but seriously disrupts sleep architecture in the second half of the night."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Don't over-trust the cycle"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Cycle timing is a useful guide, not a cure-all. If you regularly wake exhausted, feel sleepy by day, or snore heavily, that may point to something like sleep apnea, which needs a doctor rather than a schedule tweak. Cycle math fixes when you wake, not the quality of the sleep itself."
          }
        ]
      },
      "compound-interest-wealth-building": {
        "title": "How Compound Interest Makes Wealth Snowball: The Power of Starting Early",
        "excerpt": "Compound interest is often called the eighth wonder of the world. Understanding how it works—and seeing the numbers with a [compound interest calculator](/finance/compound-interest)—is the single most important step in long-term wealth building.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Compounding means each period's earnings are reinvested, so the next period's return is calculated on both your principal and the interest already accumulated. The longer the horizon, the steeper the curve."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why starting early matters so much"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Suppose you invest 1,000 per month at 8% annually. Starting at 25 versus 35 doesn't cost you 10 years of growth—it can cost you multiples, because the interest accumulated early keeps earning for decades."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The enemies of compounding are interruptions and fees. Frequent trading and high management fees quietly erode long-term returns. Rather than timing the market, start early and stay consistent."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Use the [compound interest calculator](/finance/compound-interest) to enter your monthly contribution, years, and expected return, and watch time and compounding work together."
          }
        ]
      },
      "currency-exchange-how-it-works": {
        "title": "How Exchange Rates Work: The Logic Behind Currency Conversion",
        "excerpt": "Why does 1 USD equal roughly 7 CNY? Exchange rates aren't set arbitrarily. Use a [currency calculator](/finance/currency) to see your actual net amount and avoid hidden fees.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "An exchange rate is essentially the relative price of two currencies, shaped by supply and demand, interest-rate gaps, inflation, and economic expectations."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Mid-market rate, cash rate, and fees"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The rate you see quoted is often not what you end up with. Banks and platforms add a spread and fees on top of the mid-market rate. Use a [currency calculator](/finance/currency) to compute the net amount before comparing channels."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "When spending abroad, dynamic currency conversion (DCC) is usually more expensive than settling in the local currency. On overseas cards, choose to be charged in the local currency."
          }
        ]
      },
      "mortgage-amortization-explained": {
        "title": "Understanding Mortgage Amortization: Where Your Interest Really Goes",
        "excerpt": "Of each monthly payment, how much is principal versus interest? Early on, you're mostly paying interest. Use a [mortgage calculator](/finance/mortgage) to see your repayment structure and judge whether early repayment pays off.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Amortization means the loan is repaid in periodic installments of a fixed amount, but the split between principal and interest shifts every period."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Interest first, principal later"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "With a fixed monthly payment, early payments are mostly interest and little principal. As the balance falls, the interest portion shrinks. Use a [mortgage calculator](/finance/mortgage) to see the yearly principal/interest breakdown."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "When rates are low, the savings from prepayment are limited; at high rates or with spare cash, prepaying can cut total interest substantially. Weigh it against your investment return."
          }
        ]
      },
      "roi-evaluating-investments": {
        "title": "ROI Isn't the Only Metric: How to Evaluate an Investment",
        "excerpt": "Don't get excited by '20% annual return' just yet. Use an [ROI calculator](/finance/roi) to find the real return, and factor in time, risk, and opportunity cost.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "ROI (return on investment) is (gain − cost) / cost. It's simple and intuitive, but it ignores the time your money is tied up."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Time makes the same ROI wildly different"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Earning 20% in one year is nothing like 20% over five years. Use an [ROI calculator](/finance/roi) to see the annualized figure and compare it with a risk-free return."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "A high ROI with high volatility or a long lock-up may not beat a steadier, lower return. Weigh it against your risk tolerance, not just the headline number."
          }
        ]
      },
      "calorie-deficit-weight-loss": {
        "title": "A Calorie Deficit Is the Only Rule for Weight Loss: How to Get It Right",
        "excerpt": "There's no magic to losing weight—only an energy gap. Use a [calorie calculator](/health/calorie-calculator) and a [BMR calculator](/health/bmr-calculator) to find your expenditure, then build a sensible deficit.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When intake stays below expenditure over time, the body taps its reserves and weight falls. That's a calorie deficit."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Don't make the deficit too large"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A daily gap of 300–500 kcal is usually sustainable and less likely to rebound. First use a [BMR calculator](/health/bmr-calculator) for resting burn, then add activity for total expenditure."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Extreme dieting lowers metabolism and sheds muscle. Use a [calorie calculator](/health/calorie-calculator) to set a gentle deficit, paired with protein and strength training for stability."
          }
        ]
      },
      "heart-rate-training-zones": {
        "title": "Heart Rate Training Zones: Train Smarter by Using the Right Intensity",
        "excerpt": "The same run serves different goals—fat burn versus cardio fitness need different intensities. Use a [target heart rate calculator](/health/target-heart-rate) to find your zones and give every session a purpose.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Heart rate zones layer training intensity, from recovery and fat burn to anaerobic, each tied to a different energy system and training effect."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "First find your maximum heart rate"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The common estimate is 220 − age, but individual variation is large. A [target heart rate calculator](/health/target-heart-rate) that splits zones by percentage is more reliable."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Training hard all the time leads to poor recovery and injury. Blend low-intensity cardio with intervals for steadier progress."
          }
        ]
      },
      "high-yield-savings-vs-regular": {
        "title": "High-Yield vs Regular Savings: Is the Switch Worth It?",
        "excerpt": "A high-yield savings account can earn 10-20x the interest of a traditional one. Here is how to decide whether moving your cash is worth the effort.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Traditional savings accounts at big brick-and-mortar banks often pay around 0.01% APY. High-yield savings accounts (HYSA), usually from online banks, commonly pay 4-5% APY in today's rate environment. On a $10,000 balance that difference is roughly $400-$500 a year for doing almost nothing."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Where the extra interest comes from"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Online banks have no branch network to maintain, so they pass the savings to you as higher rates. The deposits are still insured up to the usual limits, so safety is generally comparable."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When it is worth switching"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Your emergency fund is sitting in a 0.01% account - this is the clearest win.",
              "You have short-term savings (house down payment, wedding, tax bill) you will not touch for a year or more.",
              "You want a separate sinking-fund account that earns something while you wait."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Run your numbers in our [Savings Goal Calculator](/finance/savings-goal) to see how a higher rate changes your timeline, or use the [Compound Interest Calculator](/finance/compound-interest) to project the gap over several years."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The rate on a HYSA is variable - it can fall when the central bank cuts rates. Lock in certainty with a CD or ladder if you need a guaranteed return."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Switching takes an afternoon: open the account, link your old bank, move the cash, and set up direct deposit. The interest starts accruing immediately."
          }
        ]
      },
      "pay-yourself-first-system": {
        "title": "Pay Yourself First: The Automation Trick That Builds Wealth",
        "excerpt": "Saving what is left over rarely works. Pay yourself first flips the order - and automation makes it stick.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Most people save what remains after spending. The problem: there is rarely anything left. Pay-yourself-first reverses the flow: you move money to savings and investing the moment you are paid, then spend what is left."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Why the order matters"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When saving is the first line item, it competes with nothing. When it is the last, it loses to every impulse purchase and subscription that came before it."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to automate it"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Set up an automatic transfer to savings on payday - the same day, before you can spend.",
              "Route a second transfer to an investment or retirement account.",
              "Keep the fun account separate so you are not policing every purchase."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Start small if 20% feels impossible; even 5% compounds. Use our [Savings Goal Calculator](/finance/savings-goal) to see where a modest monthly transfer gets you, and the [Retirement Calculator](/finance/retirement) to project the long game."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Treat the transfer like a bill that must be paid - because your future self is a creditor you cannot default on."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Review the amount every six months as income rises. The goal is to raise the saving rate automatically, not just once."
          }
        ]
      },
      "vo2-max-cardio-fitness": {
        "title": "VO2 Max: The Number That Tracks Real Cardio Fitness",
        "excerpt": "VO2 max is one of the best predictors of cardiovascular health. Here is what it means and how to improve it without living at the gym.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "VO2 max measures the maximum amount of oxygen your body can use during intense exercise. Higher generally means a more efficient heart and lungs - and studies link it to lower risk of heart disease and early death."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How it is estimated"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Laboratory tests are most accurate, but wearables and simple field tests give a useful estimate. The key input is your heart rate during hard effort, which is why knowing your zones helps."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Training to raise it"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Add one or two interval sessions a week - short hard bursts with recovery.",
              "Keep most other cardio in the easy zone so you can stay consistent.",
              "Track progress with pace or heart-rate workouts rather than just duration."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Find your effort zones first with our [Target Heart Rate Calculator](/health/target-heart-rate), then measure improvement with the [Pace Calculator](/health/pace-calculator) on the same route over time."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "VO2 max declines with age, but training can slow - and partly reverse - the drop well into later decades."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Consistency beats intensity. A modest weekly habit sustained for a year beats a heroic month that you quit."
          }
        ]
      },
      "body-fat-vs-bmi": {
        "title": "Body Fat Percentage vs BMI: Which Should You Trust?",
        "excerpt": "BMI is quick but blind to muscle. Body fat percentage tells a richer story - here is when each one matters.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "BMI only uses height and weight, so a muscular athlete and a sedentary person with the same BMI can have very different health. Body fat percentage measures the actual proportion of fat, which is often what people really care about."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Strengths and blind spots"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "BMI: free, instant, great for population-level trends - useless for individuals with high muscle.",
              "Body fat %: reflects composition, but home methods vary in accuracy (calipers, scales, tapes).",
              "Waist circumference: a simple, powerful red-flag check for internal fat."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Start with our [BMI Calculator](/health/bmi) for the quick screen, then estimate composition with the [Body Fat Calculator](/health/body-fat) to see the fuller picture."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Two people can share a healthy BMI yet have opposite risk profiles. Combine metrics before drawing conclusions."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Whichever number you track, trend matters more than a single reading. Measure under the same conditions each time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "mental-percentage-tricks": {
        "title": "Mental Percentage Tricks You Can Do Without a Calculator",
        "excerpt": "Tips, discounts, and tax estimates are easier than they look. A few swaps let you compute percentages in your head.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "You rarely need exact precision for everyday percentages - you need a fast, good-enough number. A handful of swaps make that possible."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The tricks that actually stick"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "X% of Y = Y% of X. Need 8% of 50? That is 50% of 8 = 4. Instant.",
              "10% is just move the decimal. 15% = 10% + half of 10%.",
              "For a 20% discount, find 10% twice and subtract from the price."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When accuracy matters - splits, grades, error margins - let our [Percentage Calculator](/math/percentage-calculator) do it, or check a result with the [Percent Error Calculator](/math/percent-error-calculator)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Memorize X% of Y = Y% of X. It turns awkward numbers into easy ones more often than you would expect."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Practice on real life: restaurant bills, store markdowns, and subscription price hikes. The patterns become automatic fast."
          }
        ]
      },
      "grams-to-cups-conversion": {
        "title": "Grams to Cups: Why Weight Beats Volume in the Kitchen",
        "excerpt": "A cup of flour can vary by 30% depending on how you scoop. Weighing in grams is the fix - and it is easier than it sounds.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Volume measures like cups are inconsistent: the same cup of flour can weigh noticeably more or less based on packing and humidity. Grams remove that guesswork entirely."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "When to switch to grams"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Baking, where small errors compound into flat cakes or tough cookies.",
              "Scaling a recipe up or down - multiply the grams, no re-converting.",
              "Following recipes from another country that already use weight."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Convert any amount with our [Cooking Units Converter](/cooking/cooking-units), and when you are scaling a dish for more people, the [Recipe Scaler](/cooking/recipe-scaler) keeps every ingredient in proportion."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "If a recipe lists cups, weigh one cup once, write the gram number on the page, and never scoop-guess again."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A cheap kitchen scale pays for itself in saved disasters and consistent results week after week."
          }
        ]
      },
      "recipe-scaling-basics": {
        "title": "Formula scale-up base: four to two instead of a simple halving",
        "excerpt": "Linear scaling is feasible, but the temperature, fermentation and heating times are judged separately, otherwise the taste changes.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The formula was changed from a four-person to a two-person formulation, and the least cost-effective approach was to multiply the factor of 0.5, but the reality was far more complex than the multiplier. The staples, such as flour, water and meat, can be scaled in linear terms, with salt, sugar and spices being more subjective, and cutting them directly in half often light or salty; yeasts in baking are also co-ordinated with temperature, time, and not a pure proportion."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Which should be proportional and which should be adjusted separately"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Powders, liquid mains: strictly proportionally scaled",
              "Salt, spices, peppers: proportional, but in turn, taste.",
              "Fermented pasta: yeast and time need to be reassessed",
              "The capacity limit for the pan is one batch, it's too big to split."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The scaling is based on the determination of the baseline and the conversion of the base amount, which is followed by the idea of [proportional versus matching] (/ratio-and-scaling). If the original formula uses a spoon and you want to use a gram, go through it first. The problem of precision after scaling is the same as [rounding-significant-figures], so do not write an inoperable amount of 0.333 tea spoons."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The baking is extremely sensitive to the scale, with more than twice the scale suggested to recalculate the flour water ratio and try to produce a small batch at a time."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Non-linear between heat and time"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "A Tongan double, boiling and collecting time does not exactly double, and a deeper, slower heat can change the rhythm, depending on the state rather than on the dead. Similarly, it takes longer for the grill to become more fully fledged at the centre. With this in hand, a combination of [common conversion errors] (/common-conversion-mistakes) would reduce the number of overturns."
          }
        ]
      },
      "measuring-cups-spoons-standard": {
        "title": "Measurement of the cup: How many milligrams of a glass is it?",
        "excerpt": "An American-style glass of about 240 milligrams, but different countries have different cup sizes.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The scalding spoon looks simple and hides standard differences behind it. In the United States recipe, 1 cup equals about 240 ml, 1 spoon equals about 15 ml, 1 tea spoon equals about 5 ml; and the British, Japanese, Australian cups do not agree. The foreign formula was reproduced in the wrong cup, and the cumulative deviation was enough to make the bake fail."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Common Standard Contrast"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "1 cup = 240 ml, 1 tbsp = 15 ml, 1 tsp = 5 ml",
              "British 1 cup about 284 ml, unlike American",
              "Day 1 contract 180 ml for rice measurement",
              "Dryness and wetness are different scales."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The most prudent approach would be to convert all quantities into grams, as the weight is absolute and does not vary by standard. This is the value of [grams-to-cups]. In conversions, care is taken [unit-conversion-pitfalls] (/unit-conversion-pitfalls) — volume conversion depends on density, with a difference in weight between different foods and a cup. Matching logic may also refer to [formulation scaling base] (/recipe-scaling-basics)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Flour must be scraped when measured in cups, with a tipping glass almost 20% more than scratched, a common killer of cake collapses."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Dry-wet-separated"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Wet material (water, oil, milk) is read at the bottom of the dent of the surface with a transparent glass of mass; dry powder is covered with a spoon. The combination of the two criteria magnifies the error. Accurate control of costs can be combined with [how each cost is calculated] (/cost-per-service), while the list of errors is avoided by [common conversion errors] (/compon-conversion-mistakes)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "nutrition-conversion-guide": {
        "title": "Nutritional conversion guidelines: how to recalculate the calorie protein from birth to ripening Heavy",
        "excerpt": "Water loss becomes heavy after cooking the food, which overestimates the amount of heat per unit by well-recognised nutrients, then separates it and converts it.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The heat and proteins on the nutrition label are mostly given `per 100 grams', but the indication is often ignored whether it is reborn or sophisticated. When the chicken gravy is cooked and water is lost, 100 grams of raw meat may only have 70 grams of cooked meat, and if recalculated, the unit heat will be significantly overestimated. The correct approach would be to use the same life-recycling or to check the corresponding conversion factors."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Common conversion elements"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Meow noodles: Boiled to about 1:2.5 to 3, most stable by birth",
              "Meat: About 20 to 30% of the cooked water is lost and reborn.",
              "The oil's heat is high and the calculus effect is clear.",
              "Packed nutrition tables give priority to every 100 grams instead of every one."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Conversion of nutrition and [g and cups and how] (/grams-to-cups) to the `quality' benchmark, first converting volume to gram and then showing the most reliable. The total calorie control can be planned together with [how to calculate each cost] (/cost-per-serving). Macro-scales are set with reference [scales versus matching] (/ratio-and-scaling), while the accuracy of the figures reported is shown in [rounded and valid] (/rounding-significant-figures)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "It is important to distinguish between retrenchment periods, otherwise `the thought of eating 300 kilocalories' could actually be over 400, and the gap could be eaten quietly."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How to match food and homemade"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Extra-eat signs are more heavily heavy on the edible and cannot be directly added to home-made re-entry records and need to be harmonized to the same benchmark for comparison. It's more efficient to build a table of common food products, and to insist on it in the long term, than to double-check the APP. A summary of relevant errors is provided in [common conversion errors] (/common-conversion-mistakes)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "cost-per-serving": {
        "title": "How do you calculate each cost?",
        "excerpt": "The price of a bottle of oil is low in unit price but small in quantity, and it is often the staple food and the loss that really determines the price.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Counting the cost of a dish does not depend solely on the unit price, but on the `actual usage' of each food item multiplied by the corresponding unit price. For example, a bottle of soy sauce is cheap, but a dish uses only 10 ml at a very low cost of sharing; rather, meat and fresh vegetables are the main cost. Neglecting usage leads to an entirely erroneous conclusion."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Accounting steps"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "List all foods and quantities used, in a single g or ml",
              "Total package price ÷ unit price for total net content",
              "Unit price x actual usage = cost of the food",
              "= cost per item"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "This step depends on the precise [grams-to-cups] (/grams-to-cups) to turn the spoon into grams, otherwise the amount is incorrect. The ratio itself is derived from the [formulation scaling base] (/recipe-scaling-basics). If nutritional indications are involved, they can also be combined with [nutrient conversion-conversion-guide]. Two references to the accuracy of the numbers at the time of recording [rounded and valid figures] (/rounding-significant-figures) are sufficient."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "It should be borne in mind that, despite the small cost of cooking and energy (gas and electricity), the accumulation of long-term high-frequency cooking is worth taking into account the real cost."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Leverage to reduce costs"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Priority is given to the “high-utilized, high- unit-priced” staple foods: bulk purchasing, seasonal vegetables, and reduced wear and tear. This compares with a very low unit price return for a few milligrams of oil. Accounting is credible by circumventing [common conversion errors] (/compon-conversion-mistakes)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "grams-to-cups": {
        "title": "How do you translate a cup of flour and a cup of sugar?",
        "excerpt": "Volume conversion depends on density, same as a glass of flour, about 120 grams and sugar, about 200 grams, which must never be applied to the same number.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Turning the cup into a gram doesn't allow for an all-power coefficient, because the density of different foods is completely different. A glass of 240 ml in American: flour is loose about 120 g, white sugar about 200 g, water is just 240 g, butter about 227 g. If you have a wrong coefficient, the formula ratio is confused."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "It's about a glass of common food."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "General flour: about 120 g (shaving)",
              "White sugar: around 200 g",
              "Red sugar (pressure): about 220 g",
              "Water / Milk: around 240 g",
              "Butter: About 227 g"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "So it's better to use the gram in the baking than the cup, and it'll eliminate the density uncertainty. There are also country differences in the measure cups themselves, as detailed in [measuring-cups-spoons-standard]. The volume mass conversions in the [unit-conversion-pitfalls] by nature must be based on density. Zoom formulations with [formulate-scaling-basics] basis."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The same food is of different weights: flour is compacted and sugar bars make a glass heavier and the most secure."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "No approximation of scales"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In the absence of a kitchen scale for a temporary period, the crayons of common foods can be remembered, but only for scoffing that is not sensitive to accuracy; the baking must be weighed. Nutrient calculations are provided in the [nutrient-conversion-guide] (/nutrition-conversion-guide), cost-sharing in [how each cost is calculated] (/cost-per-service) and methods to avoid errors are aggregated in [common conversion errors] (/compon-conversion-mistakes)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "common-conversion-mistakes": {
        "title": "Common conversion errors: These pits turn over half the usual food.",
        "excerpt": "The three most high-frequency faults in the kitchen conversion are mixed standards, neglected density and static.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Miscalculation of kitchens is often not a miscalculation, but a mistake of premise. The three most typical categories: first, the mix of cups in different countries without notice; second, the thought that \"one glass\" weighs the same weight and ignores density; and third, the use of formula time as absolute truth, without a state-of-the-art adjustment. Three folds, and the finished product is naturally unstable."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "HF error list"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "The U.S. Cup mixed up with the British Cup.",
              "Flour piles are not even, they're too high.",
              "Salt sugar is converted by volume and misdensed.",
              "Liquid ounces mix with weight ounces.",
              "The time of the move doesn't look at the actual maturity."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Liquid ounce volume, constant ounce weight, both of which are highly misleading in the formulation and correspond to the dimensions of [unit-conversion-pitfalls] (/unit-conversion-pitfalls). The change to grams can circumvent most problems once and for all, see [grams-to-cups] (/grams-to-cups) and [measuring-cups-spoons-standards]. Scale and ratio reference [formulate-scaling-basics base]."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Buying a 10-dollar kitchen scale would solve more than 80 per cent of the conversions, which would be less than any comparison."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Create your own pit-shelter process"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "It is recommended that the three principles of \"full use of grams, fixed one cup standard, and state-of-the-art fire\" be fixed, and that the raw weight of common food be recorded in the phone. Nutrition and cost dimensions can be combined with [nutrient-conversion-guide] and [how each cost is calculated] (/cost-per-serving), and a review of numerical accuracy [. Rounded and valid [/rounding-significant-figures]."
          }
        ]
      },
      "compare-loan-interest-rates": {
        "title": "How does the mortgage rate compare: Equivalent principal, Equivalent principal and LPR? Organisation",
        "excerpt": "The comparison of interest rates on mortgages cannot be based on figures alone, and the repayment method determines the total interest on your actual expenditure.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Many compare the annual interest rate of the loan to that reported by the bank, while ignoring the decisive impact of repayment on total interest. It is also a loan with a fixed monthly repayment of the principal equivalent of one million dollars, with a yearly interest rate of 4.2 years and a term of 30 years, but the principal repayment of the prior period is small and the total interest is the highest; the principal equivalent is fixed and interest is declining, and total interest is lower but the previous period is under pressure. In order to see the differences, it is suggested that the monthly provision and total interest for both options be calculated using the [lending rate comparison] (/finance) approach."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The essence of LPR and fixed interest rates"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Currently, most mortgages are linked to LPR (interest rate on loan market quotations), which is re-pricing once a year, and interest rates decline and increase as the market goes down. If you judge that future interest rates will be lower as a whole, the floating rate will be more economical; if you seek absolute stability in the monthly supply and are unwilling to withstand fluctuations, you can choose a fixed rate. Note that once the weighting date and additional value are signed, they are usually fixed for a long period of time, and the details are clarified before the contract is signed."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Equivalent principal: a fixed monthly job suitable for a stable cash flow Group",
              "Equivalent principal: Total interest is lower and suitable for prior repayments People",
              "Floating interest rates: subject to LPR changes, may be more economical in the long term",
              "Fixed interest rate: month-specific, suitable for risk-averse borrowing People"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The interest rate is compared with the `nominal annual interest rate' and `real annualized cost (APR)', which includes hidden expenses such as fees, insurance, etc."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Three common traps for horizontal comparison"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "First, let's not be confused by the former N-year preferential rate, which is the standard rate to be restored after the preference expires. Second, early repayment of defaults, which vary widely from bank to bank, will have a direct impact on the flexibility of your subsequent optimization of liabilities, which can be referred to in the [advance repayment strategy] (/early-loan-repayment-strategy). Thirdly, while operating on a loan-for-housing loan appears to be at a lower interest rate, it involves a risk of compliance with the use of funds, and ordinary people should be cautious. The calculation of monthly contributions, total interest, and default money together is the only way to arrive at a real cost-saving solution."
          }
        ]
      },
      "early-loan-repayment-strategy": {
        "title": "Whether or not to pay in advance: calculate the opportunity cost before deciding",
        "excerpt": "Interest was saved on early repayments and other investment possibilities for the money were abandoned, depending on the difference between the two.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Do you want to pay the mortgage in advance? There is no standard answer to this question, and the core is to compare `the interest rate saved by early repayments' to `the return on the investment'. If the mortgage rate is 4:05, and you can only buy an annual savings or a bank account at 2:05, then early repayment is equivalent to a steady profit of two points, which is usually worth doing. On the other hand, if you are certain of the return on the investment above the mortgage rate, then the cash is retained for the better."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Shorter duration or less month"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Shortening the duration of the loan: Total interest has dropped most, suitable for debt-free as soon as possible People",
              "Reduction in monthly supply: reduced monthly pressure, with limited savings on total interest",
              "After partial early repayment, make sure you apply to the bank for a reduction instead of a default reduction.",
              "Give priority to the highest-interest-rate debt, the order is more important than the amount."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Bank default operations are often `reduced monthly supply', as this reduces the total interest and is more favourable to banks. You have to take the initiative to ask for a \"shorter term\" in order to really save interest. For the bottom logic of interest rate comparison, it is extended to read [how the mortgage rate compares] (/compare-loan-interest-rates). Advance repayments may also involve default payments, and the terms of the contract usually include a charge for early repayment within one to three years of the release, which is free of charge."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Retention of emergency funds for three to six months before early repayment, so as not to release all current deposits for repayment or otherwise be passive in case of unemployment or serious illness."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Under what circumstances?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "If you are enjoying very low historical interest rates (e.g. below three o'clock) and you have a channel of stable return above that, early repayment is not cost-effective. In addition, interest rates on Provident Fund loans are usually much lower than those on commercial loans, and priority should be given to repaying loans and retaining them. More basic planning lines can be found in [individual-tax-basics] and [establishing a contingency fund] (/build-emergency-fund)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "compound-interest-investing": {
        "title": "The power of the compound: 72 laws to estimate the time when wealth doubles",
        "excerpt": "It's not hysteria, but the law of 72 makes you count how many years it takes to double the principal under any annualized gain.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The compound interest means `interest is also interest', and the long-term insistence will result in an exponential increase. A simple estimation tool is rule 72: the number of years required to double the principal amount by dividing it by the percentage of annualized return. For example, the annualization rate is six percent, 72 percent, divided by six percent, equals 12 years, and eight percent is about nine years. This law is very practical in planning old-age and child education benefits."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Sets how to magnify the compound effect"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Fixed-term inputs, smoothing market fluctuations, low and high",
              "Time is the best friend of the compound. The sooner the better.",
              "Set the dividends and interest as automatic reinvestments, and don't let the proceeds lie on the books.",
              "For each point of reduction, the final amount will be significantly increased over the long term"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The negative side of the compound interest is \"negative co-benefits\": high fees, frequent trading and inflation erode the principal. Many ignore the rate and, in fact, one per cent of the annual management fee eats nearly a quarter of the final gain on a 30-year scale. To understand asset allocation in a systematic manner, reference can be made to the investment logic in [the Introduction to Retributive Investments] (/compund-interest-investing) and to avoid hidden costs of cross-border investments in combination with [currency-exchange-exchange-tips]."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The pitfall of compoundity is the \"linear intuition\" — the human brain is accustomed to linear thinking, which tends to seriously underestimate the exponential growth of more than 20 years."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Minimum action from today."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "We don't have to wait for enough money to invest. A fixed 10 per cent of income per month is invested in an index fund, which lasts for 20 years and is expected to complete most of your work. Returning to the overall framework of financial planning, it is proposed to read the [establishment of contingency fund] (/build-emergency-fund) to ensure that the basic security cushion is in place before increasing the proportion of investments."
          }
        ]
      },
      "build-emergency-fund": {
        "title": "Establishment of a contingency fund: a few months to secure expenditure",
        "excerpt": "The contingency fund is the foundation of financial security and the choice of amounts and target accounts is more important than you think.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The contingency fund is a cash reserve that covers contingencies such as unemployment, disease and unforeseen expenses. The generic proposal is to keep three to six times the cost of living: people with stable jobs and secondary jobs are offered a minimum of three to four months; and people with free professions or high income fluctuations should work for six months or more. The value of the money is not added, but rather that you need not sell your assets or borrow high interest."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Where should I put the money?"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Current or IMF: Any time is desirable, liquidity first",
              "Small amounts of innovative bank deposits: slightly higher returns, pay attention to foreclosure rules",
              "Don't put it in stocks or in long-term closed-off financial management.",
              "Opening a separate account to avoid mixing with daily consumption leading to being spent"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The Fund sought certainty and liquidity rather than a rate of return. Instead of trying to make more than a few percentage points, it should be ensured that it is available in one day. Once the security pads are in place, the remaining funds will be suitable for investment.[ Long-term value-added tools such as Retrieving Investment. If households have multiple sources of income, they can rebalance their savings and debt-servicing priorities by reference to [early-loan-repayment-strategy]."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The contingency fund was not recommended for early repayment of loans or ventured into investments until it reached its target; the building would be built without the foundation being filled, and an unexpected event would take its toll."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How long will it last?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Assuming a monthly expenditure of $10,000 and a target of $660,000 for six months, the target would have been achieved in about 30 months if 2,000 dollars had been saved each month. It is recommended that the automatic transfer of wages on the day of payment be set as the first expenditure, with priority over any consumption. More family financial bases are found in [personal-tax-basics] and [currency-exchange-related] (/currency-exchange-tips)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "currency-exchange-tips": {
        "title": "Currency exchange pits: exchange rates, fees and airport traps",
        "excerpt": "The same money is exchanged through different channels, and the difference in the amount to be paid may be several percentage points, and the key is to see hidden costs.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "When travelling abroad or cross-border remittances, the real cost is not just the exchange rate on the price of the card, but also the cost of handling, differentials and intermediary costs. The price difference between the bank's cash purchase price and the offer price, together with a fixed fee, makes small conversions particularly uneconomical. It's a \"actual amount\" to compare the channels, not to look at which exchange rate figures are good."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Common high-cost scenarios"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Airports and hotel counters: worst exchange rates, highest handling fees, emergency response",
              "Dynamic currency conversion (DCC): local currency should be selected for settlement in the renminbi, which is a loss",
              "Small multiple exchange: more painful per fixed fee",
              "Non-comparison to multiple banks or exchange: the difference could be a few percentage points"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Credit card swipe consumption is usually settled at the international organization ' s exchange rate, which is better than the exchange of cash, provided that the card is recognized as free of currency conversion fees. Cross-border investments also need to note that exchange rate fluctuations offset some of the gains, which is in line with the tariff erosion discussed in [the introduction to compound-investment] (/compunding-interest-investing). If you often consume overseas, you can integrate the cost of exchange into the overall financial comparison along the lines of [how does the mortgage rate compare to] (/compare-loan-interest-rates)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "On the POS machine, there is a hint that the billing will be made in the RMB."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Operational checklist"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Prior to the trip, key banknotes were exchanged in domestic banks, leaving a small amount of local currency contingency; large consumption was free of conversion credit cards; and the balance of the money deposited on return was timely to avoid further exchange rate fluctuations. Full financial planning can be found in [establishment of contingency fund] (/build-emergency-fund) and [personal-tax-basics base]."
          }
        ]
      },
      "personal-tax-basics": {
        "title": "The basis of personal income tax: where to start, how to use the special deductions",
        "excerpt": "Many have paid unjust taxes, but only because no special deductions have been declared on a case-by-case basis, and legal tax avoidance begins with the filing of a form.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The starting point for the personal income tax of our residents is $5,000 per month, which is partially based on the seven-degree excess progressive tax rate. But what really affects the tax burden is deductions: in addition to special deductions such as five insurance premiums, there are special deductions for children ' s education, continuing education, medical care, interest or rent on housing loans, old-age maintenance, and care for children under three years of age, which can be legally reduced by a declaration."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The most easily omitted deductions."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Interest and rent on housing loans can only be divided into one and declared to the highest amount",
              "Old-age support: the share of a non-independent child with a brother or sister",
              "Medical treatment for major diseases: deduction of over-insured reimbursement within limit",
              "Continuing education: Qualifications for academic education and vocational qualifications"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Specific deductions are made through a tax APP, which is reported at the time of the annual remittance, and may be added before 30 June of the following year. Reasonable use of the deduction is equivalent to a disguised increase, and the savings can be shifted to [establishment of contingency fund] (/building-emergency-fund) or [initiation of a compound investment] (/compund-interest-investing). The credit-related deduction details are proposed to be planned against the [early-loan-repayment-strategy] (/early-loan-repayment-strategy) because the repayment itself can generate deductible interest."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The end-of-year awards may be taxed separately or combined, and the difference may be as high as thousands of dollars, depending on which one is more provincial."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Cross-income type focus"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The combination of wages, labour payments, manuscripts and royalties is a year-on-year tax on the combined proceeds, so that those with multiple sources of income are more concerned with calculating their contributions, avoiding prepayment and even stalling. For exchange rate-related offshore income declarations, reference is made to [currency-exchange notes] (/currency-exchange-tips). The overall planning approach can be found in [how to compare interest rates on mortgages] (/compare-loan-interest-rates)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "body-fat-percentage-guide": {
        "title": "What do you think of the lipid rate?",
        "excerpt": "Normal body weight is not equal to body body health, and the lipid ratio is the key indicator of fat content.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Many people stare at the weight scales every day, ignoring a more important figure — the lipid rate. The body weight consists of muscles, moisture, bones and fat, two persons of the same weight, and the lipid ratio may vary significantly. The lipid rate is a direct reflection of the body's fat content, which is more an indication of your \"hidden obesity\" than your mere body weight."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "There's a difference between men's and women's health zones"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Adult males have about 10 to 20 percent lipid.",
              "About 18 to 28 percent of adult women are more affected by the biological cycle.",
              "Over 25% male and 32% female are usually considered obese.",
              "Athletes have lower lipid rates, but it's not appropriate to pursue them in the long term."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "There are many ways to measure lipid rates: gymnasium fattens, household biotemporometer scales, and hospital DXA scan accuracy in turn. The household scales are heavily influenced by water consumption, exercise and time periods, and it is recommended that the morning be measured at the same time, in the light of trends rather than single absolute values. After understanding the lipid, targets can be set in combination with [fat-loss-calorie-deficit] (/fat-calorie-deficit) and with reference to [the daily protein intake] (/daily-protein-intake)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The low fat of women disrupts the endocrines and even stops. The lower the fat, the better, the more important the health curve is."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How's the fat and weight fit? Look."
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Fatty reduction is common: body weight is not changed but the waist is smaller, which is a good sign of loss of fat and muscles. Please therefore record the weight, lipid ratio and condensation at the same time and do not be deterred by short-term body weight fluctuations. In order to manage the body indicators in a comprehensive manner, they can be extended to read [basal-metabolic-rate] and [heart-rate-training-zones]."
          }
        ]
      },
      "basal-metabolic-rate": {
        "title": "What about base metabolic rate: you burn the heat when your body is still.",
        "excerpt": "Basic metabolism accounts for more than 60 per cent of daily consumption, allowing it to determine the scientific caloric target.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Basic metabolism (BMR) refers to the amount of heat consumed by the human body in life activities such as saneness, silentness, empty abdominals and the maintenance of heartbeats at constant temperature, which usually accounts for 60 to 70 per cent of total daily consumption. Know your own BMR, with a factor of activity, can estimate total daily consumption (TDEE), which is the starting point for all dietary plans."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Common estimation formula"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Mifflin-StJeor formula: currently accepted, with weight, height, age, sex",
              "Men BMR equals about 10 x kg + 6.25 x cm - 5 x age + 5",
              "Female BMR equals approximately 10 x kg + 6.25 x cm - 5 x age - 161",
              "Activity factor from long sitting 1.2 to high intensity 1.9"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "After BMR is calculated, a gap can be created on a total consumption basis, but it should not be too large to remove muscles and slow down metabolism. What are the specific gaps and what is the reference to [fat-loss-calorie-deficit]? At the same time, the higher the muscle mass, the higher the basic metabolism, so [the daily protein intake] (/daily-protein-intake) is as critical as force training. In order to verify the strength of the exercise, the [heart-rate training area entry] (/heart-rate-training-zones) is available."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Long-term, very low-calorie diets allow the body to downgrade the `energy-saving model', and the rebound tends to be stronger than before, so that the gap is not pushed to the extreme."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Variables affecting metabolism"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Muscle mass, age, thyroid function and even body temperature affect BMR. After the age of 30, metabolism decreases naturally every 10 years, making it more important to rely on training and dietary support in the middle years. Integrated body indicator management, recommended for optimization with [sleep-cycle-explained] [sleep-cycle science]."
          }
        ]
      },
      "fat-loss-calorie-deficit": {
        "title": "What do you mean by \"fat-down\"?",
        "excerpt": "It's essentially a heat gap, but the size of the gap determines whether you lose fat or muscle.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The bottom logic of lipid loss is only one: the consumption of heat is chronically lower than the consumption of heat, creating a gap where the body uses fat for energy. Theoretically, one kilogram of fat contains approximately 7,700 kilocalories, and if a 500 kilocalorie gap is created per day, about 15 days will be lost by one kilogram of fat. Too fast, however, tends to decrease moisture and muscles rather than pure fat."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "It's a safer gap."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "It is recommended that the daily gap be kept at 15 to 20 per cent of total consumption",
              "The average man is about 300 to 500 kilocalories a day.",
              "Weight loss does not exceed 1 per cent of weight per week to avoid metabolic damage",
              "The heat record is reviewed during the platform period, not blindly before cutting."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "There are two paths to the manufacturing gap: less food or more, and it is best to go both ways. Calculate total consumption by [basal-metabolic-rate] before target intake. Combining sufficient proteins keeps muscles as detailed in [the daily protein intake] (/daily-protein-intake); ensuring sleep also stabilizes anorexic hormones by reference to [sleep-cycle-explained] science. The lipid data can track progress by [how do you see the body-fat-percentage-guide]."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Many have underestimated food consumption, overestimated consumption and recorded it with an abbreviated AP for three days, often finding that 20 percent more was actually eaten."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What happens when you meet a platform?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The reduction in total consumption following the loss of body weight will naturally reduce the original gap, which is the biological cause of the platform period. At this point, diets can be fine-tuned or non-movement consumption (long walking, standing) can be increased rather than extreme diets. The strength of the movement is sufficient to be judged by [the introduction to the heart rate training area] (/heart-rate-training-zones)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "daily-protein-intake": {
        "title": "The daily protein intake: physical fitness and lipid loss.",
        "excerpt": "Protein determines muscle retention and abdominal saturation, with intake estimates based on weight rather than random.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Protein is the basic material that makes up muscles, enzymes and immune cells. The average sitting adult recommends approximately 8 to 1 g/kg weight per day; the period of force training or lipid reduction should be increased to 1 6 to 2 2 g/kg/kg because higher intakes keep muscles, increase food heat and increase abdominal satuation."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How do we get three meals?"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "25 to 40 grams of protein absorbed highest per meal",
              "Breakfast is often neglected. Filling with protein reduces all-day hunger.",
              "After two hours of exercise, it works better.",
              "Plants and animal proteins can complement full amino acid"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In the case of a person who is in the process of defamining 70 kg, it would be sufficient if approximately one hundred and twenty-six grams of protein were to be distributed to three meals and one extra meal per kilogram. Sufficient protein is the key to preventing muscle loss in [fat-loss-calorie-deficit] and supports the muscle mass on which [basal-metabolic-rate] [basal-metabolic-rate] depends. Training intensity arrangements can be referred to [the introduction to the heart rate training area] (/heart-rate-training-zones)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Renal abnormalities are restricted to proteins according to medical instructions, and the diet of high proteins in the general population is not clearly harmful to the kidneys."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Source of quality protein"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Chicken breasts, fish, eggs, dairy products, tofu and soy milk are all high-quality sources, in which the amino acid rating for animal protein is more complete. When plant-based, attention is paid to the complementarities of cereals and beans. The change in disassembly composition can be combined with a comprehensive assessment of [body-fat-percentage-guide] and [sleep cycle science] (/sleep-cycle-explained)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "sleep-cycle-explained": {
        "title": "Sleep cycle science: Why are you sleepy enough to sleep for eight hours?",
        "excerpt": "Sleep quality looks at cycles rather than mere lengths and wakes up at the wrong stage.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The adult sleep consists of four to six cycles, each of about 90 to 110 minutes, which go through three stages of sleep, deep sleep and rapid eye movement (REM). Sleep well to repair the body, rem consolidate memories and emotions. The alarm rings during the deep sleep, and even after eight hours of sleep, it's a common reason for \"sleeping enough and tired\"."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Use the cycle to get up."
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "In 90 minutes the whole number is pushed back to sleep.",
              "For example, five cycles, about 7.5 hours, more sober than eight hours.",
              "It's more stable to get up when you're asleep. Bell",
              "Sleeping is mostly concentrated in the middle of the night."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Regularity affects quality recovery more than total duration. Sleep is also directly associated with metabolic and appetite: hunger rises and thinness declines when sleep is not enough, which indirectly undermines the implementation of [fat-loss-calorie-deficit] [fat-loss-calorie-deficit]. At the same time, inadequate recovery reduces the performance of training and allows for the observation of changes in static heart rate against [the introduction to the heart rate training area] (/heart-rate-training-zones). In combination with nutrition, reference is made to [the daily protein intake] (/daily-protein-intake)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "An hour before sleep is away from blue light and caffeine, and a slightly lower temperature (about 18 to 20 degrees) in the bedroom is most conducive to entering deep sleep."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "How do you sleep?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In addition to the length of time, it is more important to focus on whether the day is natural and sober, whether it is possible to sleep quickly and whether it is frequent at night. A two-week sleep log search can be recorded. Overall health indicators can also be combined with a combination of [body-fat-percentage-guide] and [basal-metabolic-rate] judgement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "percentage-increase-decrease": {
        "title": "How does the percentage increase or decrease count: why does it fall back?",
        "excerpt": "The base for percentage increases and decreases will change, with a 50 per cent increase and a 50 per cent drop, resulting in losses rather than even.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The most prone to errors in calculating increases or decreases is the base figure. Both increases and decreases are relative to current values, and the base figure has changed, with the same percentage corresponding to absolute amounts. For example, when a commodity goes up 50 to 150% and falls to 50%, it falls to 75 instead of 100. The same percentage rises and falls, always below the starting point."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Standard formulae"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Growth rate = (New - Original) ÷ Original value x 100%",
              "The drop rate is also based on the original value as the denominator, not the new value",
              "Multiplication of consecutive changes: Final = original value x (1+10%) x (1-10%)",
              "The percentage that needs to be increased to get back to square one is higher than the percentage that fell earlier. That's right."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Such delusions are common in investment and discounts: after a 20 per cent drop in equities, a 25 per cent increase is required to return the capital. The concept of the base figure is understood, and it is possible to avoid being misled by the `average increase' when looking at the [average median number] (/mean-median-mode). The conversion of discounts to matching can be referred to [proportional versus matching] (/ratio-and-scaling). The day-to-day mind-to-heart skills are found in [percentage mind-to-heart] techniques (/mental-percentage-tricks)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The \"30% increase\" in the news, if not the base figure, could have gone from 100 to 130; it's always the denominator."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What about the successive percentages?"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Multiple changes do not simply add or decrease by percentage, but multiply each factor. For example, the increase of 10 per cent and the drop of 10 per cent in two years, respectively, resulted in a net drop of 1 per cent at the original value of 1.1 x 0.9 = 0.99. This is consistent with the logic of cumulative errors in [rounding-significant-figures] (/rounding-significant-figures), which can be easily added to the measurement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "unit-conversion-pitfalls": {
        "title": "Unit conversion trap: same factor for length area size",
        "excerpt": "A 100-fold gap between centimetres and metres and 10,000-fold gap between square centimetres and square metres is the most easily converted volume.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The unit conversion is a routine high frequency operation, but many apply the length factor directly to area and volume. One metre is equal to 100 centimetres, which is the length; but one square metre is equal to 100 per cent, i.e. 10,000 centimetres of centimetres, and the difference is square relationships. The volume is more exaggerated, with one cubic metre equal to 1 million cubic centimetres. Using an error dimension factor, the result is several orders of magnitude."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Application factor by dimension"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Length: 1 m = 100 cm (× 100)",
              "Area: 1 m2 = 10000 cm2 (x 1002)",
              "Volume: 1 cubic metre = 1,000,000 cubic centimetres (x 1003)",
              "velocity units are to process the molecular denominator simultaneously."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The g and cup conversion in cooking is also a disaster-prone area, as detailed in [g and cup conversion] (/grams-to-cups). The accuracy of the physical volume conversion is also associated with [rounding-significant-figures] (/rounding-significant-figures) and retention of too many digits is false and too few are incorrect. The formulation scaling also needs to be matched by a multiplication of the understanding of the [scale versus match] (/ratio-and-scaling)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Write units before conversion: When `cm/s' is converted to `m/h', process length and time, respectively, and do not mix."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Speed and time trap"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The common mistake is to use \"kilometres per hour\" directly as \"metres per second\". The correct approach is 1 km/h = 1000 m/ 3600 seconds ≈ 0.278 m/s. In such cases, which involve a simultaneous change between the two units, it is suggested that the formula should move step by step to avoid intuitiveness. Where a rapid determination of the ratio is required, the [percentage heart calculation techniques] (/mental-percentage-tricks) estimation scale may be combined."
          }
        ]
      },
      "ratio-and-scaling": {
        "title": "Ratio versus match: Why does it taste different?",
        "excerpt": "Linear scaling is available for dry and wet formulations, but salt, spices and fermentation are to be judged separately from non-linear effects.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The ratio is a tool that represents a two-volume-multiple relationship, and the ratio is common for formulations and redeployments. The four-person diet is converted to a two-person diet, with a direct factor of zero-point-five, which appears to be simple, but certain ingredients do not change linearly with the number of people: salt, pepper, spices tend to be subjectivity, which may be too salty after doubling; the waking time of fermented noodles is more related to totals, ambient temperatures than simple proportionality."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "The right way to match"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Let's look for the baseline. The rest of it is expressed against it.",
              "Unified units and then compare to avoid mixing g with ml",
              "Keep a reasonable number after scaling, do not write 1.3333 g",
              "The spices are more likely to be added and adapted."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In practice, dry mains can be scaled up proportionately, and liquids and spices are recommended proportionally but leave room for adjustment. This goes hand in hand with the conversion of volume weights in [grams-to-cups] and the dimensions of [unit-conversion-pitfalls] (/unit-conversion-pitfalls). If the results are to be reported as statistics, the appropriate centre value may be selected by reference to [average median numbers] (/mean-median-mode)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "Baking is more dependent on precise matching than cooking, and a small deviation in the ratio of flour, water and yeast may fail, with more caution in scaling."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Concentration and dilution"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The dilution of disinfectant fluids and pesticides is by nature a problem of proportion: the ratio 1:100 indicates a source of 100 water. The confusion between \"1:100\" and \"1 percent\" could have caused an accident. Estimating dilution multiples can be used (/mental-percentage-tricks), while the accuracy of the measurements returns to [rounding-significant-figures]."
          }
        ]
      },
      "mean-median-mode": {
        "title": "Median number of averages: first read distribution before average",
        "excerpt": "When the average wage is raised by a few, the median is more representative of the true level of the average.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "There are three commonly used indicators that describe a set of data `centres'. The average is the sum divided by the number, which is the most vulnerable to heat; the median is the number that ranks behind and is immune to extremes; the number is the highest. Selecting the wrong indicator, the conclusion may be the exact opposite, which is also the source of the term `average'."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "What are the three indicators?"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Moderate data on income, housing prices, etc. are more realistic",
              "If you want to reward the general level, you can see the average.",
              "The size of the costume, the implosion option.",
              "The greater the gap, the more uneven the distribution of data."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "For example, nine out of ten people go to 8,000 a month and 80,000 a month, averaging about 15,000, but the median is 8,000 — the latter is close to the sense of the majority. Understanding this would avoid being misled by the `average increase' in [percentage increase/decrease] (/percentage-increase-decrease). The report on the results will also be accurate, with reference to [rounded and valid figures] (/rounding-significant-figures)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "See the `average' distribution first: if a few extremes exist, the median is often more relevant than the average."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Practical application scene"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "In business and national statistics, median income and median housing prices are often given official priority because of their resistance to interference. When conducting the study, it is recommended that both averages and medians be given and that differences be indicated. Comparable data can be collated to combine [ratio-and-scaling] (/ratio-and-scaling), while rapid estimates can be based on [percentage calculations] (/mental-percentage-tricks)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "rounding-significant-figures": {
        "title": "Rounded and valid figures: one more may be false",
        "excerpt": "The valid digital digits of the measurements are determined by the precision of the instrument, and blind retention of decimals is misleading.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Rounded to the point that it seemed simple, the real difficulty was to keep a few. Valid figures refer to all reliable figures from the first non-zero figure, which reflects the true accuracy of the measurements. It would be absurd to use a scale that can only be precise to the grams, because the scales themselves have only two valid numbers, and the numeric numbers are purely imaginary."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Rules and common errors"
          },
          {
            "type": "ul",
            "items": [
              "Rounded to see one after the reservation. Five to one.",
              "The middle is five and the back is zero, and the banker is rounded to even.",
              "Number of decimal places not exceeding the least accurate entry",
              "The valid number of multiplier results does not exceed the minimum number of valid numbers"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Many computational errors stem from insensitivity to accuracy: the ratio of 1.2 m (two valid numbers) to 3.45 m is 4.140 m, which is actually about 4.1 m. This is similar to the logic of supersing the dimensional coefficients in [unit-conversion-pitfalls], which tends to accumulate with more errors. Accuracy of ratio and scaling is found in [ratio-and-scaling] (/ratio-and-scaling)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "text": "The results should not be more valid than the least accurate of the original data, which is the most common source of `false accuracy'."
          },
          {
            "type": "h2",
            "text": "Engineering versus day-to-day trade-offs"
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Day-to-day calculations do not have to pursue long decimals, such as cooking recipes with 1.3 grams more honest and practical than 1.333 grams. However, the financial and scientific scene is to be kept in accordance with the norms. Quick estimates can be accompanied by [percentage heart calculation techniques] (/mental-percentage-tricks) Quantifications, followed by decisions to retain several. The statistical description is given in [median number of averages] (/mean-median-mode)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "categoryLabels": {
      "finance": "Finance",
      "health": "Health",
      "math": "Math",
      "cooking": "Cooking"
    }
  },
